= Quantitative Research Interview Mathematics Review
[../../../blog.html ← Blogs] \M /updated 2026-07-21/

~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="language-switch" role="navigation" aria-label="Language"><a href="quant-math-review.html" hreflang="zh-CN">中文</a><span class="active" aria-current="page">English</span></div>
~~~

This is a high-coverage mathematics checklist for quantitative research written tests and interviews. It is designed for rapid recall, not as a first textbook. Standard terms are kept explicit because interviews often expect them directly. Topics marked “Expand” include detailed notes and remain collapsed by default so that the checklist stays easy to scan.

*Priority:* P0 = must be able to derive and use without notes; P1 = commonly tested; P2 = role-dependent bonus. For every checkbox, require yourself to: define it, state the assumptions, derive the key formula, solve one small example, and explain one failure mode.

== 0. High-priority review order

Review in this order:

. *P0 regression:* derive OLS; diagnose multicollinearity; compare deletion, PCA\/SVD, ridge and lasso; explain heteroskedasticity and leakage.
. *P0 probability\/statistics:* conditional probability, expectation, variance\/covariance, common distributions, estimation, confidence intervals and hypothesis tests.
. *P0 ML:* bias-variance, regularization, validation, linear\/logistic models, trees and ensembles, PCA, evaluation metrics.
. *P0 counting:* permutations\/combinations, recurrence, state design and dynamic programming.
. *P0 linear algebra:* rank, projections, eigenvalues, SVD, positive semidefinite covariance matrices and conditioning.
. *P1 time series and quant research:* returns, stationarity, autocorrelation, rolling features, time-aware validation, transaction costs and multiple testing.

== 1. Probability

=== 1.1 Events, counting and probability rules \[P0\]

- □ Sample space, event, complement, union, intersection, mutually exclusive events.
- □ Axioms: $P(A)\\ge0$, $P(\\Omega)=1$, and additivity for disjoint events.
- □ Complement and inclusion-exclusion: $P(A\\cup B)=P(A)\\mathplus{}P(B)-P(A\\cap B)$.
- □ Equally likely outcomes require counting the correct atomic outcomes; sums of two dice are not equally likely.
- □ Multiplication rule, permutations, combinations and multinomial coefficients.
- □ Sampling with\/without replacement; ordered\/unordered; distinguishability; circular arrangements.
- □ Stars and bars, pigeonhole principle, symmetry and complementary counting.

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-inclusion-exclusion">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Inclusion–Exclusion Principle</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. What problem does it solve?</h4>
<p>When sets overlap, adding their sizes double-counts objects. Inclusion–exclusion alternately adds and subtracts intersections so that every object in the union is counted exactly once. For finite sets $A_1,\ldots,A_n$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\left|\bigcup_{i=1}^n A_i\right|
=\sum_i|A_i|-\sum_{i&lt;j}|A_i\cap A_j|
+\sum_{i&lt;j&lt;k}|A_i\cap A_j\cap A_k|-\cdots
+(-1)^{n+1}\left|\bigcap_{i=1}^n A_i\right|.
$$</div>
<p>Replacing cardinalities by probabilities gives the probability version. In particular,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(A\cup B\cup C)=P(A)+P(B)+P(C)
-P(A\cap B)-P(A\cap C)-P(B\cap C)+P(A\cap B\cap C).
$$</div>
<h4>2. Why do the signs alternate?</h4>
<p>Suppose an outcome belongs to exactly $r$ sets. The single-set sum counts it $\binom r1$ times, the pairwise term subtracts it $\binom r2$ times, and so on. Its final coefficient is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\binom r1-\binom r2+\binom r3-\cdots+(-1)^{r+1}\binom rr=1,
$$</div>
<p>by $(1-1)^r=0$. Thus each outcome in the union leaves exactly one copy, while an outcome outside the union is never counted.</p>
<h4>3. Standard interview workflow</h4>
<ol>
<li>Define bad events $A_i$; the target is often that none of them occurs.</li>
<li>Count an arbitrary intersection $A_{i_1}\cap\cdots\cap A_{i_j}$. Symmetry often makes all intersections of the same order equal.</li>
<li>Alternate signs by intersection order; if the target is “none,” subtract the union from the total.</li>
<li>Check signs and boundaries on a small instance.</li>
</ol>
<h4>4. Example: derangements</h4>
<p>Permute $n$ objects and let $A_i$ mean that object $i$ remains fixed. If a specified set of $j$ objects is fixed, the remaining objects have $(n-j)!$ permutations. Therefore</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
D_n=n!-\binom n1(n-1)!+\binom n2(n-2)!-\cdots+(-1)^n
=n!\sum_{j=0}^n\frac{(-1)^j}{j!}.
$$</div>
<p>The probability that a random permutation has no fixed points is $D_n/n!$, which tends to $e^{-1}$ as $n\to\infty$.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> an intersection probability is not a product unless independence is justified; stopping after only a few terms usually gives Bonferroni bounds rather than an exact answer; “exactly $r$ events” requires an additional combinatorial adjustment.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

*Interview reflex:* before calculating, say what one outcome is and why outcomes are equally likely. Most counting errors come from changing the sample space halfway through.

=== 1.2 Conditional probability, independence and Bayes \[P0\]

- □ Conditional probability: $P(A\\mid B)=\\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(B)}$, for $P(B)>0$.
- □ Product rule: $P(A\\cap B)=P(A\\mid B)P(B)$.
- □ Law of total probability over a partition {B_i}.
- □ Bayes rule: posterior is proportional to likelihood times prior.
- □ Pairwise independence is weaker than mutual independence.
- □ Conditional independence can hold when marginal independence does not, and vice versa.
~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-bayes-reasoning-traps">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Base-Rate Neglect, Prosecutor's Fallacy, Monty Hall, and False Positives</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Shared core: do not reverse a conditional probability</h4>
<p>All of these problems warn that</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(A\mid B)\ne P(B\mid A).
$$</div>
<p>Bayes' rule combines a likelihood, a base rate, and a posterior:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(A\mid B)
=\frac{P(B\mid A)P(A)}
{P(B\mid A)P(A)+P(B\mid A^c)P(A^c)}.
$$</div>
<p>A robust solution method is to imagine 10,000 people or 1,000 cases and write natural-frequency counts along every branch rather than manipulating tiny decimals immediately.</p>
<h4>2. Base-rate neglect and false positives</h4>
<p>Base-rate neglect focuses on test accuracy such as $P(+\mid D)$ while ignoring how rare the disease is, $P(D)$. Suppose prevalence is $1\%$, sensitivity is $99\%$, and specificity is $95\%$. Among 10,000 people:</p>
<ul>
<li>100 have the disease and about 99 test positive;</li>
<li>9,900 do not have it, and a $5\%$ false-positive rate produces about 495 positive tests.</li>
</ul>
<p>The positive predictive value is therefore</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(D\mid+)
=\frac{99}{99+495}
=\frac16
\approx16.7\%.
$$</div>
<p>Even a highly sensitive test can have a modest posterior disease probability when the healthy population is enormous. Distinguish carefully between</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\underbrace{P(+\mid D^c)}_{\text{false-positive rate}}
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\underbrace{P(D^c\mid+)}_{\text{false-discovery probability}}.
$$</div>
<h4>3. Prosecutor's fallacy</h4>
<p>The prosecutor's fallacy turns “this evidence is rare under innocence” into “innocence is rare after observing this evidence”:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(E\mid I)\quad\text{is mistaken for}\quad P(I\mid E).
$$</div>
<p>A one-in-a-million random DNA match probability is not a one-in-a-million probability of innocence. The posterior also depends on how many possible matches existed, how the suspect was selected, how the evidence was generated, and other case information. Searching a database of millions makes at least one accidental match much less surprising and introduces a multiple-comparisons issue.</p>
<p>The proper evidence statement uses a likelihood ratio $P(E\mid G)/P(E\mid I)$ combined with prior odds, rather than calling one likelihood a probability of guilt.</p>
<h4>4. Why does switching win Monty Hall with probability $2/3$?</h4>
<p>One of three doors hides a car. The initially selected door has probability $1/3$ of holding it, while the two unselected doors together have probability $2/3$. The host knows the answer, always opens a goat door among the unselected doors, and always offers a switch. This does not reset the first door to probability $1/2$; it concentrates the other doors' $2/3$ probability on the only unopened alternative:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(\text{stay wins})=\frac13,\qquad
P(\text{switch wins})=\frac23.
$$</div>
<p>Equivalently, if the initial choice is the car, which happens with probability $1/3$, switching loses. If the initial choice is a goat, probability $2/3$, the host is forced to reveal the other goat and switching wins.</p>
<p>The answer depends on the host protocol. If the host does not know the car location, might reveal the car, or offers a switch only in selected situations, recompute the conditional probability under that behavior.</p>
<h4>5. Unified workflow</h4>
<ol>
<li>Name the posterior target, such as $P(D\mid+)$, rather than saying only “accuracy.”</li>
<li>Write the prior or base rates $P(D)$ and $P(D^c)$.</li>
<li>Write the branch likelihoods $P(+\mid D)$ and $P(+\mid D^c)$.</li>
<li>Use total probability for the evidence denominator, or draw a tree/use natural frequencies.</li>
<li>Model the information-selection mechanism; Monty Hall and database searches both depend on the protocol.</li>
</ol>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> sensitivity is not positive predictive value; false-positive rate is not the fraction of positive results that are false; rare evidence does not automatically imply a high guilt probability; Monty Hall's $2/3$ assumes a knowledgeable host following a fixed rule; and selective reporting or multiple searches changes the conditional law of the evidence.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

*Quick check:* if a disease prevalence is 1\%, sensitivity is 99\%, and specificity is 95\%, can you compute P(disease | positive) without confusing P(A | B) with P(B | A)?

=== 1.3 Random variables and distributions \[P0\]

- □ PMF, PDF and CDF; discrete versus continuous variables.

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-survival-function">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Survival Function</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Definition and intuition</h4>
<p>The survival function is the probability that a random variable exceeds a threshold:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
S(x)=P(X&gt;x)=1-F(x).
$$</div>
<p>For a lifetime it means “survives beyond $x$”; for a waiting time it means “has not happened by $x$”; in finance it can represent the tail probability that a loss exceeds a level. For a continuous variable, $P(X=x)=0$, so $&gt;$ and $\ge$ are equivalent. For a discrete variable they differ, so check whether the convention uses $P(X&gt;x)$ or $P(X\ge x)$.</p>
<h4>2. Recovering the density and hazard</h4>
<p>If $X$ is continuous and differentiable, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
f(x)=F'(x)=-S'(x).
$$</div>
<p>The hazard rate is not “the probability of immediate failure.” It is the instantaneous failure intensity conditional on surviving to $x$:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
h(x)=\frac{f(x)}{S(x)}.
$$</div>
<p>The cumulative hazard is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
H(x)=\int_0^x h(u)\,\mathrm du=-\log S(x),
\qquad
S(x)=e^{-H(x)}.
$$</div>
<p>Thus a hazard function determines the full distribution. An Exponential variable has constant $h(x)=\lambda$; an increasing or decreasing hazard does not have the same memoryless behavior.</p>
<h4>3. Tail-integral formula</h4>
<p>For $X\ge0$, expectation can be recovered directly from survival probabilities:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[X]=\int_0^\infty P(X&gt;t)\,\mathrm dt
=\int_0^\infty S(t)\,\mathrm dt.
$$</div>
<p>The discrete counterpart for a nonnegative integer variable is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[X]=\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}P(X\ge k).
$$</div>
<p>These identities are often simpler than direct PMF/PDF calculations in waiting-time, maximum, and stopping-time problems.</p>
<h4>4. Residual life and conditioning</h4>
<p>Conditional on $X&gt;s$, the probability of waiting at least another $t$ units is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(X&gt;s+t\mid X&gt;s)=\frac{S(s+t)}{S(s)}.
$$</div>
<p>Memorylessness holds only when this ratio equals $S(t)$ for every $s$. With right-censored lifetime data, the complete lifetime is unknown but the observation is known to survive to the censoring time, making the survival function more natural than an ordinary mean.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> a survival function is a tail probability, not a density; a hazard may exceed 1 because it is an instantaneous rate, not a probability; discrete variables require care with $&gt;$ versus $\ge$; and the tail-integral formula above assumes nonnegativity.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-variable-transformations-extrema">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Variable Transformations, the One-Dimensional Jacobian, Sums, Minima, and Maxima</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Monotone transformations: probability is preserved while length is rescaled</h4>
<p>Let continuous $X$ have density $f_X$, let $Y=g(X)$, and suppose $g$ is strictly monotone and differentiable. Writing $x=g^{-1}(y)$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
f_Y(y)=f_X\!\left(g^{-1}(y)\right)
\left|\frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm dy}g^{-1}(y)\right|.
$$</div>
<p>The absolute derivative is the one-dimensional Jacobian. It preserves the probability mass in corresponding short intervals:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
f_Y(y)\,\mathrm dy\approx f_X(x)\,\mathrm dx.
$$</div>
<p>For increasing $g$, one may instead write $F_Y(y)=F_X(g^{-1}(y))$ and differentiate. For decreasing $g$, the inequality reverses, so the density formula with the absolute value is safer.</p>
<h4>2. Non-one-to-one transformations: sum over every preimage</h4>
<p>If $g(x)=y$ has roots $x_1,\ldots,x_m$, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
f_Y(y)=\sum_{j=1}^m
\frac{f_X(x_j)}{|g'(x_j)|},
$$</div>
<p>where only roots inside the support of $X$ are included. For example, if $X\sim\operatorname{Uniform}(-1,1)$ and $Y=X^2$, the preimages of $0&lt;y&lt;1$ are $\pm\sqrt y$, so</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
f_Y(y)
=\frac{1/2}{2\sqrt y}+\frac{1/2}{2\sqrt y}
=\frac1{2\sqrt y}.
$$</div>
<h4>3. Sums of independent variables: convolution</h4>
<p>For independent continuous $X,Y$ and $Z=X+Y$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
f_Z(z)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
f_X(x)f_Y(z-x)\,\mathrm dx.
$$</div>
<p>The effective limits require both $x$ to lie in the support of $X$ and $z-x$ in the support of $Y$. The discrete counterpart is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(X+Y=k)=\sum_jP(X=j)P(Y=k-j).
$$</div>
<p>Independence factors the joint density. Without independence, use the joint density $f_{X,Y}(x,z-x)$ instead of a product.</p>
<h4>4. Minima and maxima: start with a CDF or survival function</h4>
<p>For i.i.d. $X_1,\ldots,X_n$ with CDF $F$, let $M=\max_iX_i$ and $m=\min_iX_i$. Then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(M\le x)=F(x)^n,\qquad
P(m&gt;x)=[1-F(x)]^n.
$$</div>
<p>If a density $f$ exists, differentiation gives</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
f_M(x)=nF(x)^{n-1}f(x),\qquad
f_m(x)=n[1-F(x)]^{n-1}f(x).
$$</div>
<p>For independent but non-identical variables, replace $F(x)^n$ by $\prod_iF_i(x)$ and $[1-F(x)]^n$ by $\prod_i[1-F_i(x)]$.</p>
<h4>5. Problem-solving checklist</h4>
<ol>
<li>Write the original support and determine the possible range of the transformed variable.</li>
<li>Check whether the map is monotone and one-to-one; otherwise list all preimages.</li>
<li>Use convolution for sums, “all are below” for maxima, and “all are above” for minima.</li>
<li>Check that a density is nonnegative and integrates to 1, and that a CDF has the correct boundary limits.</li>
</ol>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> the Jacobian needs an absolute value; non-one-to-one maps require every root; convolution and product formulas require independence; and supports determine the actual integration region.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

- □ Joint, marginal and conditional distributions; independence via factorization.
- □ LOTUS: compute E\[g(X)\] from the distribution of X without first finding the distribution of g(X).
- □ Quantiles, median, mode, skewness, kurtosis and why moments may not exist.

*Discrete families:*

- □ $\\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p)$: mean $p$, variance $p(1-p)$.
- □ $\\operatorname{Binomial}(n,p)$: independent Bernoulli count; mean $np$, variance $np(1-p)$.
- □ Geometric(p): waiting time, memoryless; be explicit whether support starts at 0 or 1.

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-negative-binomial">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Negative Binomial Distribution</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Two common counting conventions</h4>
<p>Run independent Bernoulli trials with success probability $p$ until success $r$. If $X$ counts the <strong>failures before success $r$</strong>, then $X=0,1,2,\ldots$ and</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(X=k)=\binom{k+r-1}{k}p^r(1-p)^k.
$$</div>
<p>The last trial must be a success, while the first $k+r-1$ positions contain $r-1$ successes and $k$ failures. Under this convention,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[X]=\frac{r(1-p)}p,\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(X)=\frac{r(1-p)}{p^2}.
$$</div>
<p>If $T$ counts the <strong>total trials required</strong>, then $T=X+r$ with support $r,r+1,\ldots$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(T=t)=\binom{t-1}{r-1}p^r(1-p)^{t-r},
\qquad
\mathbb E[T]=\frac rp.
$$</div>
<h4>2. Relation to the Geometric distribution</h4>
<p>A Negative Binomial variable is a sum of $r$ independent Geometric waiting blocks. If each Geometric variable counts failures before one success, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
X=G_1+\cdots+G_r,
\qquad
G_i\sim\operatorname{Geometric}(p).
$$</div>
<p>Setting $r=1$ gives the Geometric distribution. This representation immediately yields the mean and variance. Waiting for several successes in discrete time gives Negative Binomial, analogous to waiting for several Poisson arrivals in continuous time giving Gamma/Erlang.</p>
<h4>3. An overdispersed count model</h4>
<p>Negative Binomial counts can also arise from heterogeneity in a Poisson rate. If</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\Lambda\sim\operatorname{Gamma}(r,\beta),
\qquad
Y\mid\Lambda\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\Lambda),
$$</div>
<p>using a rate-parameterized Gamma, then marginally $Y$ is Negative Binomial with $p=\beta/(\beta+1)$. Mixing different Poisson rates gives</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{Var}(Y)&gt;\mathbb E[Y],
$$</div>
<p>which makes the model useful for overdispersed count data. Poisson fixes the rate, whereas Negative Binomial can capture unobserved heterogeneity or clustering.</p>
<h4>4. Worked example</h4>
<p>A coin succeeds with probability $p=0.4$ and is tossed until success 3. The probability of exactly 2 failures before that success is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(X=2)=\binom42(0.4)^3(0.6)^2=0.13824.
$$</div>
<p>The expected failure count is $3(0.6)/0.4=4.5$, while the expected total trial count is $3/0.4=7.5$.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> books and software may count failures, total trials, or successes before failures, and may reverse the meaning of $p$. State the support and PMF before solving. A formula cannot be inferred safely from the label “Negative Binomial$(r,p)$” alone.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-hypergeometric">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Hypergeometric Distribution: Sampling Without Replacement and the Finite-Population Correction</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Model and PMF</h4>
<p>A population contains $N$ objects, of which $K$ are labeled successes. Draw $n$ objects uniformly without replacement and let $X$ be the number of successes in the sample. Then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
X\sim\operatorname{Hypergeometric}(N,K,n),
$$</div>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(X=k)=
\frac{\binom Kk\binom{N-K}{n-k}}{\binom Nn}.
$$</div>
<p>The denominator counts all samples of size $n$. The numerator selects $k$ of the $K$ successes and $n-k$ of the remaining objects. The feasible support is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\max\{0,n-(N-K)\}\le k\le\min\{n,K\},
$$</div>
<p>not automatically every integer from 0 to $n$.</p>
<h4>2. Mean, variance, and the finite-population correction</h4>
<p>Let the population success proportion be $p=K/N$. Then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[X]=np,
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=np(1-p)\frac{N-n}{N-1}.
$$</div>
<p>Relative to $\operatorname{Binomial}(n,p)$, the extra factor</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\frac{N-n}{N-1}
$$</div>
<p>is the finite-population correction. Without replacement, draws are negatively related: after drawing a success, the remaining success proportion falls. The variance is therefore below that of independent sampling. If $n=N$, the full population is observed, $X=K$ is deterministic, and the variance is zero.</p>
<h4>3. Indicator derivation of the mean</h4>
<p>Let $I_j$ indicate whether sampled position $j$ is a success, so $X=\sum_{j=1}^nI_j$. Each position has marginal success probability $K/N$, even though positions are dependent. Therefore</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[X]=\sum_{j=1}^n\mathbb E[I_j]
=n\frac KN.
$$</div>
<p>Variances cannot simply be added because $I_i$ and $I_j$ have negative covariance. Keeping those covariance terms produces the finite-population correction.</p>
<h4>4. Difference from and approximation by Binomial</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hypergeometric:</strong> a finite population, sampling without replacement, and dependent draws.</li>
<li><strong>Binomial:</strong> sampling with replacement or a fixed success probability across independent trials.</li>
</ul>
<p>When the sampling fraction $n/N$ is small, early draws barely change the population composition. Hypergeometric is then well approximated by $\operatorname{Binomial}(n,K/N)$, and the finite-population correction is close to 1.</p>
<h4>5. Worked example</h4>
<p>From a standard 52-card deck, the probability that a 5-card hand contains exactly 2 aces is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\frac{\binom42\binom{48}3}{\binom{52}5}.
$$</div>
<p>Here $N=52,K=4,n=5,k=2$. The same model appears in acceptance sampling, inventory audits, and Fisher's exact test.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> without-replacement draws are not independent Bernoulli trials; verify the feasible support of $k$; the finite-population correction changes the variance, not the mean; and do not swap population success count $K$ with sample size $n$.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-poisson-distribution">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Poisson distribution</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. What does it model?</h4>
<p>Let $X$ count events in a fixed time interval, spatial region, or other exposure. When events are approximately independent, the average rate is stable, and the probability of two or more events in a very short interval is negligible, a standard model is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
X\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda),\qquad
P(X=k)=e^{-\lambda}\frac{\lambda^k}{k!},\quad k=0,1,2,\ldots,\quad \lambda&gt;0.
$$</div>
<p>The parameter $\lambda$ is the <strong>expected count</strong> in that exposure, not a probability, so it may exceed 1. If the average rate is $r$ per unit time and the observation lasts $t$ units, then $\lambda=rt$. The PMF is normalized because</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}e^{-\lambda}\frac{\lambda^k}{k!}
=e^{-\lambda}e^\lambda=1.
$$</div>
<h4>2. Why are both the mean and variance $\lambda$?</h4>
<p>The Poisson PMF satisfies the useful shift identity</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
kP(X=k)=\lambda P(X=k-1),\qquad k\ge1.
$$</div>
<p>Shifting the summation index gives</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[X]
=\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}kP(X=k)
=\lambda\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}P(X=j)
=\lambda.
$$</div>
<p>Applying the same idea to the second factorial moment yields</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[X(X-1)]=\lambda^2,
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=\mathbb E[X(X-1)]+\mathbb E[X]-(\mathbb E[X])^2
=\lambda.
$$</div>
<p>Thus the standard deviation is $\sqrt{\lambda}$, while the relative fluctuation is $\sqrt{\lambda}/\lambda=1/\sqrt{\lambda}$ and decreases as the expected count grows.</p>
<h4>3. Superposition, conditioning, and thinning</h4>
<p>If $X_i\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda_i)$ are independent, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\sum_{i=1}^m X_i\sim
\operatorname{Poisson}\!\left(\sum_{i=1}^m\lambda_i\right).
$$</div>
<p>This follows immediately from the probability-generating function $G_X(s)=\exp\{\lambda(s-1)\}$. Conversely, if every Poisson event is independently labeled A with probability $p$, the A count is $\operatorname{Poisson}(p\lambda)$, the remaining count is $\operatorname{Poisson}((1-p)\lambda)$, and the two counts are independent. This is Poisson <em>thinning</em>.</p>
<p>Another useful result is that for independent $X_i\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda_i)$, conditional on the total $N=\sum_iX_i=n$, the vector $(X_1,\ldots,X_m)$ is Multinomial with parameters</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\left(n;\frac{\lambda_1}{\sum_j\lambda_j},\ldots,
\frac{\lambda_m}{\sum_j\lambda_j}\right).
$$</div>
<p>Intuitively, Poisson determines the total count; once the total is known, each event is classified according to the sources' relative intensities.</p>
<h4>4. Why does it approximate a Binomial count?</h4>
<p>Let $X_n\sim\operatorname{Binomial}(n,p_n)$. If events become rare while their expected total stays finite, so that $n\to\infty$, $p_n\to0$, and $np_n\to\lambda$, then for each fixed $k$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\begin{aligned}
P(X_n=k)
&amp;=\binom nk p_n^k(1-p_n)^{n-k}\\
&amp;\longrightarrow
\frac{\lambda^k}{k!}e^{-\lambda}.
\end{aligned}
$$</div>
<p>The three ingredients are $\binom nk\sim n^k/k!$, $p_n^k\sim(\lambda/n)^k$, and $(1-p_n)^n\to e^{-\lambda}$. Therefore a Binomial count can be approximated by Poisson$(np)$ when there are many approximately independent opportunities and each success is rare. The approximation deteriorates when probabilities are not small, trials are dependent, or success probabilities vary greatly across trials.</p>
<h4>5. Connection to the Poisson process and Exponential waiting times</h4>
<p>A Poisson distribution describes the <strong>count in one fixed interval</strong>; a Poisson process describes how that count evolves over time. If $(N(t))_{t\ge0}$ is a homogeneous Poisson process with rate $r$, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
N(t)\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(rt),
\qquad
N(t+s)-N(t)\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(rs),
$$</div>
<p>and increments over disjoint intervals are independent. The waiting times between consecutive events are independent $\operatorname{Exponential}(r)$ variables. A useful mnemonic is: <strong>Poisson counts events; Exponential measures waiting time.</strong></p>
<h4>6. Worked example</h4>
<p>Suppose a system receives 2.5 requests per minute on average and requests follow a homogeneous Poisson process. The number $X$ received in four minutes satisfies $X\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(10)$, so</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(X=8)=e^{-10}\frac{10^8}{8!}\approx0.1126.
$$</div>
<p>For no requests in 30 seconds, the parameter is $\lambda=2.5\times0.5=1.25$, hence $P(X=0)=e^{-1.25}\approx0.2865$. Rescaling $\lambda$ to the observation window is the key first step.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> superposition requires independent counts; $\lambda$ is an expected count, not a probability; and equality of mean and variance is a defining diagnostic of the Poisson model. Real data with clustering, event interaction, time-varying rates, or heterogeneous subjects often exhibit overdispersion, with variance well above the mean. A Negative Binomial model, mixed Poisson model, or nonhomogeneous process may then be more appropriate.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-poisson-process-to-distribution">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Deriving the Poisson distribution from a Poisson process</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Distinguish the process rate from the distribution parameter</h4>
<p>Let $(N(t))_{t\ge0}$ be a homogeneous Poisson process with rate $r$, where $N(t)$ counts events up to time $t$. The rate $r$ is the <strong>expected number of events per unit time</strong>, whereas the Poisson parameter for an interval of length $t$ is $rt$. We will derive</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(N(t)=k)=e^{-rt}\frac{(rt)^k}{k!},
\qquad k=0,1,2,\ldots,
$$</div>
<p>and hence $N(t)\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(rt)$. If a text denotes the process rate by $\lambda$, the same statement is $N(t)\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda t)$; the rate $\lambda$ and the fixed-horizon mean $\lambda t$ should not be confused.</p>
<h4>2. What happens in an infinitesimal interval?</h4>
<p>A homogeneous Poisson process has independent, stationary increments. For a short interval of length $h$, it also satisfies</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\begin{aligned}
P(N(t+h)-N(t)=0)&amp;=1-rh+o(h),\\
P(N(t+h)-N(t)=1)&amp;=rh+o(h),\\
P(N(t+h)-N(t)\ge2)&amp;=o(h),
\end{aligned}
$$</div>
<p>where $o(h)/h\to0$. Thus the probability of one event is approximately proportional to the interval length, while two or more events in an infinitesimal interval have smaller-order probability. Define</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
p_k(t)=P(N(t)=k).
$$</div>
<h4>3. First derive the zero-event probability</h4>
<p>For there to be no events in $[0,t+h]$, there must be no events during the first $t$ units or during the final interval of length $h$. Independent increments give</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
p_0(t+h)=p_0(t)\bigl(1-rh\bigr)+o(h).
$$</div>
<p>Subtract $p_0(t)$, divide by $h$, and let $h\to0$:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
p_0'(t)=-rp_0(t),\qquad p_0(0)=1.
$$</div>
<p>Therefore</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
p_0(t)=e^{-rt}.
$$</div>
<p>The exponential factor in the Poisson PMF is therefore exactly the probability of observing no events during an interval of length $t$.</p>
<h4>4. Write the recursion for a general count</h4>
<p>For $k\ge1$, there are two first-order ways to have $N(t+h)=k$:</p>
<ul>
<li>there are already $k$ events by time $t$, followed by no event in the final interval;</li>
<li>there are $k-1$ events by time $t$, followed by exactly one event.</li>
</ul>
<p>Two or more events in the last short interval contribute only $o(h)$, so</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
p_k(t+h)
=p_k(t)(1-rh)+p_{k-1}(t)rh+o(h).
$$</div>
<p>Rearranging gives the Kolmogorov forward equations</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
p_k'(t)=-rp_k(t)+rp_{k-1}(t),
\qquad p_k(0)=0.
$$</div>
<p>Multiplying by the integrating factor $e^{rt}$ gives</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm dt}\!\left(e^{rt}p_k(t)\right)
=r e^{rt}p_{k-1}(t).
$$</div>
<p>We already know $p_0(t)=e^{-rt}$. If, inductively,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
p_{k-1}(t)=e^{-rt}\frac{(rt)^{k-1}}{(k-1)!},
$$</div>
<p>then integration from $0$ to $t$ gives</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\begin{aligned}
e^{rt}p_k(t)
&amp;=\int_0^t r\frac{(rs)^{k-1}}{(k-1)!}\,\mathrm ds\\
&amp;=\frac{(rt)^k}{k!}.
\end{aligned}
$$</div>
<p>Consequently, for every $k\ge0$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\boxed{P(N(t)=k)=e^{-rt}\frac{(rt)^k}{k!}}.
$$</div>
<h4>5. The same result through a Binomial-limit intuition</h4>
<p>Partition $[0,t]$ into $n$ intervals of length $t/n$. Each interval contains one event with probability approximately $rt/n$, contains multiple events with negligible probability, and is independent of the other intervals. Hence</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
N(t)\approx\operatorname{Binomial}\!\left(n,\frac{rt}{n}\right).
$$</div>
<p>For fixed $k$, let $n\to\infty$:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\begin{aligned}
P(N(t)=k)
&amp;\approx
\binom nk\left(\frac{rt}{n}\right)^k
\left(1-\frac{rt}{n}\right)^{n-k}\\
&amp;\longrightarrow
\frac{(rt)^k}{k!}e^{-rt}.
\end{aligned}
$$</div>
<p>The approximation $\binom nk\sim n^k/k!$ produces the factor $1/k!$, while $(1-rt/n)^n\to e^{-rt}$ produces the exponential factor. A Poisson count can thus be viewed as the sum of rare Bernoulli events across infinitely many infinitesimal intervals.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Derivation map:</strong> independent stationary increments and small-interval probabilities $\Rightarrow$ forward differential equations $\Rightarrow$ $p_0(t)=e^{-rt}$ $\Rightarrow$ the full sequence $p_k(t)$. The differential-equation route is the exact derivation; the Binomial limit supplies the most useful intuition.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

- □ Multinomial and occupancy problems.

*Continuous families:*

- □ Uniform(a,b): location-scale and geometric probability.

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-exponential-distribution">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Exponential$(\lambda)$ distribution</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Definition: waiting for the next event</h4>
<p>If $T\sim\operatorname{Exponential}(\lambda)$, where $\lambda&gt;0$ is the event rate per unit time, then for $t\ge0$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
f_T(t)=\lambda e^{-\lambda t},\qquad
F_T(t)=1-e^{-\lambda t},\qquad
P(T&gt;t)=e^{-\lambda t}.
$$</div>
<p>The survival function $P(T&gt;t)$ is often more useful than the CDF because it directly gives the probability of waiting longer than $t$. The mean and variance are</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[T]=\frac1\lambda,\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(T)=\frac1{\lambda^2}.
$$</div>
<p>Thus $\lambda$ is a <strong>rate</strong>, not a mean waiting time: a larger rate means a shorter wait. The hazard rate is constant:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
h(t)=\frac{f_T(t)}{P(T&gt;t)}=\lambda.
$$</div>
<p>Conditional on having waited until time $t$, the instantaneous event intensity does not depend on how long one has already waited.</p>
<h4>2. What does memorylessness really mean?</h4>
<p>For any $s,t\ge0$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\begin{aligned}
P(T&gt;s+t\mid T&gt;s)
&amp;=\frac{P(T&gt;s+t)}{P(T&gt;s)}\\
&amp;=\frac{e^{-\lambda(s+t)}}{e^{-\lambda s}}
=e^{-\lambda t}
=P(T&gt;t).
\end{aligned}
$$</div>
<p>After waiting $s$ time units, the residual waiting time, conditional on $T&gt;s$, still has the same $\operatorname{Exponential}(\lambda)$ distribution. This does not erase the past; it says the amount of elapsed waiting does not change the conditional law of the future. Among nondegenerate continuous distributions, Exponential is the unique memoryless family.</p>
<h4>3. Minimum of independent exponentials: competing risks</h4>
<p>Let independent $T_i\sim\operatorname{Exponential}(\lambda_i)$ and define $T_{\min}=\min_iT_i$. Multiplying survival functions gives</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(T_{\min}&gt;t)
=\prod_iP(T_i&gt;t)
=\exp\!\left(-t\sum_i\lambda_i\right),
$$</div>
<p>so</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
T_{\min}\sim\operatorname{Exponential}(\Lambda),
\qquad
\Lambda=\sum_i\lambda_i.
$$</div>
<p>The probability that clock $i$ rings first is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(T_i=T_{\min})=\frac{\lambda_i}{\sum_j\lambda_j}.
$$</div>
<p>The first-event time is independent of the identity of the winning clock. This result appears in competing failures, order arrivals, and continuous-time Markov chains. The <strong>minimum</strong> remains Exponential; the maximum generally does not.</p>
<h4>4. Links to Poisson processes, Gamma variables, and simulation</h4>
<p>In a homogeneous Poisson process with rate $\lambda$, the wait to the next arrival is $\operatorname{Exponential}(\lambda)$ because</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(T&gt;t)=P(N(t)=0)=e^{-\lambda t}.
$$</div>
<p>The total wait for $k$ arrivals is a sum of $k$ independent Exponential variables and follows an Erlang distribution, an integer-shape Gamma, rather than another Exponential distribution. If $U\sim\operatorname{Uniform}(0,1)$, inverse-transform sampling gives</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
T=-\frac{\log U}{\lambda}
\sim\operatorname{Exponential}(\lambda).
$$</div>
<p>If $a&gt;0$, then $aT\sim\operatorname{Exponential}(\lambda/a)$. Changing the time unit therefore requires changing the rate as well.</p>
<h4>5. Worked example</h4>
<p>Two independent servers complete jobs at rates 2 and 3 per minute. The next completion time is $\operatorname{Exponential}(5)$ with mean $1/5$ minute, and the probability that the second server finishes first is $3/(2+3)=3/5$.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> do not confuse rate $\lambda$ with mean $1/\lambda$; a sum of independent Exponentials is generally Gamma, not Exponential; and memorylessness does not claim real equipment never ages. It is a constant-hazard modeling assumption and should not be imposed when the true hazard changes over time.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-gamma-beta-dirichlet">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Gamma, Erlang, Beta, and Dirichlet distributions</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Gamma: positive waiting times and rate parameters</h4>
<p>We use the shape-rate convention throughout. If $X\sim\operatorname{Gamma}(\alpha,\beta)$, with shape $\alpha&gt;0$ and rate $\beta&gt;0$, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
f_X(x)=\frac{\beta^\alpha}{\Gamma(\alpha)}
x^{\alpha-1}e^{-\beta x},\qquad x&gt;0,
$$</div>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[X]=\frac{\alpha}{\beta},\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(X)=\frac{\alpha}{\beta^2}.
$$</div>
<p>Some books instead use the scale $\theta=1/\beta$ and also write $\operatorname{Gamma}(\alpha,\theta)$. Always identify whether the second parameter is a rate or a scale. If independent $X_i\sim\operatorname{Gamma}(\alpha_i,\beta)$ share the same rate, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\sum_iX_i\sim\operatorname{Gamma}\!\left(\sum_i\alpha_i,\beta\right).
$$</div>
<h4>2. Erlang: the integer-shape Gamma special case</h4>
<p>When $\alpha=k$ is a positive integer, Gamma is also called an Erlang distribution. If $T_1,\ldots,T_k$ are independent $\operatorname{Exponential}(\lambda)$ waiting times, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
S_k=T_1+\cdots+T_k\sim\operatorname{Erlang}(k,\lambda)
=\operatorname{Gamma}(k,\lambda).
$$</div>
<p>In a rate-$\lambda$ Poisson process, $S_k$ is exactly the arrival time of event $k$ and</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(S_k\le t)=P(N(t)\ge k).
$$</div>
<p>Poisson asks how many events occur by a fixed time; Erlang asks how long it takes to observe event $k$. Setting $k=1$ recovers Exponential.</p>
<h4>3. Beta: probabilities and proportions on $[0,1]$</h4>
<p>If $P\sim\operatorname{Beta}(\alpha,\beta)$, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
f_P(p)=\frac{p^{\alpha-1}(1-p)^{\beta-1}}{B(\alpha,\beta)},
\qquad 0&lt;p&lt;1,
$$</div>
<div class="math-display">$$
B(\alpha,\beta)=\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(\beta)}
{\Gamma(\alpha+\beta)},\qquad
\mathbb E[P]=\frac{\alpha}{\alpha+\beta},
$$</div>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{Var}(P)
=\frac{\alpha\beta}
{(\alpha+\beta)^2(\alpha+\beta+1)}.
$$</div>
<p>The parameters $\alpha$ and $\beta$ may be viewed as prior strengths for successes and failures. A larger sum makes the distribution more concentrated. Beta$(1,1)$ is Uniform$(0,1)$; when both parameters exceed 1 the density is typically unimodal, while parameters below 1 push mass toward 0 and 1.</p>
<h4>4. Dirichlet: the simplex generalization of Beta</h4>
<p>Let $\mathbf P=(P_1,\ldots,P_K)$ with $P_i\ge0$ and $\sum_iP_i=1$. If $\mathbf P\sim\operatorname{Dirichlet}(\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_K)$ and $\alpha_0=\sum_i\alpha_i$, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
f(\mathbf p)
=\frac{\Gamma(\alpha_0)}{\prod_i\Gamma(\alpha_i)}
\prod_{i=1}^Kp_i^{\alpha_i-1},
$$</div>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[P_i]=\frac{\alpha_i}{\alpha_0},\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(P_i)
=\frac{\alpha_i(\alpha_0-\alpha_i)}
{\alpha_0^2(\alpha_0+1)},
$$</div>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{Cov}(P_i,P_j)
=-\frac{\alpha_i\alpha_j}
{\alpha_0^2(\alpha_0+1)},\qquad i\ne j.
$$</div>
<p>The negative covariance is natural: because the components sum to 1, increasing one component squeezes the others. Each marginal satisfies $P_i\sim\operatorname{Beta}(\alpha_i,\alpha_0-\alpha_i)$, and the $K=2$ Dirichlet reduces to Beta.</p>
<h4>5. Beta-Gamma structure and conjugate priors</h4>
<p>If independent $G_1\sim\operatorname{Gamma}(\alpha,\rho)$ and $G_2\sim\operatorname{Gamma}(\beta,\rho)$ share the same rate, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\frac{G_1}{G_1+G_2}\sim\operatorname{Beta}(\alpha,\beta),
\qquad
G_1+G_2\sim\operatorname{Gamma}(\alpha+\beta,\rho),
$$</div>
<p>and the ratio is independent of the sum. Normalizing several independent Gamma variables by their total produces a Dirichlet vector.</p>
<p>These families are common in Bayesian inference because the posterior stays in the same family:</p>
<ul>
<li>Beta prior with a Bernoulli/Binomial likelihood: after $s$ successes and $f$ failures, $\operatorname{Beta}(\alpha,\beta)$ becomes $\operatorname{Beta}(\alpha+s,\beta+f)$;</li>
<li>Dirichlet prior with a Categorical/Multinomial likelihood: after observing $n_i$ outcomes in class $i$, update $\alpha_i$ to $\alpha_i+n_i$;</li>
<li>Gamma prior for a Poisson rate: with total exposure $T$ and total count $S$, $\operatorname{Gamma}(\alpha,\beta)$ becomes $\operatorname{Gamma}(\alpha+S,\beta+T)$.</li>
</ul>
<h4>6. Worked example</h4>
<p>Use a $\operatorname{Beta}(2,2)$ prior for a coin's head probability. After 8 heads and 2 tails, the posterior is $\operatorname{Beta}(10,4)$ with posterior mean $10/14\approx0.714$. It differs from the sample proportion $8/10$ because the prior provides smoothing.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> identify Gamma's rate-versus-scale convention; Erlang requires an integer shape; Beta's $\alpha,\beta$ are not interval endpoints; and Dirichlet components are not independent because they must sum to 1. Conjugacy makes computation convenient but does not automatically make a prior substantively appropriate.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-normal-distributions">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Normal and Multivariate Normal distributions</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Univariate Normal and standardization</h4>
<p>If $X\sim N(\mu,\sigma^2)$, where $\sigma&gt;0$ is the standard deviation, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
f_X(x)=\frac1{\sqrt{2\pi}\sigma}
\exp\!\left[-\frac{(x-\mu)^2}{2\sigma^2}\right].
$$</div>
<p>The distribution is symmetric about $\mu$, with $\mathbb E[X]=\mu$ and $\operatorname{Var}(X)=\sigma^2$. Standardization gives</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
Z=\frac{X-\mu}{\sigma}\sim N(0,1),
$$</div>
<p>reducing any univariate Normal probability to the standard Normal CDF $\Phi$. Roughly $68\%,95\%,99.7\%$ of the mass lies within $1,2,3$ standard deviations of the mean. This is a useful magnitude check, not a replacement for exact calculation.</p>
<h4>2. Affine transformations, linear combinations, and addition</h4>
<p>If $X\sim N(\mu,\sigma^2)$, then for constants $a,b$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
aX+b\sim N(a\mu+b,a^2\sigma^2).
$$</div>
<p>If $X$ and $Y$ are jointly Normal, every linear combination $aX+bY$ is Normal, and its variance must include the covariance term:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{Var}(aX+bY)
=a^2\operatorname{Var}(X)+b^2\operatorname{Var}(Y)
+2ab\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y).
$$</div>
<p>In particular, a sum of independent Normal variables is Normal, with means and variances added. The CLT explains why sums of many small independent effects are often approximately Normal; it does not imply that every real-world variable itself should be modeled as Normal.</p>
<h4>3. Multivariate Normal definition and geometry</h4>
<p>A random vector $\mathbf X\in\mathbb R^d$ has distribution $N_d(\boldsymbol\mu,\boldsymbol\Sigma)$ if every fixed linear combination $\mathbf a^{\mathsf T}\mathbf X$ is univariate Normal. Here</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[\mathbf X]=\boldsymbol\mu,\qquad
\operatorname{Cov}(\mathbf X)=\boldsymbol\Sigma,
$$</div>
<p>and $\boldsymbol\Sigma$ must be symmetric positive semidefinite. If it is positive definite, the density is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
f(\mathbf x)=
\frac{\exp\!\left[-\tfrac12(\mathbf x-\boldsymbol\mu)^{\mathsf T}
\boldsymbol\Sigma^{-1}(\mathbf x-\boldsymbol\mu)\right]}
{(2\pi)^{d/2}|\boldsymbol\Sigma|^{1/2}}.
$$</div>
<p>Equal-density contours are ellipsoids whose orientation and scaling are determined by $\boldsymbol\Sigma$. If $\mathbf Y=A\mathbf X+\mathbf b$, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbf Y\sim N(A\boldsymbol\mu+\mathbf b,\,
A\boldsymbol\Sigma A^{\mathsf T}).
$$</div>
<p>Marginals, portfolio returns $\mathbf w^{\mathsf T}\mathbf X$, and Gaussian linear models can therefore be handled directly with matrix operations.</p>
<h4>4. When does zero covariance imply independence?</h4>
<p>In general, independence implies zero covariance, but zero covariance does not imply independence. The special property is that if $(X,Y)$ is <strong>jointly Normal</strong>, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=0
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
X\ \text{and}\ Y\ \text{are independent}.
$$</div>
<p>Knowing only that both marginals are Normal is insufficient. For example, let $X\sim N(0,1)$, let an independent $S$ take $\pm1$ with equal probability, and define $Y=SX$. Then $Y\sim N(0,1)$ and $\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=\mathbb E[SX^2]=0$, but $Y$ is always either $X$ or $-X$. The pair is dependent and not jointly Normal.</p>
<h4>5. Conditional Normal formula</h4>
<p>Partition the mean and covariance as</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\begin{pmatrix}\mathbf X_A\\ \mathbf X_B\end{pmatrix}
\sim N\!\left(
\begin{pmatrix}\boldsymbol\mu_A\\ \boldsymbol\mu_B\end{pmatrix},
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol\Sigma_{AA}&amp;\boldsymbol\Sigma_{AB}\\
\boldsymbol\Sigma_{BA}&amp;\boldsymbol\Sigma_{BB}
\end{pmatrix}
\right).
$$</div>
<p>If $\boldsymbol\Sigma_{BB}$ is invertible, then conditional on $\mathbf X_B=\mathbf b$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbf X_A\mid\mathbf X_B=\mathbf b
\sim N\!\left(
\boldsymbol\mu_A+
\boldsymbol\Sigma_{AB}\boldsymbol\Sigma_{BB}^{-1}
(\mathbf b-\boldsymbol\mu_B),\,
\boldsymbol\Sigma_{AA}-
\boldsymbol\Sigma_{AB}\boldsymbol\Sigma_{BB}^{-1}
\boldsymbol\Sigma_{BA}
\right).
$$</div>
<p>The conditional mean is linear in the observation. The conditional covariance is the Schur complement, does not depend on the observed value, and does not exceed the original marginal uncertainty. In the bivariate case,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
X\mid Y=y\sim N\!\left(
\mu_X+\rho\frac{\sigma_X}{\sigma_Y}(y-\mu_Y),\,
\sigma_X^2(1-\rho^2)
\right).
$$</div>
<h4>6. Simulation, quadratic forms, and interview applications</h4>
<p>If $\boldsymbol\Sigma=LL^{\mathsf T}$ is a Cholesky factorization and $\mathbf Z\sim N_d(\mathbf0,I)$, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbf X=\boldsymbol\mu+L\mathbf Z
\sim N_d(\boldsymbol\mu,\boldsymbol\Sigma).
$$</div>
<p>When $\boldsymbol\Sigma$ is positive definite, the Mahalanobis quadratic form satisfies</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
(\mathbf X-\boldsymbol\mu)^{\mathsf T}
\boldsymbol\Sigma^{-1}
(\mathbf X-\boldsymbol\mu)\sim\chi_d^2.
$$</div>
<p>This connects Multivariate Normal distributions to confidence ellipsoids and anomaly detection. For portfolio weights $\mathbf w$, if the return vector is approximately Multivariate Normal, the portfolio mean and variance are $\mathbf w^{\mathsf T}\boldsymbol\mu$ and $\mathbf w^{\mathsf T}\boldsymbol\Sigma\mathbf w$.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> the second parameter of $N(\mu,\sigma^2)$ is usually the variance, not the standard deviation; Normal marginals do not guarantee a jointly Normal vector; zero covariance guarantees independence only under joint Normality; and a positive-semidefinite singular $\boldsymbol\Sigma$ produces a degenerate distribution with no ordinary $d$-dimensional density, so formulas containing $\boldsymbol\Sigma^{-1}$ cannot be used directly.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

- □ Lognormal: positive multiplicative quantities; arithmetic versus geometric return.
~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-chi-square-t-f">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Chi-Square, Student $t$, and $F$: Sampling Distributions for Variance Estimation and Classical Tests</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. All three arise from one construction chain</h4>
<p>Start with independent standard Normal variables:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
Z_1,\ldots,Z_\nu\overset{\mathrm{i.i.d.}}{\sim}N(0,1).
$$</div>
<p>A sum of squares produces Chi-square; a standard Normal divided by the root mean square of an independent Chi-square produces $t$; and a ratio of two independent Chi-square mean squares produces $F$:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\sum_{i=1}^{\nu}Z_i^2\sim\chi_\nu^2,
$$</div>
<div class="math-display">$$
\frac{Z}{\sqrt{V/\nu}}\sim t_\nu,
\qquad Z\sim N(0,1),\quad V\sim\chi_\nu^2,\quad Z\perp V,
$$</div>
<div class="math-display">$$
\frac{U/d_1}{V/d_2}\sim F_{d_1,d_2},
\qquad U\sim\chi_{d_1}^2,\quad V\sim\chi_{d_2}^2,\quad U\perp V.
$$</div>
<p>Degrees of freedom can be viewed as the number of independent directions remaining in the relevant random sum of squares.</p>
<h4>2. Chi-square: Normal sums of squares and sample variance</h4>
<p>If $Q\sim\chi_\nu^2$, then $Q\ge0$ and</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[Q]=\nu,\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(Q)=2\nu,
\qquad
\chi_\nu^2=\operatorname{Gamma}\!\left(\frac\nu2,\frac12\right),
$$</div>
<p>using a shape-rate Gamma convention. Small degrees of freedom give a strongly right-skewed law; larger degrees of freedom look increasingly Normal.</p>
<p>For $X_1,\ldots,X_n\overset{\mathrm{i.i.d.}}{\sim}N(\mu,\sigma^2)$, define</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
S^2=\frac1{n-1}\sum_{i=1}^n(X_i-\bar X)^2.
$$</div>
<p>Then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\frac{(n-1)S^2}{\sigma^2}\sim\chi_{n-1}^2.
$$</div>
<p>One degree of freedom is lost because the residuals satisfy $\sum_i(X_i-\bar X)=0$. This pivot gives an exact confidence interval for a Normal population variance:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P\!\left(
\chi^2_{\alpha/2,n-1}
\le\frac{(n-1)S^2}{\sigma^2}
\le\chi^2_{1-\alpha/2,n-1}
\right)=1-\alpha.
$$</div>
<p>Chi-square also underlies goodness-of-fit and contingency-table independence tests for counts. Those are usually large-sample approximations and require expected cells not to be too small.</p>
<h4>3. Student $t$: extra uncertainty from an unknown variance</h4>
<p>When the population is Normal but $\sigma$ is unknown, replace it by $S$. A key Normal-sample result is that $\bar X$ and $S^2$ are independent, so</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
T=\frac{\bar X-\mu}{S/\sqrt n}\sim t_{n-1}.
$$</div>
<p>$t_\nu$ is symmetric around 0 but has heavier tails than a standard Normal because the denominator $S$ is random. Smaller degrees of freedom mean a noisier variance estimate and heavier tails. As $\nu\to\infty$, $t_\nu$ approaches $N(0,1)$.</p>
<p>The $t$ law supports confidence intervals for a mean with unknown variance, one-sample/paired/two-sample tests, and studentized tests for individual regression coefficients. For $\nu&gt;2$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{Var}(t_\nu)=\frac{\nu}{\nu-2}.
$$</div>
<h4>4. $F$: a ratio of two independent variance scales</h4>
<p>$F_{d_1,d_2}$ is nonnegative and usually right-skewed. Two important identities are</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\frac1{F_{d_1,d_2}}\sim F_{d_2,d_1},
\qquad
t_\nu^2\sim F_{1,\nu}.
$$</div>
<p>For two independent Normal samples, under $H_0:\sigma_1^2=\sigma_2^2$, an appropriately ordered $S_1^2/S_2^2$ has an $F$ distribution. ANOVA compares</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
F=\frac{\text{between-group mean square}}
{\text{within-group mean square}}.
$$</div>
<p>Under equal group means, both numerator and denominator estimate the same error variance, so the ratio should be near 1. A large $F$ argues against the null. Overall regression significance and nested-model joint tests similarly compare explained sum-of-squares gains with residual mean square.</p>
<h4>5. Quick selection guide</h4>
<ul>
<li>A Normal population <strong>variance itself</strong>, or deviations in categorical counts: consider $\chi^2$.</li>
<li>A mean or regression coefficient with error variance estimated from the sample: consider $t$.</li>
<li>A ratio of variance scales, ANOVA, or a joint test of several regression restrictions: consider $F$.</li>
</ul>
<p>The raw data need not “follow a $t$ or $F$ distribution.” A standardized statistic has that sampling distribution when the null model and assumptions hold.</p>
<h4>6. Assumptions and traps</h4>
<p>Exact small-sample $t$, variance Chi-square, and classical $F$ results rely on Normal errors. The variance-ratio $F$ test is especially sensitive to non-Normality and outliers. ANOVA also requires independence and, classically, equal variances; Welch ANOVA addresses unequal variances. Contingency-table Chi-square is asymptotic, while sparse small samples may require Fisher's exact test.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> degrees of freedom are not synonymous with sample size; replacing $\sigma$ by $S$ means standard Normal critical values are no longer exact; $t^2$ equals $F_{1,\nu}$ but two-sided $t$ and one-sided $F$ p-value conventions must match; a large test statistic is not automatically a large effect; and numerator/denominator degrees of freedom must be ordered correctly.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~
- □ Pareto\/heavy tails: tail risk, unstable high moments and why Gaussian intuition can fail for returns.

=== 1.4 Expectation, variance and covariance \[P0\]

- □ Linearity: $\\mathbb E(aX\\mathplus{}bY\\mathplus{}c)=a\\mathbb E X\\mathplus{}b\\mathbb E Y\\mathplus{}c$, with no independence assumption.
- □ Indicator method: $\\mathbb E(\\sum_i I_i)=\\sum_iP(I_i=1)$; use it for matches, collisions, inversions and occupancy.
- □ Tail-sum formula for nonnegative integer $X$: $\\mathbb E X=\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}P(X\\ge k)$.
- □ $\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\mathbb E(X^2)-(\\mathbb E X)^2$; $\\operatorname{Var}(aX\\mathplus{}b)=a^2\\operatorname{Var}(X)$.

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-covariance-correlation">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Covariance and Correlation</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Covariance measures shared linear movement</h4>
<p>When second moments exist,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)
=\mathbb E[(X-\mathbb E X)(Y-\mathbb E Y)]
=\mathbb E[XY]-\mathbb E[X]\mathbb E[Y].
$$</div>
<p>Positive covariance means the variables tend to deviate from their means in the same direction; negative covariance means opposite directions. A value near zero indicates little <strong>linear</strong> co-movement only. Its units are the product of the units of $X$ and $Y$, so its magnitude is not directly comparable across scales.</p>
<h4>2. Bilinearity and the variance of a sum</h4>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{Cov}(aX+b,cY+d)=ac\,\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y),
$$</div>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{Cov}(X,X)=\operatorname{Var}(X).
$$</div>
<p>Consequently,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{Var}\!\left(\sum_i a_iX_i\right)
=\sum_i a_i^2\operatorname{Var}(X_i)
+2\sum_{i&lt;j}a_ia_j\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,X_j).
$$</div>
<p>Variances add only when the cross-covariances are zero. Independence is sufficient but not necessary.</p>
<h4>3. Correlation standardizes the scale</h4>
<p>When $\sigma_X,\sigma_Y&gt;0$, Pearson correlation is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\rho_{X,Y}
=\operatorname{Corr}(X,Y)
=\frac{\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)}{\sigma_X\sigma_Y},
\qquad -1\le\rho_{X,Y}\le1.
$$</div>
<p>The bound follows from Cauchy–Schwarz. Positive rescaling and translation leave correlation unchanged; multiplying one variable by a negative number flips its sign. Equality $|\rho|=1$ holds exactly when the centered variables are proportional almost surely, or equivalently when $Y=aX+b$ exactly.</p>
<h4>4. Zero correlation is not independence</h4>
<p>If $X,Y$ are independent and have finite second moments, then $\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=0$. The converse usually fails. For example, with $X\sim N(0,1)$ and $Y=X^2$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=\mathbb E[X^3]-\mathbb E[X]\mathbb E[X^2]=0,
$$</div>
<p>yet $Y$ is completely determined by $X$. Correlation captures linear dependence and can miss U-shaped, periodic, or other nonlinear relationships. Zero covariance implies independence only under special structure such as joint Normality.</p>
<h4>5. Covariance matrices</h4>
<p>For a random vector $\mathbf X$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\boldsymbol\Sigma
=\mathbb E[(\mathbf X-\boldsymbol\mu)
(\mathbf X-\boldsymbol\mu)^{\mathsf T}]
$$</div>
<p>is symmetric and positive semidefinite because for every $\mathbf a$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbf a^{\mathsf T}\boldsymbol\Sigma\mathbf a
=\operatorname{Var}(\mathbf a^{\mathsf T}\mathbf X)\ge0.
$$</div>
<p>This is the portfolio-variance formula. A matrix that is not positive semidefinite cannot be a valid covariance matrix.</p>
<h4>6. Sample estimation and conditional decomposition</h4>
<p>The usual unbiased sample covariance is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\widehat{\operatorname{Cov}}(X,Y)
=\frac1{n-1}\sum_{i=1}^n
(x_i-\bar x)(y_i-\bar y).
$$</div>
<p>Given another variable $Z$, the law of total covariance is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)
=\mathbb E[\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y\mid Z)]
+\operatorname{Cov}(\mathbb E[X\mid Z],\mathbb E[Y\mid Z]).
$$</div>
<p>Overall association can come from within-group co-movement or only from differences between group means, a key mechanism behind Simpson's paradox.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> correlation is not causation; zero correlation is not independence; Pearson correlation is sensitive to outliers; it is undefined when either variance is zero; and a high sample correlation can be driven by common trends, selection, or small-sample noise.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

- □ $\\operatorname{Var}(\\sum_iX_i)=\\sum_i\\operatorname{Var}(X_i)\\mathplus{}2\\sum_{i<j}\\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,X_j)$.
- □ Law of total expectation: $\\mathbb E X=\\mathbb E(\\mathbb E(X\\mid Y))$.
- □ Law of total variance: $\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\mathbb E(\\operatorname{Var}(X\\mid Y))\\mathplus{}\\operatorname{Var}(\\mathbb E(X\\mid Y))$.
- □ Conditional covariance and covariance matrices.

*Interview reflex:* try indicators, symmetry, conditioning, recursion, or a state equation before brute-force enumeration.

=== 1.5 Order statistics and geometric probability \[P1\]

- □ For i.i.d. samples with CDF $F$, $P(\\max_iX_i\\le x)=F(x)^n$ and $P(\\min_iX_i>x)=(1-F(x))^n$.

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-kth-order-statistic">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Density of the $k$-th Order Statistic</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Setup and formula</h4>
<p>Let $X_1,\ldots,X_n$ be i.i.d. from a continuous distribution with CDF $F$ and density $f$, and write $X_{(1)}\le\cdots\le X_{(n)}$ for the sorted sample. Then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
f_{X_{(k)}}(x)=
\frac{n!}{(k-1)!(n-k)!}
[F(x)]^{k-1}[1-F(x)]^{n-k}f(x).
$$</div>
<h4>2. Where does the formula come from?</h4>
<p>For $X_{(k)}$ to lie in a short interval $[x,x+\mathrm dx]$, approximately:</p>
<ul>
<li>exactly $k-1$ observations lie below $x$, contributing $F(x)^{k-1}$;</li>
<li>one observation lies in $[x,x+\mathrm dx]$, contributing $f(x)\,\mathrm dx$;</li>
<li>the remaining $n-k$ observations exceed $x$, contributing $[1-F(x)]^{n-k}$;</li>
<li>the observations can take these roles in $\frac{n!}{(k-1)!1!(n-k)!}$ ways.</li>
</ul>
<p>Multiply the factors, divide by $\mathrm dx$, and let $\mathrm dx\to0$. Equivalently, start from</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(X_{(k)}\le x)=\sum_{j=k}^n\binom nj[F(x)]^j[1-F(x)]^{n-j},
$$</div>
<p>because $X_{(k)}\le x$ exactly when at least $k$ observations do not exceed $x$, and then differentiate.</p>
<h4>3. Beta transform and useful special cases</h4>
<p>The probability integral transform gives $F(X_{(k)})\sim\operatorname{Beta}(k,n+1-k)$. Hence, for a Uniform$(0,1)$ sample,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
U_{(k)}\sim\operatorname{Beta}(k,n+1-k),\qquad
\mathbb E[U_{(k)}]=\frac{k}{n+1},\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(U_{(k)})=\frac{k(n+1-k)}{(n+1)^2(n+2)}.
$$</div>
<p>Setting $k=1$ or $k=n$ gives</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
f_{X_{(1)}}(x)=n[1-F(x)]^{n-1}f(x),\qquad
f_{X_{(n)}}(x)=n[F(x)]^{n-1}f(x).
$$</div>
<h4>4. Small example</h4>
<p>For $n=5$ independent Uniform$(0,1)$ observations, the second-smallest value has density</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
f_{U_{(2)}}(x)=\frac{5!}{1!3!}x(1-x)^3=20x(1-x)^3,\qquad 0&lt;x&lt;1,
$$</div>
<p>and mean $\mathbb E[U_{(2)}]=2/6=1/3$. Integrating the density to 1 is a quick check.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the compact formula uses i.i.d. observations and continuity, which makes ties have probability zero. Dependence, non-identical distributions, or point masses require a different joint-distribution or tie calculation.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-uniform-order-statistic-mean">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Uniform$(0,1)$ Order Statistic: $\mathbb E[U_{(k)}]=k/(n+1)$</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Result and full distribution</h4>
<p>Let $U_1,\ldots,U_n$ be i.i.d. Uniform$(0,1)$ and sort them as</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
U_{(1)}\le U_{(2)}\le\cdots\le U_{(n)}.
$$</div>
<p>Then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
U_{(k)}\sim\operatorname{Beta}(k,n+1-k),
$$</div>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[U_{(k)}]=\frac{k}{n+1},\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(U_{(k)})
=\frac{k(n+1-k)}{(n+1)^2(n+2)}.
$$</div>
<p>The denominator is $n+1$, not $n$, because the $n$ random points together with the two endpoints create $n+1$ spacings.</p>
<h4>2. Direct derivation from the Beta density</h4>
<p>The density of order statistic $k$ is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
f_{U_{(k)}}(u)
=\frac{n!}{(k-1)!(n-k)!}
u^{k-1}(1-u)^{n-k},\qquad 0&lt;u&lt;1.
$$</div>
<p>This is the $\operatorname{Beta}(k,n+1-k)$ density. The Beta-function recurrence gives</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E[U_{(k)}]
&amp;=\frac{B(k+1,n+1-k)}{B(k,n+1-k)}\\
&amp;=\frac{k}{n+1}.
\end{aligned}
$$</div>
<h4>3. A more intuitive spacing proof</h4>
<p>Add endpoints $U_{(0)}=0,U_{(n+1)}=1$ and define</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
D_i=U_{(i)}-U_{(i-1)},\qquad i=1,\ldots,n+1.
$$</div>
<p>The vector $(D_1,\ldots,D_{n+1})$ is uniform on the simplex $\sum_iD_i=1,D_i\ge0$, or equivalently $\operatorname{Dirichlet}(1,\ldots,1)$. Symmetry and the unit total length give</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[D_i]=\frac1{n+1}.
$$</div>
<p>Since $U_{(k)}=D_1+\cdots+D_k$, linearity immediately gives</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[U_{(k)}]=\frac{k}{n+1}.
$$</div>
<h4>4. CDF and a general continuous distribution</h4>
<p>The event $U_{(k)}\le u$ means that at least $k$ observations fall in $[0,u]$, so</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(U_{(k)}\le u)
=\sum_{j=k}^n\binom nj u^j(1-u)^{n-j}.
$$</div>
<p>If $X_i$ have a general continuous CDF $F$, then $F(X_{(k)})$ has the same Beta distribution. However, in general,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[X_{(k)}]
\ne F^{-1}\!\left(\frac{k}{n+1}\right).
$$</div>
<p>The right side is a useful plotting-position approximation, not an exact expectation, because a nonlinear function cannot usually be moved through an expectation.</p>
<h4>5. Quick example</h4>
<p>For $n=9$, the third-smallest value has mean $3/10=0.3$; the minimum has mean $1/10$ and the maximum $9/10$. Symmetry also gives $\mathbb E[U_{(k)}]+\mathbb E[U_{(n+1-k)}]=1$.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> parentheses in $U_{(k)}$ mean order rank, not a function or power; the formula assumes an i.i.d. Uniform$(0,1)$ sample; the denominator is $n+1$; and for a general distribution one cannot interchange $F^{-1}$ and expectation.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-broken-stick-simplex">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Interval Lengths, Broken Sticks, Simplices, and Two-Dimensional Area Ratios</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. When does geometric probability equal an area ratio?</h4>
<p>If a random point is uniform on a region $R$ and an event corresponds to $A\subseteq R$, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P((X,Y)\in A)=\frac{\operatorname{Area}(A)}
{\operatorname{Area}(R)}.
$$</div>
<p>The key is that the joint density is constant in the chosen coordinates. With a nonuniform density one must integrate; after a change of variables one must include the Jacobian rather than use an unweighted area.</p>
<h4>2. Interval lengths generated by random cut points</h4>
<p>Choose $m$ independent Uniform$(0,1)$ cut points and order them as</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
0=U_{(0)}&lt;U_{(1)}&lt;\cdots&lt;U_{(m)}&lt;U_{(m+1)}=1.
$$</div>
<p>The adjacent lengths</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
L_i=U_{(i)}-U_{(i-1)},\qquad i=1,\ldots,m+1
$$</div>
<p>satisfy $L_i\ge0,\sum_iL_i=1$ and</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
(L_1,\ldots,L_{m+1})
\sim\operatorname{Dirichlet}(1,\ldots,1).
$$</div>
<p>The length vector is uniform on the probability simplex. Each marginal is $\operatorname{Beta}(1,m)$ with mean $1/(m+1)$, but the lengths are not independent because their sum is fixed.</p>
<h4>3. The classic broken-stick triangle problem</h4>
<p>Place two random cuts on a unit stick, producing lengths $L_1,L_2,L_3$. They form a nondegenerate triangle exactly when the longest piece is below $1/2$:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\max(L_1,L_2,L_3)&lt;\frac12.
$$</div>
<p>The length vector is uniform on the two-dimensional simplex $L_i\ge0,\sum_iL_i=1$. The event $L_i\ge1/2$ is a corner triangle scaled by $1/2$ in each dimension, so its area ratio is $(1/2)^2=1/4$. The three bad regions are disjoint apart from boundaries; hence</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(\text{the pieces form a triangle})
=1-3\cdot\frac14=\frac14.
$$</div>
<h4>4. One cut and the longer interval</h4>
<p>With one cut $U\sim\operatorname{Uniform}(0,1)$, the pieces are $U$ and $1-U$. The expected longer piece is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[\max(U,1-U)]
=2\int_{1/2}^1u\,\mathrm du
=\frac34.
$$</div>
<p>With several cuts, the maximum spacing can be handled through inclusion–exclusion or truncated-simplex geometry. Treating the dependent spacings as independent Beta variables is a common error.</p>
<h4>5. A two-dimensional feasible-region example</h4>
<p>If independent $U,V\sim\operatorname{Uniform}(0,1)$, the event $U+V\le1$ is a right triangle inside the unit square, so</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(U+V\le1)=\frac{1/2}{1}=\frac12.
$$</div>
<p>For questions about two arrival times being within $c$ of each other or three pieces satisfying triangle inequalities, draw the support first and turn the inequalities into a feasible region. The standard $d$-simplex $\{x_i\ge0,\sum_{i=1}^dx_i\le1\}$ has volume $1/d!$.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> the original cut points are independent but ordered spacings are not; include the endpoint spacings; use raw area or volume ratios only under a uniform joint law; and when changing from cut coordinates to length coordinates, verify the support, dimension, and Jacobian.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

- □ Exchangeability and symmetry shortcuts.

=== 1.6 Limits, inequalities and approximations \[P1\]

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-core-probability-inequalities">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Jensen, Cauchy–Schwarz, Markov, and Chebyshev Inequalities</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Jensen: averaging before a convex transformation is smaller</h4>
<p>If $\varphi$ is convex and the relevant expectations exist, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\varphi(\mathbb E[X])\le\mathbb E[\varphi(X)].
$$</div>
<p>For concave $\varphi$, the direction reverses. Geometrically, a convex graph lies below every secant line. Common examples are</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
(\mathbb E[X])^2\le\mathbb E[X^2],
\qquad
\mathbb E[\log X]\le\log\mathbb E[X]\quad(X&gt;0).
$$</div>
<p>For strictly convex $\varphi$, equality normally requires $X$ to be constant almost surely; more generally, the support of $X$ must lie where $\varphi$ is affine.</p>
<h4>2. Cauchy–Schwarz: an inner product is at most the product of lengths</h4>
<p>For square-integrable $X,Y$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
|\mathbb E[XY]|
\le\sqrt{\mathbb E[X^2]\mathbb E[Y^2]}.
$$</div>
<p>Applying it to centered variables gives</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
|\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)|
\le\sigma_X\sigma_Y,
$$</div>
<p>and hence $|\rho|\le1$. Equality holds when $X$ and $Y$ are linearly dependent in $L^2$, meaning $X=cY$ almost surely for some constant $c$, aside from degenerate cases.</p>
<h4>3. Markov: control a tail using only a nonnegative mean</h4>
<p>If $X\ge0$ and $a&gt;0$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(X\ge a)\le\frac{\mathbb E[X]}a.
$$</div>
<p>The proof is one line: $X\ge a\,\mathbf1_{\{X\ge a\}}$, then take expectations. Its weak assumptions make it widely applicable but often loose. If the right-hand side exceeds 1, cap the bound at 1.</p>
<h4>4. Chebyshev: control two-sided deviations using mean and variance</h4>
<p>If $\mathbb E[X]=\mu$ and $\operatorname{Var}(X)=\sigma^2&lt;\infty$, then for $t&gt;0$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(|X-\mu|\ge t)\le\frac{\sigma^2}{t^2}.
$$</div>
<p>This is Markov applied to the nonnegative variable $(X-\mu)^2$:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P((X-\mu)^2\ge t^2)
\le\frac{\mathbb E[(X-\mu)^2]}{t^2}.
$$</div>
<p>Equivalently, $P(|X-\mu|\ge k\sigma)\le1/k^2$. No Normality, symmetry, or independence is required; finite variance is enough.</p>
<h4>5. How to choose</h4>
<ul>
<li>An expectation composed with a function: inspect convexity and use Jensen.</li>
<li>A product, covariance, or inner product: try Cauchy–Schwarz.</li>
<li>A nonnegative variable with only a first moment: use Markov.</li>
<li>A two-sided deviation from a mean with known variance: use Chebyshev.</li>
</ul>
<p>For example, an i.i.d. sample mean has $\operatorname{Var}(\bar X_n)=\sigma^2/n$, so</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(|\bar X_n-\mu|\ge\varepsilon)
\le\frac{\sigma^2}{n\varepsilon^2},
$$</div>
<p>which directly proves the weak law of large numbers. With an MGF or bounded variables, Chernoff/Hoeffding usually gives a sharper exponential tail.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> Jensen's direction depends on convexity versus concavity; Markov requires a nonnegative variable; Chebyshev requires finite variance; and these are worst-case distribution-free guarantees, so a loose numerical bound need not signal a bad derivation.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

- □ Union bound: $P(\\bigcup_i A_i)\\le\\sum_iP(A_i)$; independence is not required.
~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-laws-large-numbers">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Weak and strong laws of large numbers: sample-average convergence and assumptions</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. What do the two laws say?</h4>
<p>Let $X_1,X_2,\ldots$ have common mean $\mu$ and let $\bar X_n=n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^nX_i$. A law of large numbers does not say that a finite-sample average equals the population mean. It says that substantial deviations become negligible as the sample grows.</p>
<p>The <strong>weak law (WLLN)</strong> gives convergence in probability:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\bar X_n\xrightarrow{P}\mu
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\forall\varepsilon&gt;0,\quad
P(|\bar X_n-\mu|&gt;\varepsilon)\to0.
$$</div>
<p>It examines each large $n$: a noticeable error is unlikely. It does not directly make a pathwise statement about the entire infinite sequence.</p>
<p>The <strong>strong law (SLLN)</strong> gives almost-sure convergence:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P\!\left(\lim_{n\to\infty}\bar X_n=\mu\right)=1.
$$</div>
<p>Outside one exceptional set of sample paths having probability zero, every remaining path eventually approaches and stays near $\mu$. Almost-sure convergence implies convergence in probability, so the strong law is generally the stronger conclusion; the converse need not hold.</p>
<h4>2. Why does averaging stabilize? A Chebyshev proof</h4>
<p>If the $X_i$ are i.i.d. with $\mathbb E[X_i]=\mu$ and $\operatorname{Var}(X_i)=\sigma^2&lt;\infty$, independence makes variances add:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[\bar X_n]=\mu,
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(\bar X_n)=\frac{\sigma^2}{n}.
$$</div>
<p>Chebyshev's inequality then gives</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(|\bar X_n-\mu|&gt;\varepsilon)
\le \frac{\sigma^2}{n\varepsilon^2}\to0,
$$</div>
<p>which proves a standard WLLN. Intuitively, independent noise partly cancels: a sum's standard deviation grows like $\sqrt n$, but averaging divides by $n$, so typical fluctuations of the mean shrink like $1/\sqrt n$.</p>
<h4>3. Standard conditions and an example</h4>
<p>For i.i.d. observations, finite variance is sufficient for the short WLLN proof above but is not the weakest possible assumption. A classical SLLN requires only $\mathbb E|X_1|&lt;\infty$. If $X_i\sim\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p)$, then $\bar X_n$ is the observed success frequency, and the laws say that this long-run frequency converges to $p$; they do not make every short run look representative.</p>
<h4>4. Do not confuse the LLN with the CLT</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>LLN:</strong> identifies the limit of $\bar X_n$ and underlies consistency.</li>
<li><strong>CLT:</strong> rescales the error by $\sqrt n$ and describes its approximate distribution, enabling standard errors and confidence intervals.</li>
<li>An LLN alone usually gives neither a useful finite-sample error probability nor a universal threshold for when $n$ is “large.”</li>
</ul>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> a Cauchy variable has no finite population mean, so its sample mean does not stabilize in the usual way; strong dependence, structural breaks, or nonstationarity may also invalidate a naive law. “Almost surely” does not mean “for every conceivable path,” and unusual finite streaks do not violate an asymptotic theorem.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~
- □ Central limit theorem; standard error scales as $1\\mathord/\\sqrt n$; know when dependence\/heavy tails break naive use.
- □ Normal, Poisson and continuity-corrected approximations to binomial probabilities.
- □ Delta method, Slutsky's theorem and continuous mapping theorem at an interview-level intuition.
- □ Chernoff\/Hoeffding concentration bounds \[P2\].

=== 1.7 Stochastic processes \[P1\/P2\]

- □ Discrete-time Markov chains: transition matrix, n-step transitions, communicating classes, recurrence, absorption and stationary distribution.
- □ First-step analysis for hitting probabilities and expected hitting times.

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-random-walk">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Random Walk</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Basic model</h4>
<p>A one-dimensional simple random walk is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
S_n=S_0+\sum_{t=1}^n X_t,\qquad
P(X_t=1)=p,\qquad P(X_t=-1)=q=1-p,
$$</div>
<p>with i.i.d. increments. Given the current state, the next step does not depend on the earlier path, so $(S_n)$ is a Markov chain. If $S_0=i$, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[S_n]=i+n(p-q),\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(S_n)=4npq.
$$</div>
<p>When $p=q=1/2$, the walk has no drift and $(S_n)$ is a martingale. When $p\ne q$, the centered process $S_n-n(p-q)$ is a martingale.</p>
<h4>2. The key interview tool: first-step analysis</h4>
<p>Do not begin by enumerating entire paths. If $h_i$ is the probability of eventually hitting a target when starting at $i$, condition on the first step:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
h_i=p\,h_{i+1}+q\,h_{i-1}.
$$</div>
<p>If $e_i$ is the expected number of steps until the target, the current step must be counted:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
e_i=1+p\,e_{i+1}+q\,e_{i-1}.
$$</div>
<p>Absorbing boundary conditions turn each path problem into a second-order difference equation. The hitting-probability equation is homogeneous; the expected-time equation contains the extra 1, which is easy to miss.</p>
<h4>3. Distribution and long-run behavior</h4>
<p>For $S_0=0$, reaching $j$ after $n$ steps requires $(n+j)/2$ upward steps, so the probability is nonzero only when $n$ and $j$ have the same parity:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(S_n=j)=\binom{n}{(n+j)/2}p^{(n+j)/2}q^{(n-j)/2}.
$$</div>
<p>On the infinite line, a one-dimensional fair walk returns to zero with probability 1, yet its expected return time is infinite. A walk with nonzero drift is transient in the drift direction. Thus an almost-sure event need not have a finite expected waiting time.</p>
<h4>4. Problem-solving map</h4>
<ol>
<li>Specify the state space, increment law, starting point, boundaries, and stopping rule.</li>
<li>Use binomial counting for a fixed-time location; use first-step recurrences for which boundary is hit first.</li>
<li>Use reflection for path crossings or maxima, and martingales or optional stopping for stopped probabilities and expectations.</li>
<li>Check parity, boundary values, and that probabilities lie in $[0,1]$.</li>
</ol>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> $S_n=a$ does not say that time $n$ is the first visit to $a$; on an infinite state space, hitting with probability 1 does not imply a finite mean hitting time.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-gamblers-ruin">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Gambler's Ruin</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Model and winning probability</h4>
<p>Capital moves on $\{0,1,\ldots,N\}$ from $i$. Each round wins one unit with probability $p$ and loses one with probability $q=1-p$; play stops at 0 or $N$. Define</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
u_i=P_i(\tau_N&lt;\tau_0).
$$</div>
<p>First-step analysis gives $u_i=p\,u_{i+1}+q\,u_{i-1}$ with $u_0=0,u_N=1$. Solving the difference equation yields</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
u_i=
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{i}{N}, &amp; p=q=\dfrac12,\\[6pt]
\dfrac{1-(q/p)^i}{1-(q/p)^N}, &amp; p\ne q.
\end{cases}
$$</div>
<h4>2. Why is the fair-game answer $i/N$?</h4>
<p>For $p=q=1/2$, the recurrence says $u_{i+1}-u_i=u_i-u_{i-1}$, so adjacent differences are constant and $u_i$ is linear. The line through $(0,0)$ and $(N,1)$ is $u_i=i/N$. Alternatively, optional stopping at the bounded absorbing time gives</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
i=\mathbb E_i[S_\tau]=0\cdot(1-u_i)+Nu_i.
$$</div>
<h4>3. Expected duration</h4>
<p>Let $e_i=\mathbb E_i[\tau_0\wedge\tau_N]$. It satisfies $e_0=e_N=0$ and $e_i=1+p e_{i+1}+q e_{i-1}$, giving</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
e_i=
\begin{cases}
i(N-i), &amp; p=q=\dfrac12,\\[6pt]
\dfrac{i}{q-p}-\dfrac{N}{q-p}
\dfrac{1-(q/p)^i}{1-(q/p)^N}, &amp; p\ne q.
\end{cases}
$$</div>
<h4>4. Small example</h4>
<p>In a fair game starting from $i=3$, with ruin at 0 and target $N=10$, the probability of reaching 10 first is $3/10$ and the expected duration is $3(10-3)=21$ rounds. Winning probability and game duration are separate quantities.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> the biased case does not use $i/N$; non-unit payoffs can overshoot a boundary and change the recurrence; optional stopping requires its integrability or boundedness conditions.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-reflection-principle">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Reflection Principle</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Core idea</h4>
<p>The reflection principle turns a historical path constraint—“the path crossed a boundary”—into an unconstrained endpoint condition. Let a fair simple random walk start at 0 and let $M_n=\max_{0\le t\le n}S_t$. Fix an integer $a&gt;0$ and endpoint $b&lt;a$, and consider paths satisfying $M_n\ge a,S_n=b$.</p>
<p>For each path, find its first hitting time $T$ of $a$. Keep the path through $T$ and reverse every later up/down step, equivalently setting $S'_t=2a-S_t$ for $t\ge T$. The new endpoint is $2a-b&gt;a$. Reflection is an involution, hence a bijection:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(M_n\ge a,\,S_n=b)=P(S_n=2a-b),\qquad b&lt;a.
$$</div>
<h4>2. Distribution of the maximum</h4>
<p>Sum over endpoints $b&lt;a$ and include paths already ending at $S_n\ge a$:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(M_n\ge a)=P(S_n\ge a)+P(S_n&gt;a).
$$</div>
<p>The two discrete tails differ because of lattice parity. Brownian motion has a continuous endpoint, so the correction disappears and the familiar formula is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P\!\left(\sup_{0\le s\le t}B_s\ge a\right)=2P(B_t\ge a)
=2\left[1-\Phi\!\left(\frac{a}{\sqrt t}\right)\right].
$$</div>
<h4>3. Why reflect after the first hit?</h4>
<p>At the first hitting time the path is exactly at $a$, so the reflected suffix joins continuously. Every crossing path also has a unique cutting point, which makes the map reversible. Reflecting after an arbitrary crossing need not be one-to-one and can overcount or omit paths.</p>
<h4>4. Common applications</h4>
<ul>
<li>random-walk maxima, boundary-crossing probabilities, and first-passage distributions;</li>
<li>nonnegative lattice paths, the ballot theorem, and Catalan-number counts;</li>
<li>Brownian barrier probabilities and the intuition behind barrier options.</li>
</ul>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Check:</strong> symmetry of the increments is essential for equal path weights. With drift, the reflected path usually has a different probability weight. In discrete time, also enforce endpoint and time parity.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-first-passage-time">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ First-Passage Time</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Definition</h4>
<p>The first time a process $(X_t)$ enters a set $A$ is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\tau_A=\inf\{t\ge0:X_t\in A\}.
$$</div>
<p>For a random walk, write $\tau_a=\inf\{n\ge0:S_n=a\}$. A first return to the starting state $i$ must exclude time zero: $\tau_i^+=\inf\{n\ge1:S_n=i\}$. A hitting probability, the distribution of the hitting time, and its expectation are three distinct questions.</p>
<h4>2. Fair random walk hitting a positive integer $a$</h4>
<p>Let $S_0=0$ and let each increment be $\pm1$ with equal probability. A ballot/reflection argument gives, for $m\ge a$ with $m$ and $a$ of the same parity,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(\tau_a=m)=\frac{a}{m}P(S_m=a)
=\frac{a}{m}\binom{m}{(m+a)/2}2^{-m};
$$</div>
<p>the probability is zero otherwise. Because $\{\tau_a\le n\}=\{M_n\ge a\}$, one may also obtain the CDF from reflection and difference it to get the PMF.</p>
<h4>3. A subtle but important fact</h4>
<p>A one-dimensional fair random walk eventually reaches every fixed integer $a$ with probability 1, but</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(\tau_a&lt;\infty)=1,\qquad \mathbb E[\tau_a]=\infty.
$$</div>
<p>The first-passage distribution has a heavy tail, so “almost surely finite” does not imply “finite expectation.” Confining the walk to $[0,N]$ with absorbing boundaries makes the expected duration finite; in the fair case it is $i(N-i)$.</p>
<h4>4. Continuous-time analogue</h4>
<p>For standard Brownian motion starting at 0 and $a&gt;0$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(\tau_a\le t)=2\left[1-\Phi\!\left(\frac{a}{\sqrt t}\right)\right],
\qquad
f_{\tau_a}(t)=\frac{a}{\sqrt{2\pi t^3}}
\exp\!\left(-\frac{a^2}{2t}\right),\qquad t&gt;0.
$$</div>
<h4>5. Standard methods</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>First-step analysis:</strong> write a recurrence or boundary-value problem for hitting probabilities or expected times.</li>
<li><strong>Reflection:</strong> turn “hit by time $n$” into a maximum or endpoint-tail event.</li>
<li><strong>Tail sums:</strong> for a nonnegative integer time, $\mathbb E\tau=\sum_{n\ge0}P(\tau&gt;n)$.</li>
<li><strong>Generating functions or martingales:</strong> obtain a full law, Laplace transform, or handle more complicated boundaries.</li>
</ul>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> $P(S_n=a)$ is not $P(\tau_a=n)$ because it includes paths that arrived earlier and returned; check optional-stopping integrability or boundedness before using it to derive an expectation.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-poisson-process">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Poisson process: independent stationary increments, interarrival times, thinning, and superposition</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Definition and count distribution</h4>
<p>$N(t)$ counts events observed by time $t$. A homogeneous Poisson process with rate $\lambda&gt;0$ has $N(0)=0$ and:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>independent increments:</strong> counts in disjoint time intervals are independent;</li>
<li><strong>stationary increments:</strong> an interval's count depends on its length, not its starting time;</li>
<li>as $h\downarrow0$, one arrival has probability $\lambda h+o(h)$ and two or more have probability $o(h)$.</li>
</ul>
<p>Consequently, for $s,t\ge0$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
N(t+s)-N(t)\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda s),
\qquad
\mathbb E[N(t)]=\operatorname{Var}(N(t))=\lambda t.
$$</div>
<p>$\lambda$ is a rate per unit time; $\lambda t$ is the Poisson parameter over an interval of length $t$.</p>
<h4>2. Interarrival times and the $k$th arrival</h4>
<p>Let $T_k$ be the time of arrival $k$ and $W_k=T_k-T_{k-1}$ the interarrival time. Since</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(W_1&gt;t)=P(N(t)=0)=e^{-\lambda t},
$$</div>
<p>the $W_k$ are i.i.d. $\operatorname{Exponential}(\lambda)$. Hence, under the shape–rate convention,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
T_k=\sum_{i=1}^kW_i\sim\operatorname{Gamma}(k,\lambda),
\qquad
\{T_k\le t\}=\{N(t)\ge k\}.
$$</div>
<p>This equivalence converts questions about waiting for arrival $k$ into fixed-horizon count questions and vice versa.</p>
<h4>3. Thinning</h4>
<p>Independently label every arrival A with probability $p$ and B otherwise. The two labeled processes are themselves independent Poisson processes:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
N_A(t)\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(p\lambda t),
\qquad
N_B(t)\sim\operatorname{Poisson}((1-p)\lambda t).
$$</div>
<p>For example, orders arrive at rate 5 per minute and are independently buys with probability 0.4; buy and sell rates are then 2 and 3. If labels depend on congestion or on one another, ordinary thinning need not apply.</p>
<h4>4. Superposition</h4>
<p>If $N_1,\ldots,N_m$ are independent Poisson processes with rates $\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_m$, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
N(t)=\sum_{j=1}^mN_j(t)
$$</div>
<p>is Poisson with rate $\sum_j\lambda_j$. Given that a combined arrival occurs, its source is process $j$ with probability $\lambda_j/\sum_ell\lambda_ell$. Independence is essential.</p>
<h4>5. Arrival locations conditional on the total</h4>
<p>Conditional on $N(t)=n$, the $n$ arrival times in $(0,t)$ have the same joint distribution as the order statistics of $n$ i.i.d. Uniform$(0,t)$ variables. This turns questions such as “given $n$ arrivals, when was the last one?” into uniform-order-statistic problems.</p>
<h4>6. Nonhomogeneous extension and checks</h4>
<p>If the instantaneous rate is $\lambda(u)$, define the cumulative intensity $\Lambda(t)=\int_0^t\lambda(u)\,du$. A nonhomogeneous Poisson process has $N(t)\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\Lambda(t))$, although increments are generally no longer stationary.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> a Poisson distribution is a fixed-interval count, whereas a Poisson process is an entire time-indexed model; do not confuse rate with interval mean. A statement such as “two events per minute on average” alone does not establish a Poisson process. Superposition needs independent component processes, and thinning needs independent event-by-event labels.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-martingales-optional-stopping">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Martingales, Stopping Times, and Conditions for Optional Stopping</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Filtrations and martingales</h4>
<p>A filtration $(\mathcal F_n)_{n\ge0}$ represents accumulating information: $\mathcal F_n$ contains everything known by time $n$ and $\mathcal F_n\subseteq\mathcal F_{n+1}$. A process $(M_n)$ is a martingale with respect to this filtration when:</p>
<ol>
<li>$M_n$ is $\mathcal F_n$-measurable, so it is determined by current information (adapted);</li>
<li>$\mathbb E|M_n|&lt;\infty$;</li>
<li>the fair-game condition holds:</li>
</ol>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[M_{n+1}\mid\mathcal F_n]=M_n.
$$</div>
<p>Replacing equality by $\ge$ gives a submartingale and by $\le$ a supermartingale. A fair random walk $S_n$ is a martingale. If an increment has mean $\mu$, the centered process $S_n-n\mu$ is a martingale.</p>
<h4>2. What is a stopping time?</h4>
<p>A random time $T$ is a stopping time exactly when for every $n$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\{T\le n\}\in\mathcal F_n.
$$</div>
<p>By time $n$, current information must be enough to decide whether stopping has already occurred; the rule may not look into the future. The first hitting time of a set is a stopping time. “The last time the process visits 0” usually is not, because one must inspect the future to know that a visit is the last.</p>
<p>The stopped process</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
M_{n\wedge T}
$$</div>
<p>follows the original process until $T$ and remains frozen at $M_T$ afterward. Under the basic integrability conditions, a stopped martingale remains a martingale.</p>
<h4>3. Optional sampling for bounded stopping times</h4>
<p>If $S\le T$ are bounded stopping times and $(M_n)$ is a martingale, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[M_T\mid\mathcal F_S]=M_S.
$$</div>
<p>In particular, taking $S=0$ gives</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[M_T]=\mathbb E[M_0].
$$</div>
<p>The intuition is that one may choose when to stop using past information, but this does not change the expectation of a fair game. The key requirements are both non-anticipation and sufficient boundedness or integrability.</p>
<h4>4. Common sufficient conditions for an unbounded stopping time</h4>
<p>For a possibly unbounded $T$, one cannot simply replace a deterministic $n$ by $T$. The following are common, distinct <strong>sufficient conditions</strong>; one such set is typically enough for $\mathbb E[M_T]=\mathbb E[M_0]$:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>$T$ is bounded:</strong> a deterministic $N$ satisfies $T\le N$;</li>
<li><strong>finite expected time and bounded increments:</strong> $\mathbb E[T]&lt;\infty$ and $|M_{n+1}-M_n|\le C$ for some constant $C$;</li>
<li><strong>uniform integrability of the stopped family:</strong> $\{M_{n\wedge T}:n\ge0\}$ is uniformly integrable, for example if an integrable $Y$ dominates $|M_{n\wedge T}|$ for every $n$.</li>
</ul>
<p>The third condition justifies interchanging the limit $n\to\infty$ and expectation in</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[M_{n\wedge T}]=\mathbb E[M_0].
$$</div>
<p>These are practical sufficient conditions, not an exhaustive list of all theorem variants or necessary conditions.</p>
<h4>5. Why almost-sure stopping is still insufficient</h4>
<p>Let $S_n$ be a fair simple random walk starting at 0 and let $T=\inf\{n:S_n=1\}$. A one-dimensional fair walk eventually hits 1, so $T&lt;\infty$ almost surely and</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
S_T=1.
$$</div>
<p>Blindly applying optional stopping would incorrectly give $\mathbb E[S_T]=\mathbb E[S_0]=0$. In fact $\mathbb E[T]=\infty$, and $\{S_{n\wedge T}\}$ is not uniformly integrable. Almost-sure finiteness does not replace the theorem's tail and integrability conditions.</p>
<h4>6. Valid application: fair gambler's ruin</h4>
<p>A fair random walk starts at $i\in\{1,\ldots,N-1\}$ and stops at 0 or $N$. Let $T=\tau_0\wedge\tau_N$. The stopped process always remains in $[0,N]$, so it is uniformly integrable and optional stopping is valid:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
i=\mathbb E_i[S_T]
=0\cdot P_i(\tau_0&lt;\tau_N)
+N\cdot P_i(\tau_N&lt;\tau_0).
$$</div>
<p>Hence $P_i(\tau_N&lt;\tau_0)=i/N$. The bounded stopped value is established before invoking the theorem; the phrase “fair game” alone is not a proof of its conditions.</p>
<h4>7. Interview checklist</h4>
<ol>
<li>Specify the filtration and verify adaptedness, integrability, and the conditional-expectation identity.</li>
<li>Verify that $T$ is truly a stopping time rather than a last time defined using the future.</li>
<li>First apply the theorem to $T\wedge n$, then justify taking $n\to\infty$.</li>
<li>Name the sufficient condition used: bounded time, finite mean plus bounded increments, or uniform integrability.</li>
<li>Check for overshoot, infinite expected stopping time, and unbounded stakes.</li>
</ol>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> $T&lt;\infty$ a.s. is not enough; a doubling strategy uses growing stakes and typically violates lower-bound, integrability, or uniform-integrability assumptions; a process with drift is not a martingale until it is centered or replaced by a correct exponential martingale; and optional stopping is not a loophole for creating positive expected profit.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-brownian-motion">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Brownian motion, quadratic variation, and Itô intuition [P2, mainly derivatives/systematic macro roles]</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Definition of standard Brownian motion</h4>
<p>Standard Brownian motion, also called a Wiener process, is a continuous-time stochastic process $(B_t)_{t\ge0}$ satisfying:</p>
<ol>
<li>$B_0=0$;</li>
<li><strong>independent increments:</strong> increments over disjoint time intervals are independent;</li>
<li><strong>stationary Gaussian increments:</strong> for $0\le s&lt;t$,</li>
</ol>
<div class="math-display">$$
B_t-B_s\sim N(0,t-s);
$$</div>
<ol start="4">
<li>sample paths are almost surely continuous.</li>
</ol>
<p>Consequently, $B_t\sim N(0,t)$ and</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[B_t]=0,
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(B_t)=t,
\qquad
\operatorname{Cov}(B_s,B_t)=\min(s,t).
$$</div>
<p>For $s&lt;t$, write $B_t=B_s+(B_t-B_s)$. The new increment is independent of $B_s$, leaving covariance $\operatorname{Var}(B_s)=s$. Notice that $B_s$ and $B_t$ themselves are not independent; the future increment $B_t-B_s$ is independent of the past.</p>
<h4>2. Intuition from a random-walk limit</h4>
<p>A fair random walk converges toward Brownian motion when time steps shrink and space is scaled by the square root of the step count. Over a short interval,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\Delta B\sim N(0,\Delta t),
\qquad
|\Delta B|\text{ is typically of order }\sqrt{\Delta t}.
$$</div>
<p>This also gives the self-similarity property</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
(B_{ct})_{t\ge0}\overset d=(\sqrt c\,B_t)_{t\ge0}.
$$</div>
<p>Brownian motion is Markov and is a martingale: with information through time $s$, $\mathbb E[B_t\mid\mathcal F_s]=B_s$. Its paths are continuous but almost surely nowhere differentiable, because the typical slope $|\Delta B|/\Delta t$ grows like $1/\sqrt{\Delta t}$ as the interval shrinks.</p>
<h4>3. Why does quadratic variation equal time?</h4>
<p>Partition $[0,t]$ into fine intervals. Brownian motion has infinite total variation, but its sum of squared increments converges:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
[B]_t
=\lim_{\lVert\Pi\rVert\to0}
\sum_i(B_{t_{i+1}}-B_{t_i})^2
=t.
$$</div>
<p>Each squared increment has expectation $\Delta t_i$, so their expectations sum to $t$. Stochastic-differential notation summarizes the relevant orders as</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
dB_t=O(\sqrt{dt}),
\qquad
(dB_t)^2=dt,
\qquad
dB_t\,dt=0,
\qquad
(dt)^2=0.
$$</div>
<p>These are bookkeeping rules through order $dt$, not ordinary algebraic identities between infinitesimals. Because $(dB_t)^2$ has the same order as $dt$, stochastic calculus differs from ordinary calculus.</p>
<h4>4. Itô's formula: why is there a second-order term?</h4>
<p>Taylor-expand a sufficiently smooth $f(t,B_t)$. Since $dB_t$ is of order $\sqrt{dt}$, its square cannot be discarded. Itô's formula is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
df(t,B_t)
=f_t(t,B_t)\,dt
+f_x(t,B_t)\,dB_t
+\frac12f_{xx}(t,B_t)\,dt.
$$</div>
<p>The key example is $f(x)=x^2$:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
d(B_t^2)=2B_t\,dB_t+dt,
\qquad
B_t^2=2\int_0^tB_s\,dB_s+t.
$$</div>
<p>The Itô integral has mean zero, so $\mathbb E[B_t^2]=t$. Applying the ordinary chain rule and omitting $dt$ would incorrectly imply zero second moment.</p>
<h4>5. Drift, volatility, and geometric Brownian motion</h4>
<p>The process $X_t=x_0+\mu t+\sigma B_t$ satisfies</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
dX_t=\mu\,dt+\sigma\,dB_t,
\qquad
X_t-X_s\sim N(\mu(t-s),\sigma^2(t-s)).
$$</div>
<p>A common financial model is geometric Brownian motion, $dS_t=\mu S_tdt+\sigma S_tdB_t$. Applying Itô's formula to $\log S_t$ gives</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
S_t=S_0\exp\!\left[\left(\mu-\frac12\sigma^2\right)t+\sigma B_t\right].
$$</div>
<p>The $-\sigma^2/2$ in the exponent is the Itô correction and ensures $\mathbb E[S_t]=S_0e^{\mu t}$.</p>
<h4>6. Maximum, reflection, and first passage</h4>
<p>Let $M_t=\sup_{0\le s\le t}B_s$ and $a&gt;0$. The reflection principle gives</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(M_t\ge a)=2P(B_t\ge a)
=2\left[1-\Phi\!\left(\frac a{\sqrt t}\right)\right].
$$</div>
<p>Thus the first-passage time $\tau_a=\inf\{t\ge0:B_t=a\}$ obeys $P(\tau_a\le t)=P(M_t\ge a)$. It is almost surely finite, but $\mathbb E[\tau_a]=\infty$: eventual arrival does not imply finite average waiting time.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> continuous paths need not be differentiable; $B_t$ is not stationary because its variance grows with time—its increments are stationary; $dB_t/dt$ is not an ordinary random variable; $(dB_t)^2=dt$ is a quadratic-variation rule; and writing an asset model with Brownian motion does not itself validate constant drift, volatility, no jumps, or market stationarity.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

== 2. Statistics

=== 2.1 Samples, estimators and sampling distributions \[P0\]

- □ Population versus sample; statistic versus parameter; i.i.d. assumptions.
- □ Sample mean, unbiased sample variance, covariance and correlation.
- □ Bias, variance, MSE = variance \+ bias², consistency, efficiency and sufficiency.
- □ Standard error is the standard deviation of an estimator, not the sample standard deviation.
- □ Bootstrap: resample observations\/blocks and know when dependence requires block bootstrap.
- □ Jackknife and permutation\/randomization tests at a conceptual level.
- □ Robust summaries: median, MAD, trimmed\/winsorized mean; sensitivity to outliers.

=== 2.2 Estimation: MLE, MAP and Bayesian updating \[P0\]

- □ Write likelihood and log-likelihood; differentiate; verify boundary and second-order conditions.
- □ MLE invariance, asymptotic normality and Fisher information.
- □ Method of moments and comparison with MLE.
- □ MAP = likelihood plus log-prior; distinguish MAP from posterior mean.
~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-conjugate-priors">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Beta–Binomial, Gamma–Poisson, and Normal–Normal conjugacy</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. What does conjugacy mean?</h4>
<p>A prior is <strong>conjugate</strong> to a likelihood when multiplying them produces a posterior in the same distribution family as the prior. Updating then reduces to changing a few hyperparameters instead of recomputing a numerical integral. The starting point is always</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
p(\theta\mid D)\propto p(D\mid\theta)p(\theta).
$$</div>
<h4>2. Beta–Binomial: updating probability by pseudo-counts</h4>
<p>Let $p\sim\operatorname{Beta}(\alpha,\beta)$ and observe $x$ successes in $n$ Bernoulli trials. Then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
p\mid x\sim\operatorname{Beta}(\alpha+x,\,\beta+n-x).
$$</div>
<p>$\alpha$ and $\beta$ can be viewed as prior success and failure pseudo-counts. The posterior mean equals the posterior predictive probability of success on the next trial:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[p\mid x]
=P(X_{n+1}=1\mid x)
=\frac{\alpha+x}{\alpha+\beta+n}.
$$</div>
<p>Integrating $p$ out of the success count in $m$ future trials yields a Beta–Binomial distribution, which incorporates parameter uncertainty beyond an ordinary Binomial with fixed $p$.</p>
<h4>3. Gamma–Poisson: updating an event rate</h4>
<p>Use the shape–rate convention: $\lambda\sim\operatorname{Gamma}(a,b)$ has density proportional to $\lambda^{a-1}e^{-b\lambda}$. If observation $i$ has exposure $t_i$ and $X_i\mid\lambda\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda t_i)$, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\lambda\mid x_1,\ldots,x_n
\sim\operatorname{Gamma}\!\left(a+\sum_i x_i,\ b+\sum_i t_i\right).
$$</div>
<p>The shape accumulates events and the rate accumulates exposure. The posterior mean is $(a+\sum_i x_i)/(b+\sum_i t_i)$. Integrating $\lambda$ out of a future count gives a Negative Binomial predictive distribution; Poisson rate mixing therefore creates overdispersion.</p>
<h4>4. Normal–Normal: precision-weighted averaging</h4>
<p>Suppose the observation variance is known: $X_i\mid\mu\sim N(\mu,\sigma^2)$ and $\mu\sim N(m_0,s_0^2)$. Writing precision as inverse variance,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
s_n^2=\left(\frac1{s_0^2}+\frac n{\sigma^2}\right)^{-1},
\qquad
m_n=s_n^2\left(\frac{m_0}{s_0^2}+\frac{n\bar x}{\sigma^2}\right),
$$</div>
<p>and $\mu\mid D\sim N(m_n,s_n^2)$. The posterior mean combines prior and sample means in proportion to precision: more observations or lower noise increases the data weight. The posterior predictive distribution is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
X_{n+1}\mid D\sim N(m_n,\,\sigma^2+s_n^2),
$$</div>
<p>where the two variance terms represent new-observation noise and remaining uncertainty about $\mu$. If $\sigma^2$ is also unknown, a Normal prior alone is not fully conjugate; a common choice is Normal–Inverse-Gamma.</p>
<h4>5. Fast recognition</h4>
<ul>
<li>unknown Bernoulli/Binomial probability $p$: think Beta;</li>
<li>unknown Poisson/Exponential rate $\lambda$: think Gamma;</li>
<li>unknown Normal mean with known variance: think Normal and precision weighting.</li>
</ul>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> declare whether Gamma uses a rate or scale parameter; conjugacy is computational convenience, not evidence that the prior expresses genuine beliefs; posterior prediction integrates over the parameter rather than merely plugging in its posterior mean; and the simple Normal–Normal formula assumes known observation variance.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~
- □ L2 regularization corresponds to a Gaussian prior; L1 corresponds to a Laplace prior under the usual MAP interpretation.

=== 2.3 Confidence intervals and hypothesis tests \[P0\]

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-confidence-intervals">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ A frequentist confidence interval describes long-run coverage, not posterior probability for a fixed parameter</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Separate the parameter, estimate, and standard error</h4>
<p>A population parameter $\theta$, such as a population mean $\mu$, is fixed but unknown. Its estimator $\hat\theta$ changes across samples. The standard error describes variability of the <strong>estimator under repeated sampling</strong>, not variability of individual observations:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{SE}(\hat\theta)
=\sqrt{\operatorname{Var}(\hat\theta)}.
$$</div>
<p>Many large-sample confidence intervals have the form</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\text{estimate} \pm\ \text{critical value}\times\operatorname{SE}.
$$</div>
<p>The point estimate is at the center, while the margin of error reflects both sampling noise and the requested confidence level.</p>
<h4>2. The correct meaning of 95% confidence</h4>
<p>Suppose every new sample is used to construct an interval by the same rule $C(X)$. A 95% coverage statement means</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P_\theta\{\theta\in C(X)\}=0.95.
$$</div>
<p>The sample and interval are random; the frequentist parameter is not. Once one interval has been calculated, it either contains the true value or it does not. Strictly speaking, “there is a 95% probability that $\theta$ lies in this realized interval” is a Bayesian credible-interval interpretation, conditional on a prior and model.</p>
<h4>3. A confidence interval for a mean</h4>
<p>Let $n=25$, $\bar x=10$, and $s=2$, with unknown population variance. Under independent approximately normal observations, use $t_{24}$:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\bar x\pm t_{0.975,24}\frac{s}{\sqrt n}
=10\pm2.064\frac{2}{5}
=10\pm0.826.
$$</div>
<p>The 95% CI is approximately $[9.174,10.826]$. The associated procedure covers $\mu$ in about 95% of repeated experiments when its assumptions hold.</p>
<h4>4. What controls interval width?</h4>
<ul>
<li>Higher confidence requires a larger critical value and a wider interval.</li>
<li>More noise produces a larger SE and wider interval.</li>
<li>For independent data, SE commonly shrinks as $1/\sqrt n$; halving a margin of error requires roughly four times the sample size.</li>
<li>Dependence, clustering, serial correlation, or misspecification can invalidate naive SEs and coverage; use suitable cluster, HAC, or bootstrap methods.</li>
</ul>
<h4>5. Duality with two-sided tests</h4>
<p>When the model, SE, approximation, and direction match, a level-$\alpha$ two-sided test rejects $H_0:\theta=\theta_0$ exactly when the $(1-\alpha)$ CI excludes $\theta_0$. Thus a matching 95% CI excluding zero generally corresponds to a two-sided $p&lt;0.05$. The duality can fail when the test and interval use different variance estimates or corrections.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong>95% does not mean 95% of observations lie inside the interval; a CI is not a prediction interval; narrowness measures precision under the model, not freedom from bias; and the confidence level or method should not be changed after inspecting the result.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-hypothesis-testing-foundations">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Null/alternative, test statistic, rejection region, p-value, and significance level</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Six pieces of a hypothesis test</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Estimand:</strong>state the parameter of interest, such as $\Delta=\mu_A-\mu_B$.</li>
<li><strong>Null $H_0$:</strong>a baseline model, often $\Delta=0$.</li>
<li><strong>Alternative $H_1$:</strong>$\Delta\ne0$ for a two-sided test, or a pre-specified $\Delta&gt;0$/$\Delta&lt;0$ when a one-sided claim is justified.</li>
<li><strong>Test statistic:</strong>standardize distance from the null, for example $T=(\hat\Delta-0)/\operatorname{SE}(\hat\Delta)$.</li>
<li><strong>Null distribution and rejection region:</strong>derive the distribution of $T$ under $H_0$, choose $\alpha$ before seeing results, and reserve the most extreme $\alpha$ tail area.</li>
<li><strong>Decision:</strong>reject when the observed statistic enters the rejection region; otherwise fail to reject. Do not claim that the null has been proved.</li>
</ol>
<h4>2. What is a p-value?</h4>
<p>A p-value is the probability, <strong>assuming $H_0$ and the model are true</strong>, of observing the current statistic or one more adverse to $H_0$. For a two-sided test,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
p=P_{H_0}\bigl(|T|\ge |T_{\mathrm{obs}}|\bigr).
$$</div>
<p>It is not $P(H_0\mid\text{data})$, the probability that chance caused the result, or the probability of a zero effect. A small p-value says the data are incompatible with predictions under $H_0$; it does not directly measure effect size or research quality.</p>
<h4>3. A numerical decision</h4>
<p>If a large-sample statistic is $z=2.10$, the two-sided p-value is approximately</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
p=2\{1-\Phi(2.10)\}\approx0.036.
$$</div>
<p>Reject at $\alpha=0.05$ but not at $\alpha=0.01$. The data have not changed; the decision depends on the pre-specified false-positive tolerance. The value 0.05 is not a law-of-nature boundary between truth and falsehood.</p>
<h4>4. One-sided or two-sided?</h4>
<p>A two-sided test protects against both directions and is the usual default. A one-sided test puts all of $\alpha$ in one tail, gaining power in the chosen direction but being unable to reject for even a large effect in the opposite direction. Direction must be chosen before inspecting the data and only when the opposite direction would not support the same claim.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Safe wording:</strong>“Under $H_0$ and the model assumptions, results this extreme or more have probability p. Since p is below the pre-specified $\alpha$, we reject $H_0$.” Never say “the probability that $H_0$ is true is p” or “the result has probability $1-p$ of replicating.”</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-errors-power-sample-size">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Type I/II errors, power, and sample-size trade-offs</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Truth and the test decision are separate dimensions</h4>
<div class="table-scroll">
<table class="concept-table">
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Fail to reject $H_0$</th><th>Reject $H_0$</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th>$H_0$ is true</th><td>Correct</td><td>Type I error / false positive</td></tr>
<tr><th>$H_1$ is true</th><td>Type II error / false negative</td><td>Correct detection</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>The significance level controls</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\alpha=P(\text{reject }H_0\mid H_0\text{ true}),
$$</div>
<p>while at a specific true effect $\theta$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\beta(\theta)=P_\theta(\text{fail to reject }H_0),
\qquad
\operatorname{Power}(\theta)=1-\beta(\theta).
$$</div>
<p>Power is always defined for a particular effect size; a study does not have one context-free power value.</p>
<h4>2. What increases power?</h4>
<ul>
<li>A larger true effect $|\theta-\theta_0|$.</li>
<li>A larger sample, which reduces SE.</li>
<li>Less noise, or an efficient paired, stratified, or covariate-adjusted design.</li>
<li>A larger $\alpha$, at the cost of more Type I error.</li>
<li>A genuinely pre-specified one-sided test in the target direction, at the cost of ignoring the other direction.</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. Why sample size depends on a minimum important effect</h4>
<p>For a rough two-sided one-sample mean calculation with known $\sigma$, achieving level $\alpha$ and power $1-\beta$ at true difference $\delta$ requires</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
n\approx
\left(
\frac{(z_{1-\alpha/2}+z_{1-\beta})\sigma}{\delta}
\right)^2.
$$</div>
<p>Halving the target $\delta$ roughly quadruples required $n$. Study design should therefore define a minimum detectable or economically meaningful effect before collecting data. Real designs must also account for allocation, attrition, clustering, repeated measures, and multiple testing.</p>
<h4>4. Risks of low and high power</h4>
<p>Low power misses real effects and, when only significant results are reported, the few estimates that pass are often exaggerated. With enormous samples, negligible effects can have tiny p-values. High power therefore cannot replace effect-size and cost-benefit analysis.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong>$\alpha$ is not the fraction of reported discoveries that are false; that is related to FDR. Failure to reject does not prove no effect. Post-hoc “observed power” computed from the observed effect and p-value adds little; report the estimate and confidence interval instead.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-z-t-paired-welch">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ One/two-sample z and t tests; paired tests; Welch's unequal-variance test</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Start from the design: one sample, two independent samples, or pairs</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>One-sample:</strong>compare one sample mean with a benchmark $\mu_0$.</li>
<li><strong>Independent two-sample:</strong>groups A and B contain distinct independent units.</li>
<li><strong>Paired:</strong>before/after observations on the same unit or explicitly matched units; analyze pair differences $D_i=X_i-Y_i$.</li>
</ul>
<p>Two columns do not automatically imply independent samples. Ignoring real pairing discards useful within-pair correlation and often inflates SE; inventing pairs for independent observations creates meaningless differences.</p>
<h4>2. The core difference between z and t</h4>
<p>With known population standard deviation $\sigma$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
Z=\frac{\bar X-\mu_0}{\sigma/\sqrt n}\sim N(0,1)
\quad(H_0).
$$</div>
<p>In practice $\sigma$ is usually unknown. Replacing it with $s$ yields</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
T=\frac{\bar X-\mu_0}{s/\sqrt n}\sim t_{n-1}
$$</div>
<p>exactly for independent normal observations. The heavier t tails account for uncertainty in estimating $\sigma$; t approaches standard normal as degrees of freedom grow. At large $n$, z and t are numerically similar, but t remains the natural default for an unknown variance.</p>
<h4>3. Two independent means: Welch is usually the default</h4>
<p>Welch's statistic does not require equal variances:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
T=
\frac{\bar X_1-\bar X_2-\Delta_0}
{\sqrt{s_1^2/n_1+s_2^2/n_2}}.
$$</div>
<p>Its Welch-Satterthwaite degrees of freedom are approximated by</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\nu\approx
\frac{(s_1^2/n_1+s_2^2/n_2)^2}
{(s_1^2/n_1)^2/(n_1-1)+(s_2^2/n_2)^2/(n_2-1)}.
$$</div>
<p>The pooled t test assumes $\sigma_1^2=\sigma_2^2$ and pools variance estimates. Use it only with substantive justification. It can be badly distorted when sample sizes and variances are both unbalanced, while Welch loses little efficiency when variances are actually equal.</p>
<h4>4. How does a paired test work?</h4>
<p>Create $D_i=X_{i,\mathrm{after}}-X_{i,\mathrm{before}}$ and perform a one-sample t test on the differences:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
T=\frac{\bar D}{s_D/\sqrt n},
\qquad T\sim t_{n-1}\quad(H_0).
$$</div>
<p>Different pairs should be independent, and the difference distribution should be approximately normal for a small sample. Before and after measurements within a pair are expected to be dependent; removing stable unit-level variation is precisely why pairing can improve precision.</p>
<h4>5. Quick selection table</h4>
<div class="table-scroll">
<table class="concept-table">
<thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Common method</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>One mean, unknown $\sigma$</td><td>One-sample t</td></tr>
<tr><td>Two independent means, variances may differ</td><td>Welch two-sample t</td></tr>
<tr><td>Before/after or strict matching</td><td>Paired t = one-sample t on differences</td></tr>
<tr><td>One or two large-sample proportions</td><td>Proportion z / suitable binomial method</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Assumption check:</strong>independence across sampling units is primary. Severe skew and outliers can undermine small-sample mean tests. Do not first run a noisy equal-variance test and then choose pooled versus Welch; use Welch by default.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-chi-square-f-anova-tests">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Chi-square goodness-of-fit/independence; F tests and ANOVA assumptions</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Two chi-square tests share the observed-versus-expected idea</h4>
<p>Both use</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
X^2=\sum_{\text{cells}}
\frac{(O-E)^2}{E}.
$$</div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Goodness-of-fit:</strong>does one categorical variable follow specified probabilities $p_1,\ldots,p_k$? Here $E_j=np_j$, and df is $k-1$ if no additional parameters were estimated; usually subtract one more df per estimated parameter.</li>
<li><strong>Independence/homogeneity:</strong>are two categorical variables independent, or are category proportions the same across populations? In an $r\times c$ table, $E_{ij}=(\text{row total}_i)(\text{column total}_j)/n$, with df $(r-1)(c-1)$.</li>
</ul>
<p>A large statistic means counts deviate from their null expectations. Use counts, not percentages, as $O$. The approximation assumes independent units and sufficiently large expected counts; sparse $2\times2$ tables may require Fisher's exact test. Paired binary outcomes call for McNemar's test rather than an independence test.</p>
<h4>2. Why does an F distribution appear?</h4>
<p>An F statistic is a ratio of independent variance estimates. When each standardized variance is chi-square divided by its degrees of freedom, their ratio is F distributed. The classic two-variance test uses</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
F=\frac{s_1^2}{s_2^2},
$$</div>
<p>but is sensitive to nonnormality and outliers. In regression and ANOVA, the more important use compares a mean square explained by a model with a residual-noise mean square.</p>
<h4>3. What does one-way ANOVA test?</h4>
<p>For $g$ groups,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
H_0:\mu_1=\mu_2=\cdots=\mu_g.
$$</div>
<p>Total variation decomposes as</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
SS_{\mathrm{total}}
=SS_{\mathrm{between}}+SS_{\mathrm{within}},
$$</div>
<p>and</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
F=rac{MS_{\mathrm{between}}}{MS_{\mathrm{within}}}
=\frac{SS_{\mathrm{between}}/(g-1)}
{SS_{\mathrm{within}}/(n-g)}.
$$</div>
<p>Under $H_0$, numerator and denominator estimate the same noise variance, so the ratio should be near one. Separated group means enlarge between-group variation and F.</p>
<h4>4. ANOVA assumptions and what follows rejection</h4>
<ul>
<li>Sampling units are independent; a large sample does not repair dependence.</li>
<li>Within-group residuals are approximately normal, especially important for small samples.</li>
<li>Classical ANOVA assumes equal within-group variances; use Welch ANOVA under serious variance and sample-size imbalance.</li>
<li>Repeated measures, clusters, and factorial designs require corresponding models, not ordinary one-way ANOVA.</li>
</ul>
<p>A significant omnibus F says <strong>at least one mean differs</strong>, not which one. Follow with pre-planned contrasts or multiplicity-adjusted post-hoc comparisons such as Tukey HSD, and report mean differences, CIs, and an effect size such as $\eta^2=SS_{\mathrm{between}}/SS_{\mathrm{total}}$.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Recognition:</strong>one categorical distribution versus specified probabilities → goodness-of-fit; two categorical variables → independence; three or more independent group means → ANOVA. A significant test still requires examining relevant cells/contrasts and effect size.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-multiple-testing-control">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Multiple testing: Bonferroni, Holm, Benjamini-Hochberg; FWER versus FDR</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Why does testing everything at 0.05 fail?</h4>
<p>For $m$ independent tests with all nulls true, using $\alpha=0.05$ for each gives probability</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
1-(1-0.05)^m
$$</div>
<p>of at least one false positive. At $m=20$, this is about $64.2\%$. Tests may be dependent in practice, but trying more analyses still creates more opportunities for a small p-value. Feature mining, hyperparameter sweeps, asset/period selection, and showing only the best backtest are the same selection problem.</p>
<h4>2. FWER: control the chance of any false positive</h4>
<p>With $V$ false rejections,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{FWER}=P(V\ge1).
$$</div>
<p>This is appropriate when even one false conclusion is costly.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Bonferroni:</strong>test each hypothesis at $\alpha/m$, or use adjusted p-value $\min(mp_i,1)$. It controls FWER under arbitrary dependence but can be conservative; for 20 tests the per-test threshold is $0.0025$.</li>
<li><strong>Holm:</strong>sort $p_{(1)}\le\cdots\le p_{(m)}$, compare sequentially with $\alpha/(m-k+1)$, and stop at the first failure. Holm also controls FWER and is uniformly no worse than basic Bonferroni.</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. FDR: control the false fraction among discoveries</h4>
<p>With $R$ total rejections,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{FDR}
=\mathbb E\!\left[\frac{V}{\max(R,1)}\right].
$$</div>
<p>FDR allows some false discoveries in exchange for power and is useful for large exploratory screens. Benjamini-Hochberg at target $q$:</p>
<ol>
<li>sort $p_{(1)}\le\cdots\le p_{(m)}$;</li>
<li>find the largest $k$ with $p_{(k)}\le(k/m)q$;</li>
<li>reject the first $k$ ordered hypotheses.</li>
</ol>
<p>Original BH controls FDR under independence and certain positive dependencies; arbitrary dependence may need a more conservative method. FDR $q$ does not mean that every rejected hypothesis individually has probability $q$ of being false.</p>
<h4>4. What belongs to the analysis family?</h4>
<p>The family cannot contain only tests shown in the final report. It should reflect outcomes, subgroups, features, model specifications, and windows actually searched. Preregistration, a truly untouched test set, nested validation, and independent replication reduce data snooping by design. Adjusted p-values cannot repair leakage, selective reporting, or post-hoc hypothesis redefinition.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Choice:</strong>few confirmatory claims where any false positive is costly → FWER/Holm; large exploratory screening where a controlled fraction of false discoveries is acceptable → BH/FDR. Report the family, raw and adjusted p/q-values, effect sizes, and the selection process.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-statistical-economic-significance">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Statistical significance is not economic significance; report effect size and uncertainty</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. A p-value depends on both effect size and precision</h4>
<p>Many test statistics are approximately</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
T=\frac{\text{estimated effect}-\text{null effect}}
{\operatorname{SE}(\text{estimated effect})}.
$$</div>
<p>With enormous samples and tiny SEs, a negligible effect can be highly significant. With a small noisy sample, an economically important effect may fail to reach significance. A p-value measures standardized incompatibility with the null, not whether benefits exceed costs.</p>
<h4>2. What is an effect size?</h4>
<p>Prefer raw effects in decision units: mean-return difference, conversion-rate change, prediction-error reduction, basis points per trade, cost, or risk change. For cross-scale comparison, add standardized effects such as</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
d=\frac{\bar X_1-\bar X_2}{s_{\mathrm{pooled}}},
$$</div>
<p>or an odds ratio, correlation, $R^2$, or $\eta^2$. A standardized effect still is not automatically economic value; translate it back into actual units and decision consequences.</p>
<h4>3. Compare the CI with a decision threshold</h4>
<p>Define a minimum economically meaningful effect $\delta_*$ before analysis. Suppose annualized excess return is estimated at $0.8\%$ with 95% CI $[0.1\%,1.5\%]$:</p>
<ul>
<li>It is statistically significant if zero is the only threshold.</li>
<li>If costs and capacity require at least $1.0\%$ to deploy, the interval still includes many economically inadequate values, so evidence is inconclusive for deployment.</li>
<li>If the CI were $[-0.2\%,1.8\%]$, nonsignificance would not prove no value; the interval contains both losses and meaningful gains, indicating poor precision.</li>
</ul>
<h4>4. What uncertainty matters in quantitative research?</h4>
<ul>
<li>Effect estimate and CI, not only stars or p-values.</li>
<li>Transaction costs, turnover, capacity, drawdown, and tail risk.</li>
<li>Serial dependence, nonstationarity, regime selection, and backtest overfitting.</li>
<li>Extra uncertainty from multiple testing, model selection, and data cleaning.</li>
<li>Robustness out of sample, across market periods, and on independent data.</li>
</ul>
<h4>5. A useful reporting template</h4>
<p>“A minus B is estimated as $\hat\Delta$ (95% CI $[L,U]$, p-value $p$). The pre-specified economic threshold is $\delta_*$. After costs and risk adjustment, much/little of the interval exceeds this threshold. Current evidence therefore supports/does not yet support deployment, with the main uncertainty arising from …”</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong>nonsignificant does not prove zero effect; significant does not mean large effect or a correct model; a CI does not include measurement bias, confounding, leakage, or data snooping. Statistical inference must be interpreted together with study design and the real decision.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

=== 2.4 Linear regression \[P0\]

~~~
{}{raw}
<p>Model: $y=X\beta+\varepsilon$. If $X$ has full column rank, OLS minimizes $\lVert y-X\beta\rVert_2^2$ and solves $X^{\mathsf T}X\hat\beta=X^{\mathsf T}y$, giving</p>
~~~

~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="math-display">$$
\hat\beta=(X^{\mathsf T}X)^{-1}X^{\mathsf T}y.
$$</div>
~~~

Numerically, prefer QR\/SVD to explicitly computing an inverse.

- □ Derive OLS by gradient and by orthogonal projection.
- □ Interpret coefficients conditionally; distinguish prediction from causal interpretation.
- □ Gauss-Markov assumptions and BLUE; normal errors are needed for exact small-sample t\/F inference, not for the OLS point estimate.
- □ Residuals, fitted values, leverage, hat matrix $H=X(X^{\\mathsf T}X)^{-1}X^{\\mathsf T}$, influence and Cook's distance.
- □ R² versus adjusted R²; training R² never decreases when adding features.
- □ Heteroskedasticity: coefficients may remain unbiased under exogeneity, but classical standard errors fail; use robust\/sandwich SE where appropriate.
- □ Autocorrelation and clustered dependence; HAC\/Newey-West or clustered SE conceptually.
- □ Endogeneity\/omitted variables and why correlation does not identify causality.

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-multicollinearity">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Multicollinearity: Intuition, Diagnosis, and Remedies</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Start with the simplest example: two variables say the same thing</h4>
<p>Consider a linear regression with two features:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
y_i=\beta_0+\beta_1x_{i1}+\beta_2x_{i2}+\varepsilon_i.
$$</div>
<p>If $x_{i2}=x_{i1}$ for every observation, the prediction depends only on</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\beta_1x_{i1}+\beta_2x_{i2}
=(\beta_1+\beta_2)x_{i1}.
$$</div>
<p>The data identify $\beta_1+\beta_2$ but cannot decide how to allocate credit between the two coefficients. For any constant $c$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
(\beta_1,\beta_2)
\quad\text{and}\quad
(\beta_1+c,\beta_2-c)
$$</div>
<p>produce exactly the same fitted values and residuals. This is <strong>perfect collinearity</strong>: $X$ lacks full column rank, $X^{\mathsf T}X$ is singular, and the OLS coefficient vector is not unique.</p>
<p>A practical example is including an intercept, age, and years of experience when experience is exactly age minus 22. The columns satisfy</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
x_{\text{experience}}-x_{\text{age}}+22\cdot\mathbf1=0.
$$</div>
<p>The dummy-variable trap is the same issue: with an intercept and all category indicators, the dummy columns sum exactly to the intercept column.</p>
<h4>2. Near collinearity: the solution is unique but the loss is nearly flat</h4>
<p>The more common case is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
x_2\approx x_1,
$$</div>
<p>such as $x_2=x_1+\delta$ with very little variation in $\delta$. OLS usually has a unique solution, but the data mostly identify $\beta_1+\beta_2$ and contain little information for separating $\beta_1$ from $\beta_2$. SSE contours form a long, flat valley: moving far in a direction that raises one coefficient and lowers the other changes predictions and SSE only slightly.</p>
<p>A small sample change, deleting a few rows, or adding a control may therefore produce very different coefficients:</p>
<ul>
<li>one fit may give $\hat\beta_1=4,\hat\beta_2=-3$;</li>
<li>another may give $\hat\beta_1=-1,\hat\beta_2=2$;</li>
<li>yet both sums are near 1 and the fitted values are nearly identical.</li>
</ul>
<p>The optimizer is not malfunctioning; the data do not contain enough independent movement to separate the two effects.</p>
<h4>3. Why can coefficients be unstable while prediction remains reasonable?</h4>
<p>Under the classical homoskedastic OLS assumptions, conditional on $X$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{Var}(\hat{\boldsymbol\beta}\mid X)
=\sigma^2(X^{\mathsf T}X)^{-1}.
$$</div>
<p>If $X$ has a small singular value, $X^{\mathsf T}X$ has an even smaller eigenvalue in that direction. Inversion magnifies it, increasing coefficient variances and standard errors. Under exogeneity, multicollinearity does not by itself bias OLS; it primarily causes <strong>low precision and weak identification</strong>.</p>
<p>If new observations still satisfy $x_2\approx x_1$, the prediction is mainly</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\hat y\approx\hat\beta_0+
(\hat\beta_1+\hat\beta_2)x_1.
$$</div>
<p>Individual coefficients may fluctuate while their sum stays stable, so in-distribution prediction can remain good. But questions such as “what happens if $x_1$ changes while $x_2$ is held fixed?” or extrapolation to observations that no longer satisfy $x_2\approx x_1$ are unreliable. <strong>Good interpolation does not validate coefficient interpretation or counterfactual extrapolation.</strong></p>
<h4>4. What exactly does VIF measure?</h4>
<p>To diagnose feature $j$, regress $x_j$ on all other features and let the auxiliary regression have $R^2=R_j^2$. Define</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{VIF}_j=\frac1{1-R_j^2}.
$$</div>
<p>In a classical homoskedastic regression with an intercept,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{Var}(\hat\beta_j\mid X)
=\frac{\sigma^2}
{\sum_i(x_{ij}-\bar x_j)^2(1-R_j^2)}.
$$</div>
<p>If the other features nearly explain $x_j$, then $R_j^2$ is close to 1. Little residual variation remains to identify its own coefficient, so VIF becomes large. Relative to orthogonal features:</p>
<ul>
<li>the coefficient <strong>variance</strong> is inflated by $\operatorname{VIF}_j$;</li>
<li>the coefficient <strong>standard error</strong> is inflated by $\sqrt{\operatorname{VIF}_j}$.</li>
</ul>
<p>If $R_j^2=0.96$, then $\operatorname{VIF}_j=25$ and the standard error is multiplied by 5. A baseline standard error of 0.2 can become 1.0, sharply reducing the t-statistic.</p>
<p>VIF values above 5 or 10 are common warning heuristics, not mathematical laws. Sample size, noise, the research goal, and the theoretical necessity of a variable matter more than a mechanical cutoff.</p>
<h4>5. How should it be diagnosed systematically?</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Inspect feature definitions first:</strong> duplicate columns, unit conversions of the same measure, an intercept plus all dummies, or compositional features that sum to 1 can create perfect collinearity directly.</li>
<li><strong>Inspect correlation matrices and scatterplots:</strong> they detect strong pairwise relationships but miss multivariate relations such as $x_3\approx x_1+x_2$. Pairwise correlation alone is insufficient.</li>
<li><strong>Compute VIF:</strong> determine how well each feature is linearly explained by all the others.</li>
<li><strong>Inspect the SVD and condition number:</strong> after separating the intercept and scaling features appropriately, let $\sigma_{\max}\ge\cdots\ge\sigma_{\min}$ be the singular values of $X$.</li>
</ol>
<div class="math-display">$$
\kappa(X)=\frac{\sigma_{\max}}{\sigma_{\min}},
\qquad
\kappa(X^{\mathsf T}X)=\kappa(X)^2.
$$</div>
<p>$\sigma_{\min}=0$ means perfect collinearity; a very small $\sigma_{\min}$ means some feature combination barely varies. A condition index is often written $\sigma_{\max}/\sigma_i$. A value near 30 is sometimes treated as a severe-warning heuristic, but it is not universal. Without standardization, a condition number can mix unit-scale problems with substantive collinearity.</p>
<ol start="5">
<li><strong>Check coefficient stability:</strong> alter the training sample, bootstrap, add variables sequentially, or inspect a regularization path. Stable predictions accompanied by violently offsetting changes among correlated coefficients are a classic signal.</li>
</ol>
<p>Surface symptoms include a high overall $R^2$ or significant F-test but insignificant individual t-tests, counterintuitive coefficient signs, wide confidence intervals, and abrupt sign changes after a correlated feature is added or removed.</p>
<h4>6. Remedies depend on the actual goal</h4>
<p><strong>A. Obvious encoding redundancy</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Remove true duplicate columns; keep a reference category for categorical variables; for compositional data, omit one component or use appropriate log-ratio coordinates.</li>
<li>Use domain knowledge to combine variables measuring the same construct into an interpretable composite score.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>B. Interpreting one coefficient or conducting inference</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Do not delete a theoretically required confounder merely to lower VIF. That can trade a smaller standard error for omitted-variable bias.</li>
<li>Reparameterize into combinations the data can identify. With strongly correlated $x_1,x_2$, a total and a difference may be more meaningful than forcing separate original coefficients.</li>
<li>Report confidence intervals, joint F-tests, or identifiable linear combinations rather than only individual p-values.</li>
<li>Collect data that make the correlated variables vary independently. Merely adding more observations with the same dependence structure may help little.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>C. Out-of-sample prediction</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Use Ridge on centered and standardized features, treating the intercept separately and usually leaving it unpenalized:</li>
</ul>
<div class="math-display">$$
\hat\beta_{\mathrm{ridge}}
=(X^{\mathsf T}X+\lambda I)^{-1}X^{\mathsf T}y.
$$</div>
<p>It lifts small-eigenvalue directions by $\lambda$, trading a little bias for a large variance reduction. Select $\lambda$ through leakage-safe validation.</p>
<ul>
<li>PCR/PCA or SVD can discard low-variance, weakly identified directions. However, principal components maximize variance in $X$, not predictive relevance for $y$, and interpretation becomes harder.</li>
<li>Elastic Net often behaves better than pure Lasso for groups of correlated features. Lasso may arbitrarily select one of several nearly equivalent variables, with the selected feature changing across samples.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>D. Causal effects</strong></p>
<p>Regularization can stabilize predictions but cannot create causal identification. If a treatment and control nearly always move together, one needs an experiment, natural experiment, instrument, or another credible source of independent variation rather than merely a more stable algorithm.</p>
<h4>7. Do centering, standardization, or interaction terms solve it?</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Standardization</strong> improves numerical scaling and makes penalties and condition numbers more comparable, but does not remove true linear dependence.</li>
<li><strong>Centering</strong> can reduce nonessential collinearity in models with $x,x^2$, or interactions caused by a nonzero mean, and improves interpretation of intercepts and main effects. It does not create new identifying information.</li>
<li><strong>Adding interactions</strong> is not a general remedy. Interactions can be even more correlated with their components; include them only when theoretically motivated and usually center continuous inputs first.</li>
</ul>
<h4>8. A one-minute interview answer</h4>
<ol>
<li>Definition: one column of $X$ is exactly or approximately a linear combination of other columns.</li>
<li>Consequence: perfect collinearity makes OLS nonunique; near collinearity inflates coefficient variance and destabilizes signs, although in-distribution prediction may remain acceptable.</li>
<li>Diagnosis: inspect feature definitions, correlation structure, VIF, singular values/condition number, and resampling stability.</li>
<li>Remedy: remove genuine encoding redundancy; protect necessary controls and report uncertainty for inference; use validated Ridge/Elastic Net/PCA for prediction; and ideally collect data with more independent variation.</li>
</ol>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> multicollinearity is not heteroskedasticity, endogeneity, or autocorrelation; it does not automatically bias OLS; high VIF does not automatically require variable deletion; higher training $R^2$ does not validate individual coefficients; regularization stabilizes predictions and numerical coefficients, not causal interpretation; and a model stable on the training-data manifold may fail badly when extrapolating beyond that correlation structure.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

=== 2.5 Regularization and model selection \[P0\]

- □ Ridge: minimize $\\mathrm{SSE}\\mathplus{}\\lambda\\lVert\\beta\\rVert_2^2$; shrinks correlated coefficients, rarely exactly to zero.
- □ Lasso: minimize $\\mathrm{SSE}\\mathplus{}\\lambda\\lVert\\beta\\rVert_1$; creates sparsity but is unstable among strongly correlated predictors.
- □ Elastic net combines L1\/L2; useful for groups of correlated features.
- □ Standardize features before scale-sensitive penalties; usually do not penalize the intercept.
- □ Choose λ using validation within a leakage-safe pipeline.
- □ AIC\/BIC, likelihood penalties and cross-validation; know their different goals and assumptions.

=== 2.6 Time series and financial data \[P0\/P1\]

- □ Simple return $r_t=\\frac{P_t}{P_{t-1}}-1$; log return $g_t=\\log P_t-\\log P_{t-1}$; log returns add across time.
- □ Arithmetic mean return, geometric growth and volatility drag.
- □ Stationarity, trend, seasonality, structural breaks and regime change.
- □ Autocovariance, ACF\/PACF; white noise versus martingale difference.
- □ AR, MA, ARMA\/ARIMA: assumptions, stationarity\/invertibility and forecasting intuition.
- □ Unit roots, spurious regression, differencing and cointegration \[P1\].
- □ Volatility clustering, ARCH\/GARCH intuition \[P1\].
- □ Cross-sectional versus time-series dependence; panel\/fixed-effects intuition.
- □ Sharpe ratio and annualization assumptions; serial correlation invalidates naive sqrt(time) scaling.
- □ Drawdown, turnover, capacity, slippage, transaction cost and look-ahead\/survivorship bias.

== 3. Linear algebra

=== 3.1 Vectors, spaces, rank and systems \[P0\]

- □ Vector norms L1\/L2\/L-infinity; inner product, angle and cosine similarity.
- □ Linear combination, span, independence, basis and dimension.
- □ Column space, row space, null space and left null space; rank-nullity.
- □ Solve Ax=b by elimination; zero\/one\/infinite solutions; consistency means b is in Col(A).
- □ Rank properties: $\\operatorname{rank}(AB)\\le\\min(\\operatorname{rank}(A),\\operatorname{rank}(B))$ and $\\operatorname{rank}(A)=\\operatorname{rank}(A^{\\mathsf T})$.
- □ Invertibility equivalences for a square matrix: full rank, trivial null space, nonzero determinant, no zero eigenvalue.
- □ Determinant as signed volume scaling; effects of row operations; $\\det(AB)=\\det(A)\\det(B)$.

=== 3.2 Orthogonality, projection and least squares \[P0\]

- □ Orthogonal\/orthonormal vectors; orthogonal matrix $Q$ has $Q^{\\mathsf T}Q=I$ and preserves lengths.
- □ Projection onto $\\operatorname{span}(A)$: $P=A(A^{\\mathsf T}A)^{-1}A^{\\mathsf T}$ when columns are independent.
- □ Least-squares residual is orthogonal to $\\operatorname{Col}(A)$: $A^{\\mathsf T}(y-A\\hat\\beta)=0$.
- □ Gram-Schmidt and QR; QR solves least squares more stably than normal equations.
- □ Moore-Penrose pseudoinverse and minimum-norm solutions.

=== 3.3 Eigenvalues, PSD matrices and dynamics \[P0\]

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-eigenspace-multiplicity">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ $Av=\lambda v$; eigenspaces, algebraic multiplicity, and geometric multiplicity</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. What does an eigenvector represent?</h4>
<p>A general linear map changes both the length and direction of a vector. If a nonzero vector $v$ satisfies</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
Av=\lambda v,
$$</div>
<p>then applying $A$ leaves the line spanned by $v$ invariant and only scales it by $\lambda$; a negative $\lambda$ also reverses direction. Eigenvalues solve</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\det(A-\lambda I)=0,
$$</div>
<p>because the homogeneous system $(A-\lambda I)v=0$ has a nonzero solution precisely when $A-\lambda I$ is singular.</p>
<h4>2. An eigenspace is not one chosen vector</h4>
<p>For a fixed eigenvalue $\lambda$, all corresponding eigenvectors together with zero form the eigenspace</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
E_\lambda=\ker(A-\lambda I).
$$</div>
<p>Thus finding an eigenspace means row-reducing $A-\lambda I$ and finding its null space. Any nonzero multiple of an eigenvector is another eigenvector, so the answer should be a subspace or a basis, not one privileged vector.</p>
<h4>3. Algebraic versus geometric multiplicity</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Algebraic multiplicity (AM):</strong>the multiplicity of $\lambda$ as a root of the characteristic polynomial.</li>
<li><strong>Geometric multiplicity (GM):</strong>the dimension of its eigenspace, $\dim E_\lambda=\operatorname{nullity}(A-\lambda I)$; this is the number of linearly independent eigenvectors contributed by $\lambda$.</li>
</ul>
<p>For every eigenvalue,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
1\le \operatorname{GM}(\lambda)\le \operatorname{AM}(\lambda).
$$</div>
<p>A repeated eigenvalue does not by itself make a matrix non-diagonalizable. For example,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;0\\0&amp;2\end{pmatrix}
\quad\Rightarrow\quad \operatorname{AM}(2)=\operatorname{GM}(2)=2,
$$</div>
<p>whereas the Jordan block</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
B=\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;1\\0&amp;2\end{pmatrix}
\quad\Rightarrow\quad \operatorname{AM}(2)=2,
\qquad \operatorname{GM}(2)=1.
$$</div>
<p>The second matrix supplies only one independent eigen-direction, so it cannot have an eigenvector basis for $\mathbb R^2$.</p>
<h4>4. Connection to diagonalization</h4>
<p>An $n\times n$ matrix is diagonalizable exactly when it has $n$ linearly independent eigenvectors, equivalently when GM equals AM for every eigenvalue. Then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
A=V\Lambda V^{-1},
$$</div>
<p>with eigenvectors in the columns of $V$. Real symmetric matrices are even better: they always admit an orthogonal diagonalization $A=Q\Lambda Q^{\mathsf T}$, and an orthonormal basis can be selected inside every repeated eigenspace.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Interview check:</strong>first obtain eigenvalues and AM from the characteristic polynomial, then compute each $\ker(A-\lambda I)$ to obtain GM. Distinct eigenvalues are sufficient, not necessary, for diagonalizability.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

- □ Trace is the sum and determinant is the product of eigenvalues, with multiplicity.
- □ Diagonalization $A=V\\Lambda V^{-1}$; powers and linear recurrences.
- □ Real symmetric matrices have real eigenvalues and orthonormal eigenvectors: $A=Q\\Lambda Q^{\\mathsf T}$.
- □ Positive semidefinite (PSD): $x^{\\mathsf T}Ax\\ge0$; covariance and Gram matrices are PSD.
- □ Positive definite tests: positive eigenvalues, Cholesky factorization, leading principal minors (for symmetric matrices).

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-rayleigh-quotient">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Rayleigh quotient and largest/smallest eigenvalues</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Definition and scale invariance</h4>
<p>For a real symmetric matrix $A$ and nonzero vector $x$, the Rayleigh quotient is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
R_A(x)=\frac{x^{\mathsf T}Ax}{x^{\mathsf T}x}.
$$</div>
<p>Multiplying $x$ by any nonzero scalar leaves the quotient unchanged, so it depends only on direction. Under the unit-length constraint, it reduces to $x^{\mathsf T}Ax$, the size or curvature of the quadratic form in direction $x$.</p>
<h4>2. Why is it trapped between the extreme eigenvalues?</h4>
<p>Write the orthogonal eigendecomposition $A=Q\Lambda Q^{\mathsf T}$ and express a unit vector as $x=\sum_i c_iq_i$, where $\sum_i c_i^2=1$. Then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
R_A(x)=x^{\mathsf T}Ax
=\sum_i\lambda_i c_i^2.
$$</div>
<p>This is a weighted average of the eigenvalues with nonnegative weights summing to one. Hence</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\lambda_{\min}(A)
=\min_{x\ne0}R_A(x)
\le R_A(x)\le
\max_{x\ne0}R_A(x)
=\lambda_{\max}(A).
$$</div>
<p>Equality holds when $x$ lies in the corresponding extreme eigenspace. If the largest eigenvalue is repeated, multiple directions achieve the same maximum.</p>
<h4>3. Lagrange-multiplier derivation</h4>
<p>Maximize $x^{\mathsf T}Ax$ subject to $x^{\mathsf T}x=1$ using</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathcal L(x,\mu)=x^{\mathsf T}Ax-\mu(x^{\mathsf T}x-1).
$$</div>
<p>For symmetric $A$, stationarity gives $2Ax-2\mu x=0$, or $Ax=\mu x$. Thus the stationary directions of the constrained quadratic form are eigenvectors and the stationary values are eigenvalues; comparing them yields the global maximum and minimum.</p>
<h4>4. Where does it appear?</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>PSD tests:</strong>symmetric $A$ is PSD exactly when $R_A(x)\ge0$ for every nonzero $x$, equivalently $\lambda_{\min}\ge0$.</li>
<li><strong>PCA:</strong>projected variance $v^{\mathsf T}Sv$ is the Rayleigh quotient of the covariance matrix, so its maximizer is the first principal direction.</li>
<li><strong>Optimization:</strong>extreme eigenvalues of a symmetric Hessian describe weakest and strongest curvature; for a positive definite matrix, the spectral condition number is $\lambda_{\max}/\lambda_{\min}$.</li>
<li><strong>Bounds:</strong>a trial direction gives a lower bound on $\lambda_{\max}$ and an upper bound on $\lambda_{\min}$ without computing the full spectrum.</li>
</ul>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfall:</strong>the extreme-eigenvalue statement requires a real symmetric matrix (Hermitian in the complex case). For nonsymmetric $A$, $x^{\mathsf T}Ax$ sees only $(A+A^{\mathsf T})/2$ and does not directly bound all eigenvalues of $A$.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-matrix-powers-dynamics">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Matrix powers and long-run behavior of Markov chains and linear dynamical systems</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Why do eigenvalues govern the long run?</h4>
<p>If $A$ is diagonalizable, $A=V\Lambda V^{-1}$, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
A^t=V\Lambda^tV^{-1},
\qquad
\Lambda^t=\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1^t,\ldots,\lambda_n^t).
$$</div>
<p>After decomposing the initial vector into eigen-directions, component $i$ is multiplied by $\lambda_i^t$. The eigenvalues of largest modulus therefore usually dominate. Let $\rho(A)=\max_i|\lambda_i|$ be the spectral radius:</p>
<ul>
<li>$\rho(A)&lt;1$: every mode decays and $A^t\to0$;</li>
<li>$\rho(A)&gt;1$: at least one mode grows exponentially unless the initial condition has no component in that direction;</li>
<li>$\rho(A)=1$: inspect $-1$, complex unit roots, and Jordan blocks; the system may converge, oscillate, or grow polynomially.</li>
</ul>
<p>For a defective matrix, Jordan blocks generate factors such as $t\lambda^t$ and $t^2\lambda^t$. Thus “the largest eigenvalue equals one” is not enough to assert convergence.</p>
<h4>2. Linear dynamical systems</h4>
<p>The homogeneous system $x_{t+1}=Ax_t$ has $x_t=A^tx_0$. With constant input,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
x_{t+1}=Ax_t+b,
\qquad
x_t=A^tx_0+\sum_{k=0}^{t-1}A^kb.
$$</div>
<p>If $\rho(A)&lt;1$, the matrix geometric series converges and the long-run state is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
x_*=(I-A)^{-1}b,
\qquad x_*=Ax_*+b.
$$</div>
<p>This is a stable steady state. A non-normal matrix can still display large transient amplification before eventual decay even when all eigenvalues lie inside the unit circle; singular values and eigenvector conditioning then matter too.</p>
<h4>3. A Markov chain is a special linear dynamical system</h4>
<p>Under the row-stochastic convention, each row of $P$ sums to one and row probability vectors evolve as</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
p_{t+1}^{\mathsf T}=p_t^{\mathsf T}P,
\qquad
p_t^{\mathsf T}=p_0^{\mathsf T}P^t.
$$</div>
<p>Because $P\mathbf1=\mathbf1$, $1$ is a right eigenvalue. A stationary distribution satisfies $\pi^{\mathsf T}P=\pi^{\mathsf T}$, so it is a normalized left eigenvector for eigenvalue one. For a finite irreducible, aperiodic chain,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P^t\longrightarrow \mathbf1\pi^{\mathsf T},
\qquad
p_t^{\mathsf T}\longrightarrow\pi^{\mathsf T}.
$$</div>
<p>The moduli of the remaining eigenvalues govern mixing speed; a second-largest eigenvalue modulus close to one means slow convergence.</p>
<h4>4. A two-state example</h4>
<p>Let</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P=\begin{pmatrix}1-a&amp;a\\b&amp;1-b\end{pmatrix},
\qquad a,b&gt;0.
$$</div>
<p>Its eigenvalues are $1$ and $1-a-b$, and its stationary distribution is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\pi=\left(\frac{b}{a+b},\frac{a}{a+b}\right).
$$</div>
<p>The deviation from stationarity decays like $|1-a-b|^t$. In a reducible chain, the starting state may determine the eventual closed class; in a periodic chain, distributions can oscillate instead of converging even though a stationary distribution exists.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Solution order:</strong>state the row/column-stochastic convention, find eigenvalue one and the stationary vector, inspect the remaining eigenvalues, and verify irreducibility, aperiodicity, and Jordan structure before claiming convergence.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

=== 3.4 Matrix decompositions \[P0\/P1\]

- □ LU: elimination and repeated solves.

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-qr-least-squares">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ QR: orthogonal bases and stable least squares</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. What does QR factorize?</h4>
<p>For a full-column-rank $A\in\mathbb R^{m\times n}$ with $m\ge n$, the thin QR factorization is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
A=QR,
\qquad Q\in\mathbb R^{m\times n},
\quad Q^{\mathsf T}Q=I,
\quad R\in\mathbb R^{n\times n}.
$$</div>
<p>The columns of $Q$ are an orthonormal basis for $\operatorname{Col}(A)$, while the invertible upper-triangular $R$ stores the coordinates of the original columns in that basis. Full QR extends $Q$ to an $m\times m$ orthogonal matrix; thin QR is sufficient for least squares.</p>
<h4>2. How does QR solve least squares?</h4>
<p>For $\min_\beta\lVert A\beta-b\rVert_2^2$, extend $Q$ to $[Q\ Q_\perp]$. Orthogonal transformations preserve length, so</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\lVert A\beta-b\rVert_2^2
=\lVert R\beta-Q^{\mathsf T}b\rVert_2^2
+\lVert Q_\perp^{\mathsf T}b\rVert_2^2.
$$</div>
<p>The second term does not depend on $\beta$, leaving the upper-triangular solve</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
R\hat\beta=Q^{\mathsf T}b.
$$</div>
<p>Geometrically, the fitted vector $QQ^{\mathsf T}b$ is the orthogonal projection onto $\operatorname{Col}(A)$ and the residual lies in its orthogonal complement.</p>
<h4>3. Why is this more stable than the normal equations?</h4>
<p>The normal equations solve $(A^{\mathsf T}A)\hat\beta=A^{\mathsf T}b$, but</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\kappa_2(A^{\mathsf T}A)=\kappa_2(A)^2.
$$</div>
<p>Forming $A^{\mathsf T}A$ squares the condition number and loses precision in accumulated inner products. QR works directly with $A$ through orthogonal transformations and avoids this artificial worsening, making it the standard stable choice for full-rank least squares. Near rank deficiency, use column-pivoted QR or SVD; SVD exposes tiny singular values most reliably but is usually more expensive.</p>
<h4>4. Gram-Schmidt, Householder, and Givens</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Classical Gram-Schmidt:</strong>intuitive but may lose orthogonality in floating point.</li>
<li><strong>Modified Gram-Schmidt:</strong>more stable and useful for understanding sequential orthogonalization.</li>
<li><strong>Householder reflections:</strong>the standard stable choice for dense QR, eliminating the entries below one column at a time.</li>
<li><strong>Givens rotations:</strong>eliminate one entry at a time and are useful for sparse matrices or online updates.</li>
</ul>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Interview summary:</strong>$Q$ supplies the orthogonal basis and $R$ the coordinates; least squares reduces to $R\hat\beta=Q^{\mathsf T}b$. Do not explicitly form $QQ^{\mathsf T}$, $(A^{\mathsf T}A)^{-1}$, or any matrix inverse merely to solve the problem.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

- □ Eigendecomposition: square diagonalizable operators.

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-svd-fundamentals">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ SVD: $A=U\Sigma V^{\mathsf T}$; rank, null space, pseudoinverse, and conditioning</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. What does SVD factorize?</h4>
<p>Every real matrix $A\in\mathbb R^{m\times n}$, whether square, symmetric, full rank, or diagonalizable, admits</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
A=U\Sigma V^{\mathsf T}.
$$</div>
<p>In a full SVD, $U\in\mathbb R^{m\times m}$ and $V\in\mathbb R^{n\times n}$ are orthogonal, while $\Sigma\in\mathbb R^{m\times n}$ is diagonal except for shape padding:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\sigma_1\ge\sigma_2\ge\cdots\ge\sigma_r&gt;0,
\qquad r=\operatorname{rank}(A).
$$</div>
<p>The $\sigma_i$ are singular values and $u_i,v_i$ are left and right singular vectors. Keeping only columns associated with nonzero singular values gives the compact SVD</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
A=U_r\Sigma_rV_r^{\mathsf T}
=\sum_{i=1}^r\sigma_i u_iv_i^{\mathsf T}.
$$</div>
<h4>2. Rotate—stretch—rotate geometry</h4>
<p>The map $x\mapsto Ax$ can be understood in three steps:</p>
<ol>
<li>$V^{\mathsf T}$ rotates or reflects the input into right-singular-vector coordinates;</li>
<li>$\Sigma$ stretches orthogonal direction $i$ by $\sigma_i$, collapsing zero-singular-value directions;</li>
<li>$U$ rotates or reflects the result into the output space.</li>
</ol>
<p>Thus $A$ maps a unit sphere to an ellipsoid with axes $u_i$ and semiaxis lengths $\sigma_i$. The defining relations are</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
Av_i=\sigma_i u_i,\qquad
A^{\mathsf T}u_i=\sigma_i v_i.
$$</div>
<p>Consequently,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
A^{\mathsf T}Av_i=\sigma_i^2v_i,\qquad
AA^{\mathsf T}u_i=\sigma_i^2u_i.
$$</div>
<p>Right singular vectors are eigenvectors of $A^{\mathsf T}A$, left singular vectors are eigenvectors of $AA^{\mathsf T}$, and nonzero eigenvalues are $\sigma_i^2$. Numerically, compute SVD directly rather than explicitly forming $A^{\mathsf T}A$.</p>
<h4>3. Reading the four fundamental subspaces</h4>
<p>For $r=\operatorname{rank}(A)$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Col}(A)&amp;=\operatorname{span}(u_1,\ldots,u_r),\\
\operatorname{Null}(A^{\mathsf T})&amp;=\operatorname{span}(u_{r+1},\ldots,u_m),\\
\operatorname{Row}(A)=\operatorname{Col}(A^{\mathsf T})
&amp;=\operatorname{span}(v_1,\ldots,v_r),\\
\operatorname{Null}(A)&amp;=\operatorname{span}(v_{r+1},\ldots,v_n).
\end{aligned}
$$</div>
<p>Nonzero singular directions carry information through the map. A zero singular value marks an input direction that is completely unidentifiable and annihilated by $A$. Rank-nullity is visible immediately.</p>
<h4>4. Moore-Penrose pseudoinverse</h4>
<p>Invert each nonzero singular value and leave zero at zero:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
A^+=V\Sigma^+U^{\mathsf T},
\qquad
\Sigma^+_{ii}=
\begin{cases}
1/\sigma_i,&amp;\sigma_i&gt;0,\\
0,&amp;\sigma_i=0.
\end{cases}
$$</div>
<p>Then $x^*=A^+b$ has two central properties:</p>
<ul>
<li>if $Ax=b$ is inconsistent, it minimizes $\lVert Ax-b\rVert_2$;</li>
<li>if least-squares minimizers are not unique, it has minimum $\lVert x\rVert_2$ among them.</li>
</ul>
<p>Direction by direction,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
x^*=A^+b
=\sum_{i=1}^r\frac{u_i^{\mathsf T}b}{\sigma_i}v_i.
$$</div>
<p>A tiny $\sigma_i$ amplifies tiny noise in $b$ into a large coefficient. Truncated SVD and ridge/Tikhonov regularization stabilize least squares by suppressing such directions.</p>
<h4>5. Condition number and numerical rank</h4>
<p>For a full-column-rank matrix, the two-norm condition number is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\kappa_2(A)=\frac{\sigma_{\max}(A)}{\sigma_{\min}(A)}
=\frac{\sigma_1}{\sigma_r}.
$$</div>
<p>A large $\kappa$ means some directions are stretched strongly while others are nearly flattened, so input and rounding errors can be amplified. If $\sigma_r=0$, the matrix is rank deficient and the condition number is infinite. In floating point, numerical rank uses a threshold relative to $\sigma_1$, matrix size, and machine precision rather than an exact comparison with zero.</p>
<h4>6. A minimal example</h4>
<p>For</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
A=\operatorname{diag}(3,1,0),
$$</div>
<p>the singular values are $3,1,0$, the rank is 2, $\operatorname{Null}(A)=\operatorname{span}(e_3)$, and</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
A^+=\operatorname{diag}\!\left(\frac13,1,0\right),
\qquad
\kappa_2\text{ on the nonzero subspace}=3.
$$</div>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong>SVD is not eigendecomposition: it applies to every matrix and $u_i,v_i$ may live in spaces of different dimensions. Singular values are nonnegative. Signs can be flipped in matching pairs, and individual vectors in a repeated-singular-value subspace are nonunique although the subspace is well defined.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-pca-svd-directions">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ PCA: right singular vectors of centered data are principal directions</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Fix the orientation of the data matrix</h4>
<p>Let centered $X\in\mathbb R^{n\times p}$ have observations in rows, features in columns, and zero column means. Its thin SVD is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
X=U\Sigma V^{\mathsf T}.
$$</div>
<ul>
<li>columns $v_j\in\mathbb R^p$ of $V$ live in <strong>feature space</strong>, so they define linear-combination directions of the original features;</li>
<li>$Xv_j=\sigma_j u_j$ gives every observation's coordinates along direction $j$, the principal-component scores;</li>
<li>columns of $U$ live in observation space and should not be confused with feature directions.</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Why are right singular vectors exactly the PCA directions?</h4>
<p>The sample covariance matrix is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
S=\frac{1}{n-1}X^{\mathsf T}X.
$$</div>
<p>Substitute the SVD:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
S
=\frac{1}{n-1}V\Sigma U^{\mathsf T}U\Sigma V^{\mathsf T}
=V\frac{\Sigma^2}{n-1}V^{\mathsf T}.
$$</div>
<p>Therefore $v_j$ is an eigenvector of $S$ with eigenvalue $\sigma_j^2/(n-1)$. The sample variance after projecting onto a unit direction $v$ is also</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{Var}(Xv)
=\frac{1}{n-1}\lVert Xv\rVert_2^2
=v^{\mathsf T}Sv.
$$</div>
<p>This is the Rayleigh quotient of $S$, so maximizing projected variance gives $v_1$. Maximizing successively under orthogonality constraints gives $v_2,v_3,\ldots$.</p>
<h4>3. Variance, scores, and explained variance</h4>
<p>The score vector for component $j$ is $z_j=Xv_j=\sigma_j u_j$, with sample variance</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{Var}(z_j)=\frac{\sigma_j^2}{n-1},
\qquad
\text{explained-ratio}_j
=\frac{\sigma_j^2}{\sum_k\sigma_k^2}.
$$</div>
<p>Keeping the first $k$ directions gives scores $Z=XV_k$ and reconstruction $\hat X=ZV_k^{\mathsf T}=XV_kV_k^{\mathsf T}$. This both retains maximum variance and gives the best rank-$k$ approximation in Frobenius norm.</p>
<h4>4. Why center, and when should you scale?</h4>
<p>Without centering, $X^{\mathsf T}X$ includes the mean direction, so the first component may mostly describe distance from the origin instead of variation around the sample mean. If feature units differ greatly, standardize using training data; otherwise the largest-scale feature dominates. Standardized PCA is equivalent to PCA on the correlation matrix.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong>principal directions are columns of $V$, while scores are $XV=U\Sigma$; the sign of a loading vector is arbitrary; PCA uses variation in $X$ and need not find directions predictive of $y$; centering, scaling, and PCA must be fit inside each training fold to prevent leakage.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-eckart-young">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Eckart-Young: truncated SVD is the best low-rank approximation in spectral and Frobenius norms</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. What is a truncated SVD?</h4>
<p>Write</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
A=\sum_{i=1}^r\sigma_i u_iv_i^{\mathsf T},
\qquad
\sigma_1\ge\cdots\ge\sigma_r&gt;0.
$$</div>
<p>Keeping only the first $k$ singular components gives</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
A_k
=\sum_{i=1}^k\sigma_i u_iv_i^{\mathsf T}
=U_k\Sigma_kV_k^{\mathsf T},
\qquad k&lt;r.
$$</div>
<p>Each $u_iv_i^{\mathsf T}$ has rank one, so $A_k$ has rank at most $k$. The discarded terms are the weaker singular directions.</p>
<h4>2. What exactly does “best” mean?</h4>
<p>The Eckart-Young-Mirsky theorem states that among all matrices $B$ with rank at most $k$, $A_k$ simultaneously minimizes spectral-norm and Frobenius-norm error:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\min_{\operatorname{rank}(B)\le k}\lVert A-B\rVert_2
=\lVert A-A_k\rVert_2
=\sigma_{k+1},
$$</div>
<div class="math-display">$$
\min_{\operatorname{rank}(B)\le k}\lVert A-B\rVert_F
=\lVert A-A_k\rVert_F
=\sqrt{\sum_{i=k+1}^r\sigma_i^2}.
$$</div>
<p>Here</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\lVert M\rVert_F
=\sqrt{\sum_{i,j}M_{ij}^2}
=\sqrt{\sum_i\sigma_i(M)^2}
$$</div>
<p>measures total squared entrywise error, whereas $\lVert M\rVert_2=\sigma_1(M)$ measures maximum amplification in the worst input direction. Spectral error depends only on the first discarded singular value; Frobenius error accumulates energy from every discarded direction.</p>
<h4>3. Why does keeping the largest singular values work?</h4>
<p>SVD decomposes $A$ into mutually orthogonal rank-one components, with Frobenius energy $\sigma_i^2$ in component $i$. A rank-$k$ model can retain at most $k$ independent input-output directions. Discarding a large $\sigma_i$ while keeping a smaller one must increase squared error, so retaining the first $k$ captures maximum energy. More formally, no $k$-dimensional approximation can reduce worst-direction error below $\sigma_{k+1}$, and $A_k$ attains that bound.</p>
<h4>4. Numerical example</h4>
<p>If the singular values are $5,2,1$:</p>
<ul>
<li>the rank-1 approximation keeps $5$, has spectral error $2$, and Frobenius error $\sqrt{2^2+1^2}=\sqrt5$;</li>
<li>its retained squared Frobenius energy is $25/(25+4+1)=83.3\%$;</li>
<li>the rank-2 approximation also keeps $2$, reducing both errors to $1$.</li>
</ul>
<p>This is also the source of PCA explained variance: for centered data $X$, retaining the first $k$ principal components is the best rank-$k$ reconstruction of $X$.</p>
<h4>5. Uses and limitations</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Compression:</strong>store only $U_k,\Sigma_k,V_k$ when $k\ll\min(m,n)$.</li>
<li><strong>Denoising:</strong>truncate weak directions when signal is concentrated in large singular values and noise in small ones.</li>
<li><strong>Latent factors/PCA:</strong>represent dominant variation with a few orthogonal factors.</li>
</ul>
<p>“Best” applies only to the chosen rank and unweighted spectral/Frobenius loss. It need not be optimal for missing data, outliers, sparsity, prediction of $y$, or an economic loss function. If $\sigma_k=\sigma_{k+1}$, the optimal error value is unique but the rank-$k$ subspace or approximating matrix may not be unique.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Interview summary:</strong>write $A_k=U_k\Sigma_kV_k^{\mathsf T}$, then state $\lVert A-A_k\rVert_2=\sigma_{k+1}$ and $\lVert A-A_k\rVert_F^2=\sum_{i&gt;k}\sigma_i^2$. For a general matrix these are singular values, not eigenvalues.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-cholesky-gaussian">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Cholesky: $A=LL^{\mathsf T}$ for positive definite $A$; correlated Gaussian simulation</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Conditions and form</h4>
<p>If a real matrix $A$ is symmetric positive definite, meaning $x^{\mathsf T}Ax&gt;0$ for every nonzero $x$, then there is a unique lower-triangular $L$ with positive diagonal such that</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
A=LL^{\mathsf T}.
$$</div>
<p>Cholesky is an LU-style factorization specialized to symmetric positive definite structure. It uses roughly half the storage and work of general LU and is common for covariance matrices, Gaussian likelihoods, least squares, and positive definite linear systems.</p>
<h4>2. A $2\times2$ factorization</h4>
<p>Write</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}a&amp;c\\c&amp;b\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
L=\begin{pmatrix}\ell_{11}&amp;0\\\ell_{21}&amp;\ell_{22}\end{pmatrix}.
$$</div>
<p>Matching entries in $A=LL^{\mathsf T}$ gives</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\ell_{11}=\sqrt a,
\qquad
\ell_{21}=\frac{c}{\ell_{11}},
\qquad
\ell_{22}=\sqrt{b-\ell_{21}^2}.
$$</div>
<p>Positivity under the square root reflects positive definiteness. Higher-dimensional Cholesky repeats the same idea column by column. To solve $Ax=b$, do not form $A^{-1}$: first forward-solve $Ly=b$, then backward-solve $L^{\mathsf T}x=y$.</p>
<h4>3. Why does it simulate a correlated Gaussian?</h4>
<p>For target covariance $\Sigma=LL^{\mathsf T}$, generate independent standard normals and set</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
Z\sim N(0,I),
\qquad X=\mu+LZ.
$$</div>
<p>Then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathbb E[X]=\mu,
\qquad
\operatorname{Cov}(X)
=L\operatorname{Cov}(Z)L^{\mathsf T}
=LL^{\mathsf T}
=\Sigma.
$$</div>
<p>Geometrically, $L$ stretches and rotates a spherical cloud of independent Gaussian draws into an ellipsoid with the target correlation structure.</p>
<h4>4. Constructing two correlated standard normals</h4>
<p>For</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\Sigma=\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;\rho\\\rho&amp;1\end{pmatrix},
\qquad |\rho|&lt;1,
$$</div>
<p>a Cholesky factor is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
L=\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\rho&amp;\sqrt{1-\rho^2}\end{pmatrix}.
$$</div>
<p>If independent $Z_1,Z_2\sim N(0,1)$, then</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
X_1=Z_1,
\qquad
X_2=\rho Z_1+\sqrt{1-\rho^2}Z_2
$$</div>
<p>are standard normal with $\operatorname{Corr}(X_1,X_2)=\rho$. This is the standard Monte Carlo construction for correlated normals.</p>
<h4>5. Semidefinite matrices, numerical error, and alternatives</h4>
<p>If $A$ is only PSD rather than strictly PD, as with perfectly collinear features, ordinary Cholesky encounters a zero pivot and fails. Remove redundant directions, use an eigen/SVD low-rank square root, or add small diagonal jitter $\varepsilon I$ when the model justifies regularization. If an allegedly symmetric covariance matrix fails only because of floating-point noise, investigate the construction before blindly adding large jitter.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong>check whether software returns a lower or upper factor. If $A=LL^{\mathsf T}$, use $LZ$ for simulation rather than casually replacing it with $L^{\mathsf T}Z$. Cholesky failure often reveals that a matrix is not strictly positive definite.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

=== 3.5 Matrix calculus and optimization prerequisites \[P0\]

- □ Shapes first: scalar-by-vector gradient, Jacobian and Hessian.
- □ $\\nabla_x(a^{\\mathsf T}x)=a$.

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-quadratic-form-gradient">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ $\nabla_x(x^{\mathsf T}Ax)=(A+A^{\mathsf T})x$; for symmetric $A$, this is $2Ax$</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Check shapes and treat $A$ as constant</h4>
<p>Let $x\in\mathbb R^n$ be a column vector and let $A\in\mathbb R^{n\times n}$ not depend on $x$. The quadratic form</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
f(x)=x^{\mathsf T}Ax
$$</div>
<p>has shape $(1\times n)(n\times n)(n\times1)=1\times1$, so $f$ is scalar. Under the column-gradient convention, $\nabla_x f$ must be $n\times1$, matching $(A+A^{\mathsf T})x$.</p>
<h4>2. The safest hand derivation: expand coordinates</h4>
<p>Write the scalar as a double sum:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
f(x)=\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n x_iA_{ij}x_j.
$$</div>
<p>When differentiating with respect to $x_k$, that variable appears in two roles:</p>
<ul>
<li>as the left factor $x_i$ when $i=k$;</li>
<li>as the right factor $x_j$ when $j=k$.</li>
</ul>
<p>The product rule therefore gives</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_k}
&amp;=\sum_j A_{kj}x_j+\sum_i x_iA_{ik}\\
&amp;=(Ax)_k+(A^{\mathsf T}x)_k.
\end{aligned}
$$</div>
<p>Stacking the components for $k=1,\ldots,n$ yields</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\nabla_x f=Ax+A^{\mathsf T}x=(A+A^{\mathsf T})x.
$$</div>
<p><strong>Memory cue:</strong>$x$ appears twice, so differentiation produces two terms. The left $x^{\mathsf T}$ contributes $Ax$ and the right $x$ contributes $A^{\mathsf T}x$.</p>
<h4>3. The fastest interview method: differentials</h4>
<p>Apply the product rule to $f=x^{\mathsf T}Ax$:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm df
&amp;=(\mathrm dx)^{\mathsf T}Ax
+x^{\mathsf T}A\,\mathrm dx\\
&amp;=(Ax)^{\mathsf T}\mathrm dx
+(A^{\mathsf T}x)^{\mathsf T}\mathrm dx\\
&amp;=\bigl[(A+A^{\mathsf T})x\bigr]^{\mathsf T}\mathrm dx.
\end{aligned}
$$</div>
<p>Compare this with the gradient definition</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathrm df=(\nabla_x f)^{\mathsf T}\mathrm dx
$$</div>
<p>to read off the answer. The step uses the fact that a scalar equals its transpose: $x^{\mathsf T}A\,\mathrm dx=(x^{\mathsf T}A\,\mathrm dx)^{\mathsf T}=(\mathrm dx)^{\mathsf T}A^{\mathsf T}x$.</p>
<h4>4. Why does a quadratic form see only the symmetric part of $A$?</h4>
<p>Decompose $A$ into symmetric and skew-symmetric parts:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
A=S+K,\qquad
S=\frac{A+A^{\mathsf T}}2,\qquad
K=\frac{A-A^{\mathsf T}}2.
$$</div>
<p>Since $K^{\mathsf T}=-K$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
x^{\mathsf T}Kx
=(x^{\mathsf T}Kx)^{\mathsf T}
=x^{\mathsf T}K^{\mathsf T}x
=-x^{\mathsf T}Kx,
$$</div>
<p>so $x^{\mathsf T}Kx=0$. Hence</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
x^{\mathsf T}Ax=x^{\mathsf T}Sx,
\qquad
\nabla_x f=2Sx=(A+A^{\mathsf T})x.
$$</div>
<p>Two useful special cases follow:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
A=A^{\mathsf T}\ \Rightarrow\ \nabla_x(x^{\mathsf T}Ax)=2Ax,
$$</div>
<div class="math-display">$$
A=-A^{\mathsf T}\ \Rightarrow\ x^{\mathsf T}Ax=0,\quad \nabla_x f=0.
$$</div>
<p>This is why optimization often uses $\frac12x^{\mathsf T}Ax$. For symmetric $A$, the factor $1/2$ cancels the 2:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\nabla_x\!\left(\frac12x^{\mathsf T}Ax\right)=Ax,
\qquad
\nabla_x^2(x^{\mathsf T}Ax)=A+A^{\mathsf T}.
$$</div>
<h4>5. Check with a nonsymmetric $2\times2$ example</h4>
<p>Let</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}a&amp;b\\c&amp;d\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
x=\begin{pmatrix}x_1\\x_2\end{pmatrix}.
$$</div>
<p>Expanding gives</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
f=ax_1^2+(b+c)x_1x_2+dx_2^2,
$$</div>
<p>and therefore</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\nabla_x f
=\begin{pmatrix}
2ax_1+(b+c)x_2\\
(b+c)x_1+2dx_2
\end{pmatrix}
=(A+A^{\mathsf T})x.
$$</div>
<p>The cross term contains $b+c$, not $2b$, showing why $2Ax$ is wrong unless $A$ is symmetric.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Hand-derivation template:</strong>if you forget the formula, write $f=\sum_{ij}x_iA_{ij}x_j$ and collect the $i=k$ and $j=k$ terms, or write $\mathrm df=(\mathrm dx)^{\mathsf T}Ax+x^{\mathsf T}A\,\mathrm dx$. The result assumes $A$ is independent of $x$; if $A=A(x)$, derivatives of $A$ add extra terms.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

- □ $\\nabla_x\\lVert Ax-b\\rVert_2^2=2A^{\\mathsf T}(Ax-b)$.
- □ ∇_X tr(A'X)=A; cyclic trace trick; d log det X = tr(X^(-1)dX).
- □ Chain rule\/backpropagation through vector functions.
- □ Gradient, Hessian, critical point, convexity and strong convexity.
- □ Lagrange multipliers and KKT conditions (feasibility, stationarity, complementary slackness).
- □ Gradient descent convergence intuition; learning rate; SGD noise; momentum and adaptive methods.

=== 3.6 Numerical linear algebra \[P1\]

- □ Never compute an inverse merely to solve Ax=b; use a solve\/factorization.
- □ Condition number measures sensitivity; X'X squares the condition number of X.
- □ Floating-point precision, cancellation, overflow\/underflow and log-sum-exp.
- □ Sherman-Morrison and Woodbury identities for low-rank updates \[P2\].
- □ Sparse matrices and iterative methods; power iteration\/conjugate gradient intuition \[P2\].

== 4. Combinatorics and discrete problem solving

=== 4.1 Counting toolkit \[P0\]

- □ Sum\/product rules; permutations, combinations, multinomial coefficients.
- □ Ordered versus unordered selections; repetition allowed or not.
- □ Binomial theorem and identities such as $\\binom nk=\\binom{n-1}{k}\\mathplus{}\\binom{n-1}{k-1}$.
- □ Stars and bars with upper\/lower-bound corrections.
- □ Inclusion-exclusion and derangements.
- □ Pigeonhole principle, double counting and bijective proofs.
~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-circular-permutations">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Circular Permutations and Quotienting by Rotations or Reflections</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Why divide $n!$ by $n$?</h4>
<p>Arrange $n$ distinct people around a round table and regard global rotations as identical. Every circular arrangement appears $n$ times among linear listings because any of its $n$ people can be written first. Therefore</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\frac{n!}{n}=(n-1)!.
$$</div>
<p>Equivalently, fix one person and arrange the other $n-1$ around that anchor. The reduction is not because a circle “has one fewer seat”; it comes from quotienting by rotational symmetry.</p>
<h4>2. First decide which arrangements count as identical</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Labeled seats:</strong> a rotation moves people to different seat labels, so there are $n!$ arrangements.</li>
<li><strong>Round table, rotations identified:</strong> there are $(n-1)!$ arrangements.</li>
<li><strong>Necklace or bracelet, reflections also identified:</strong> for $n\ge3$ distinct objects, the usual count is $(n-1)!/2$.</li>
</ul>
<p>With repeated colors or arrangements that themselves have rotational or reflectional symmetry, one cannot mechanically divide by $n$ or $2n$. Burnside's lemma counts fixed configurations under each symmetry.</p>
<h4>3. Common restriction: two people must be adjacent</h4>
<p>If specified people A and B must sit together, treat them as one block. The block plus the other $n-2$ people make $n-1$ circular objects, yielding $(n-2)!$ arrangements. The internal order of A and B contributes a factor 2:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
N(\text{A and B adjacent})=2(n-2)!.
$$</div>
<p>For non-adjacency, subtract this from the total $(n-1)!$.</p>
<h4>4. Alternating two groups</h4>
<p>Suppose $m$ men and $m$ women must alternate. First arrange the men circularly in $(m-1)!$ ways. Their positions create exactly $m$ gaps, into which the women can be placed in $m!$ ways. Thus</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
N(\text{alternating groups})=(m-1)!\,m!.
$$</div>
<p>Fixing one group and filling the resulting gaps is a standard circular-arrangement strategy.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> determine whether seats are labeled and whether rotations or reflections are identified; round-table and necklace counting are different; repeated objects or internal symmetry can make simple division overcount; and after using a block, count the resulting objects circularly rather than linearly.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

=== 4.2 Recurrences, generating functions and DP \[P0\/P1\]

- □ Define a state that contains exactly the information needed for the future.
- □ Base cases, transition, evaluation order and final aggregation.
- □ Memoization versus tabulation; time\/space complexity; rolling-array optimization.
- □ Linear recurrences, characteristic equations and matrix exponentiation.
- □ Ordinary generating functions as algebraic encodings of sequences \[P1\].
- □ Catalan structures: balanced parentheses, binary trees and noncrossing matchings \[P1\].
- □ Stirling numbers and Bell numbers for partitions \[P2\].

=== 4.3 Graphs, invariants and constructive reasoning \[P1\]

- □ Graph, degree, path, cycle, connected component, tree and bipartite graph.
- □ Handshaking lemma; tree has n-1 edges; unique path characterization.
- □ Euler trails versus Hamiltonian paths; do not confuse their criteria.
- □ Coloring, matching and shortest paths at a conceptual level.
- □ Invariants, monovariants, parity, extremal principle and symmetry.
- □ Probabilistic method: prove existence by showing positive probability \[P2\].

*Triangle-walk pattern:* encode the current vertex\/direction or use adjacency-matrix powers. Ask whether the walk is on three vertices, a triangular lattice, or a continuous geometric triangle—the wording changes the state space completely.

== 5. Machine-learning mathematics

=== 5.1 Problem setup and generalization \[P0\]

- □ Supervised\/unsupervised learning; regression\/classification\/ranking.
- □ Empirical risk minimization: average loss plus regularization.
- □ Train\/validation\/test roles; hyperparameters must not be chosen on test data.
- □ Bias-variance trade-off; irreducible noise; underfit versus overfit diagnostics.
- □ Parametric versus nonparametric; generative versus discriminative.
- □ Curse of dimensionality and why distance concentration hurts nearest-neighbor methods.
- □ Data leakage, selection bias, distribution shift and nonstationarity.

=== 5.2 Linear and generalized linear models \[P0\]

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-ml-linear-regularized-models">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Linear regression, weighted least squares, Ridge, Lasso, and Elastic Net</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. What does a linear model fit?</h4>
<p>Linear regression assumes the conditional mean has the form $\mathbb E[Y\mid X=x]=x^{\mathsf T}\beta$. Ordinary least squares chooses</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\hat\beta_{\mathrm{OLS}}
=\arg\min_\beta\sum_{i=1}^n(y_i-x_i^{\mathsf T}\beta)^2.
$$</div>
<p>“Linear” means linear in the parameters $\beta$; features may include polynomials, interactions, or splines. OLS can target prediction or, under stronger assumptions, coefficient inference. The two goals place different demands on bias, variance, and variable selection.</p>
<h4>2. Weighted least squares (WLS)</h4>
<p>If observation errors have unequal variances and $\operatorname{Var}(\varepsilon_i\mid x_i)=\sigma_i^2$ is known or credibly estimated, WLS minimizes</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\sum_{i=1}^n w_i(y_i-x_i^{\mathsf T}\beta)^2,
\qquad w_i\propto\frac1{\sigma_i^2}.
$$</div>
<p>More precise observations receive more weight; equal weights recover OLS. Weights should encode precision or sampling design. Downweighting a point after seeing a large residual changes the target and may introduce bias.</p>
<h4>3. Three regularizers</h4>
<div class="math-display">$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{Ridge: }&amp;\min_\beta \frac1{2n}\lVert y-X\beta\rVert_2^2+\lambda\lVert\beta\rVert_2^2,\\
\text{Lasso: }&amp;\min_\beta \frac1{2n}\lVert y-X\beta\rVert_2^2+\lambda\lVert\beta\rVert_1,\\
\text{Elastic Net: }&amp;\min_\beta \frac1{2n}\lVert y-X\beta\rVert_2^2
+\lambda\!\left[\alpha\lVert\beta\rVert_1+(1-\alpha)\lVert\beta\rVert_2^2\right].
\end{aligned}
$$</div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Ridge</strong> smoothly shrinks all coefficients and stabilizes multicollinearity, but rarely sets coefficients exactly to zero.</li>
<li><strong>Lasso</strong> yields sparse solutions but may select one correlated predictor arbitrarily.</li>
<li><strong>Elastic Net</strong> combines sparsity with grouped shrinkage and often behaves better for correlated predictors.</li>
</ul>
<h4>4. Practical workflow</h4>
<p>Center and scale continuous features inside each training fold; otherwise the penalty treats measurement units unevenly. The intercept is normally unpenalized. Choose $\lambda$ and Elastic Net's $\alpha$ by leakage-safe cross-validation. Increasing $\lambda$ usually trades more bias for less variance.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> regularization need not reduce training error; its target is validation/test performance. Ridge does not create causal interpretation, Lasso selection is not significance or causality, and valid uncertainty after model selection requires methods beyond ordinary OLS standard errors.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-logistic-regression">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Logistic regression, Bernoulli likelihood, binary cross-entropy, and gradient</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. From a linear score to probability</h4>
<p>For $Y\in\{0,1\}$, set $\eta_i=x_i^{\mathsf T}\beta$ and map any real score to $(0,1)$ using the sigmoid:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
p_i=P(Y_i=1\mid x_i)=\sigma(\eta_i)=\frac1{1+e^{-\eta_i}},
\qquad
\log\frac{p_i}{1-p_i}=x_i^{\mathsf T}\beta.
$$</div>
<p>The model is linear in <strong>log-odds</strong>. Holding other variables fixed, increasing $x_j$ by one multiplies the odds by $e^{\beta_j}$; it does not add a constant $\beta_j$ to probability.</p>
<h4>2. Why is cross-entropy a negative log-likelihood?</h4>
<p>Conditionally, $Y_i\sim\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p_i)$, so the likelihood is $\prod_i p_i^{y_i}(1-p_i)^{1-y_i}$. Its negative logarithm is binary cross-entropy:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
L(\beta)=-\sum_{i=1}^n\left[y_i\log p_i+(1-y_i)\log(1-p_i)\right].
$$</div>
<p>Maximizing the Bernoulli likelihood and minimizing binary cross-entropy are therefore the same optimization problem.</p>
<h4>3. Gradient, Hessian, and geometry</h4>
<div class="math-display">$$
\nabla L(\beta)=X^{\mathsf T}(p-y),
\qquad
\nabla^2L(\beta)=X^{\mathsf T}WX,
\quad W_{ii}=p_i(1-p_i).
$$</div>
<p>The gradient aggregates prediction errors $p_i-y_i$ weighted by features. The Hessian is positive semidefinite, so the unregularized negative log-likelihood is convex. A decision threshold should reflect costs and need not be 0.5; changing it does not change the underlying probabilities or ranking.</p>
<h4>4. Regularization and separation</h4>
<p>L2 and L1 penalties supply Ridge- and Lasso-like shrinkage. If a hyperplane perfectly separates the training classes, unpenalized MLE coefficients may diverge; regularization gives a finite, more stable solution. With imbalanced classes, consider class weights, PR-AUC, calibration, and cost-sensitive thresholds rather than accuracy alone.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> logistic regression has a linear decision boundary but not linear probabilities; coefficients are log-odds effects, low training loss does not guarantee calibration, and choosing a threshold on the test set leaks information.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-softmax-regression">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Multiclass softmax, cross-entropy, identifiability, and numerical stability</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Multiclass probabilities</h4>
<p>For $K$ mutually exclusive classes, produce logits $z_k=x^{\mathsf T}\beta_k$ and normalize them:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
p_k=P(Y=k\mid x)=\frac{e^{z_k}}{\sum_{j=1}^Ke^{z_j}},
\qquad \sum_{k=1}^Kp_k=1.
$$</div>
<p>If the true class is $y$, the single-observation cross-entropy is $-\log p_y$, heavily penalizing confident assignment of tiny probability to the true class.</p>
<h4>2. Why is identifiability an issue?</h4>
<p>Adding the same constant $c$ to all logits leaves softmax unchanged, so the raw parameterization is not unique. One can make a reference class's coefficients zero or impose an equivalent constraint. Coefficients are then interpreted as changes in class log-odds relative to that reference.</p>
<h4>3. Stable computation and gradient</h4>
<p>Direct exponentiation of large logits can overflow. With $m=\max_jz_j$, use</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\log\sum_j e^{z_j}=m+\log\sum_j e^{z_j-m}.
$$</div>
<p>This is the log-sum-exp trick. The gradient for each class is again predicted probability minus one-hot truth times features, summarized as $X^{\mathsf T}(P-Y)$. Softmax is one joint multiclass model; one-vs-rest fits independent binary models whose outputs need not naturally sum to one.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> the largest softmax value is not proof of reliability, especially under distribution shift. Implement stable log-softmax/cross-entropy directly instead of computing huge exponentials and then taking logarithms.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-glm-framework">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ GLMs: random component, linear predictor, link function, and Poisson regression</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. The three GLM components</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Random component:</strong> $Y\mid X$ belongs to an exponential-family distribution such as Normal, Bernoulli, or Poisson.</li>
<li><strong>Linear predictor:</strong> $\eta=x^{\mathsf T}\beta$.</li>
<li><strong>Link:</strong> $g(\mu)=\eta$, where $\mu=\mathbb E[Y\mid X=x]$.</li>
</ol>
<p>Standard pairings are Normal with identity link, Bernoulli with logit link, and Poisson with log link. A GLM does not require a Normal response; it matches the conditional distribution and mean–variance relationship to the data type.</p>
<h4>2. Poisson regression</h4>
<p>For a nonnegative count $Y_i$, use</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
Y_i\mid x_i\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\mu_i),
\qquad
\log\mu_i=x_i^{\mathsf T}\beta,
\qquad
\mu_i=e^{x_i^{\mathsf T}\beta}&gt;0.
$$</div>
<p>A unit increase in $x_j$ multiplies expected count by $e^{\beta_j}$. For unequal exposure $t_i$, use the offset $\log\mu_i=\log t_i+x_i^{\mathsf T}\beta$, fixing the coefficient on $\log t_i$ at one and modeling a rate per unit exposure.</p>
<h4>3. Canonical links, deviance, and overdispersion</h4>
<p>Canonical links make likelihood derivatives especially simple; Normal, Bernoulli, and Poisson use identity, logit, and log links. Deviance compares a fitted model's log-likelihood with that of a saturated model and helps compare nested models or assess fit.</p>
<p>Poisson imposes $\operatorname{Var}(Y\mid X)=\mathbb E[Y\mid X]$. Unobserved heterogeneity or clustering often creates larger variance (overdispersion), making naive standard errors too small. Robust/quasi-Poisson or Negative Binomial models may be preferable; excessive zeros may call for hurdle or zero-inflated models.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> “linear” in GLM means the linked mean is linear in parameters, not that the response itself is linear. Choose a link compatible with response support, and check dispersion, exposure, and dependence before applying Poisson automatically to count data.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-interactions-basis-expansion">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Interactions and nonlinear basis expansion: interpretation, regularization, and validation</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. An interaction means one effect depends on another variable</h4>
<p>In $y=\beta_0+\beta_1x_1+\beta_2x_2+\beta_3x_1x_2+\varepsilon$, the marginal effect of $x_1$ is</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\frac{\partial\mathbb E[Y\mid X]}{\partial x_1}=\beta_1+\beta_3x_2.
$$</div>
<p>Thus $\beta_1$ is the effect of $x_1$ only when $x_2=0$. Centering continuous variables can make zero meaningful and reduce numerical correlation between main and product terms. The hierarchy principle usually retains $x_1$ and $x_2$ whenever $x_1x_2$ is included.</p>
<h4>2. Basis expansion lets a linear model represent curves</h4>
<p>Map raw $x$ to $\phi(x)=(x,x^2,x^3,\ldots)$, piecewise polynomials, splines, or Fourier terms and fit $\phi(x)^{\mathsf T}\beta$. The model remains linear in parameters but nonlinear in input. High-degree polynomials can oscillate badly near and beyond boundaries; splines usually provide more local, stable flexibility.</p>
<h4>3. Capacity control and leakage-safe validation</h4>
<p>Many interactions and basis terms rapidly increase dimension and variance. Combine them with Ridge/Lasso/Elastic Net, limit degree or knots, and select complexity by cross-validation. Fit scaling, knot placement, and feature selection inside each training fold before transforming validation data.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> interaction coefficients cannot be interpreted without variable scales and main effects; adding $x^2$ changes the meaning of the $x$ coefficient; and better training fit does not validate an interaction or polynomial extrapolation outside the observed support.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-calibration-discrimination">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Calibration versus discrimination: probability quality, ranking, and thresholds</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. They answer different questions</h4>
<p><strong>Discrimination</strong> asks whether positives rank above negatives; ROC-AUC is approximately the probability that a random positive receives a higher score than a random negative. <strong>Calibration</strong> asks whether probability levels are trustworthy: among predictions near 0.7, roughly 70% should be positive.</p>
<p>A model may rank very well while being overconfident, or be broadly calibrated while barely discriminating. High AUC does not imply that a score of 0.8 means an 80% chance.</p>
<h4>2. How to evaluate each</h4>
<ul>
<li>Ranking: ROC-AUC, plus precision–recall curves/PR-AUC for heavily imbalanced classes.</li>
<li>Probability quality: reliability diagrams, Brier score $n^{-1}\sum_i(p_i-y_i)^2$, and log loss.</li>
<li>Decision quality: choose a threshold using false-positive/negative costs, capacity constraints, and the deployment base rate.</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. How to calibrate</h4>
<p>Platt scaling fits a logistic transform of raw scores; isotonic regression is more flexible but can overfit small samples. Train the calibrator on held-out or out-of-fold predictions and reserve an independent test set for final evaluation. Distribution or base-rate drift can invalidate old calibration, so monitor it after deployment.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> accuracy depends on threshold and base rate, AUC largely ignores probability magnitude, tuning thresholds or calibration on the test set is leakage, and calibration does not imply that 0.5 is the optimal operational threshold.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

=== 5.3 Trees and ensembles \[P0\]

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-decision-trees">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Regression and classification trees; greedy splitting</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. What does a tree do?</h4>
<p>A decision tree recursively asks questions such as $x_j\le s$, partitioning feature space into axis-aligned rectangular regions. Observations in one leaf share a prediction:</p>
<ul>
<li>a <strong>regression tree</strong> usually predicts the mean training response in the leaf;</li>
<li>a <strong>classification tree</strong> predicts the majority class or uses leaf class frequencies as probabilities.</li>
</ul>
<p>Trees naturally represent nonlinearities and interactions. Asking about volume only after first splitting on volatility says that the effect of volume depends on volatility.</p>
<h4>2. How is a greedy split selected?</h4>
<p>At node $R$, enumerate candidate feature/threshold pairs, producing children $R_L,R_R$, and maximize weighted impurity reduction:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\Delta I
=I(R)-\frac{n_L}{n}I(R_L)-\frac{n_R}{n}I(R_R).
$$</div>
<p>Regression commonly uses squared error; classification uses Gini or entropy. After committing to the best current split, repeat within each child. The procedure is greedy because it does not revisit earlier choices or search globally over complete trees, an intractable combinatorial problem in general.</p>
<h4>3. Prediction and interpretation</h4>
<p>A single prediction can be explained by its path through the tree. Trees do not require feature scaling and are unchanged by monotone unit transformations. However, axis-aligned splits may need many steps to approximate a diagonal boundary, and regression trees generally predict boundary-leaf values outside the training range rather than extrapolating a trend.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> each greedy training improvement need not improve test error; tiny-leaf class frequencies are unstable and need not be calibrated; and a deep single tree is highly sensitive to small data changes, motivating bagging.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-tree-impurity">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ MSE, entropy, and Gini: node impurity and split gain</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Squared error for regression trees</h4>
<p>If node $R$ predicts a constant $c$, minimizing $\sum_{i\in R}(y_i-c)^2$ gives $c=\bar y_R$. A standard regression impurity is therefore</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
I_{\mathrm{MSE}}(R)=\frac1{|R|}\sum_{i\in R}(y_i-\bar y_R)^2.
$$</div>
<p>A good split makes both children more homogeneous and sharply reduces their weighted squared error. With absolute-error loss, the optimal leaf constant would instead be the median.</p>
<h4>2. Gini and entropy for classification</h4>
<p>If class $k$ has proportion $p_k$ in a node,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
I_{\mathrm{Gini}}=1-\sum_kp_k^2,
\qquad
I_{\mathrm{Entropy}}=-\sum_kp_k\log p_k.
$$</div>
<p>Both equal zero in a pure node. For a balanced binary node, Gini is $1/2$ and entropy is $\log2$, their respective maxima. Gini can also be viewed as the probability that two labels independently drawn from the node proportions disagree.</p>
<h4>3. Why weight child nodes by size?</h4>
<p>A split that isolates one easy observation but leaves almost everyone else mixed should not look excellent. Child impurities must be weighted by $n_L/n$ and $n_R/n$. Entropy reacts slightly more to small class probabilities and Gini is cheaper to compute, but they usually produce similar trees; regularization and noise often matter more.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> node impurity is not whole-tree test error; training impurity can always decline as a tree grows, so it does not justify unlimited depth; and raw values from different impurity definitions are not directly comparable.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-tree-complexity-pruning">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Depth, minimum leaf size, pruning, and the bias–variance trade-off</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Why do deep trees overfit?</h4>
<p>More depth and smaller leaves capture finer local structure, lowering training bias but estimating each leaf from fewer observations and increasing variance. At the extreme, one observation per leaf nearly memorizes training data.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>max depth</strong> limits how many questions a path can ask;</li>
<li><strong>min samples leaf</strong> directly smooths leaf predictions;</li>
<li><strong>min samples split / min impurity decrease</strong> blocks poorly supported splits.</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Pre-pruning versus post-pruning</h4>
<p>Pre-pruning stops during growth. Post-pruning grows a larger tree and then removes branches whose fit improvement is not worth their complexity. Cost-complexity pruning minimizes</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
R_\alpha(T)=R(T)+\alpha|T_{\mathrm{leaves}}|,
$$</div>
<p>where $R(T)$ is leaf loss and $\alpha$ penalizes leaf count; larger $\alpha$ produces a smaller tree.</p>
<h4>3. Selecting complexity</h4>
<p>Select depth, minimum leaf size, or pruning strength by cross-validation rather than training accuracy. Learning curves help diagnose high bias (both training and validation poor) versus high variance (training strong, validation weak).</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> excessive pruning can erase real interactions; min leaf size is not tree depth; and tree size must be selected inside training folds rather than after inspecting the test set.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-bagging-random-forest">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Bagging and Random Forest: bootstrap, feature subsampling, and variance reduction</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Core bagging idea</h4>
<p>Bagging draws $B$ bootstrap samples, fits one usually deep tree to each, and averages regression predictions or classification probabilities/votes:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\hat f_{\mathrm{bag}}(x)=\frac1B\sum_{b=1}^B\hat f^{(b)}(x).
$$</div>
<p>A deep tree has relatively low bias but high variance. Averaging diverse trees cancels unstable fluctuations without usually increasing bias much.</p>
<h4>2. Why add Random Forest feature sampling?</h4>
<p>If one strong feature is chosen near the top of nearly every bootstrap tree, tree predictions remain highly correlated and averaging helps less. Random Forest considers a random feature subset at every node, deliberately weakening individual trees but decorrelating them.</p>
<p>If each tree has variance $\sigma^2$ and pairwise correlation about $\rho$, the variance of an average of $B$ trees is approximately</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{Var}(\bar f)
=\rho\sigma^2+\frac{1-\rho}{B}\sigma^2.
$$</div>
<p>More trees remove only the second term; lowering $\rho$ through feature subsampling is therefore crucial.</p>
<h4>3. Tuning and practice</h4>
<p>More trees usually stabilize rather than cause classical overfitting, but cost more. Smaller max-features reduces correlation while potentially increasing individual-tree bias; minimum leaf size controls smoothing. Scaling is unnecessary, but missing-data handling, encoding, and leakage-safe time/group validation still matter.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> Random Forest randomness includes per-node feature subsampling, not just bootstrap; bagging mainly reduces variance and cannot repair leakage, biased labels, or distribution shift; many correlated trees do not represent many independent pieces of information.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-gradient-boosting">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Boosting: sequential residual/negative-gradient fitting and the learning-rate/tree-count trade-off</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Fundamental difference from bagging</h4>
<p>Bagging fits trees independently and averages them. Boosting fits weak learners sequentially, each correcting what the current ensemble still gets wrong:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
F_m(x)=F_{m-1}(x)+\eta h_m(x).
$$</div>
<p>For squared loss, the negative gradient equals residual $y_i-F_{m-1}(x_i)$, so the next tree fits residuals. For a general differentiable loss it fits the negative gradient with respect to current predictions—hence gradient boosting.</p>
<h4>2. Learning rate, number of trees, and depth</h4>
<ul>
<li>Small $\eta$ makes cautious updates and usually requires more trees, often improving generalization at higher computation cost.</li>
<li>Too few trees underfit; too many under a fixed configuration may fit noise, so use validation/early stopping.</li>
<li>Base-tree depth controls interaction order: stumps emphasize main effects, deeper trees capture complex interactions with higher variance.</li>
</ul>
<p>Learning rate and tree count must be interpreted jointly; halving $\eta$ while keeping tree count fixed roughly reduces total update capacity.</p>
<h4>3. Implementations and regularization</h4>
<p>XGBoost/LightGBM add second-order approximations, leaf penalties, row/column subsampling, missing-value paths, and efficient binning. Common controls include shallow trees, shrinkage, subsampling, L1/L2 penalties, and early stopping. Boosting is inherently sequential across trees and less parallel than bagging.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> classification boosting fits loss gradients, not merely incorrect 0/1 labels; falling training loss need not mean improving validation loss; and depth, tree count, learning rate, and sampling rates are strongly coupled.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-oob-feature-importance">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ OOB estimation, feature-importance bias, and correlated-feature substitution</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Out-of-bag estimation</h4>
<p>In a bootstrap sample of size $n$, an original observation is omitted with probability</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\left(1-\frac1n\right)^n\approx e^{-1}\approx36.8\%.
$$</div>
<p>For observation $i$, aggregate only trees that did not train on it to obtain an approximately out-of-sample OOB prediction. OOB error is useful for tuning without a separate validation set, but does not replace a final test set or dependency-aware time/group validation.</p>
<h4>2. Why is impurity importance biased?</h4>
<p>Mean decrease in impurity sums a feature's training impurity reductions. Continuous or high-cardinality variables offer more candidate splits and therefore more chances for accidental gains, biasing their importance upward; fitted noise is also counted as importance.</p>
<h4>3. Permutation importance and correlated predictors</h4>
<p>Permutation importance shuffles one validation feature and measures performance loss. With highly correlated $X_1,X_2$, the model may substitute $X_2$ after $X_1$ is shuffled, making both marginal importances look small. Shuffling may also create unrealistic combinations. Grouped/conditional permutation, drop-column retraining, SHAP, and domain knowledge can help, but they explain the fitted model rather than establish causality.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> OOB is natural only for the corresponding bootstrap ensemble; feature importance has no single universal definition; correlated variables split or substitute importance, so a low rank does not prove absence of information or causal effect.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

=== 5.4 SVM, kernels and nearest neighbors \[P1\]

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-svm-kernels">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ SVM: margin, hinge loss, soft margin, support vectors, and the kernel trick</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Maximum-margin classification</h4>
<p>Let $y_i\in\{-1,+1\}$ and $f(x)=w^{\mathsf T}x+b$. Classification uses $\operatorname{sign}f(x)$, while the signed geometric margin of $(x_i,y_i)$ is $y_if(x_i)/\lVert w\rVert$. For linearly separable data, hard-margin SVM solves</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\min_{w,b}\frac12\lVert w\rVert_2^2
\quad\text{s.t.}\quad
y_i(w^{\mathsf T}x_i+b)\ge1\quad\forall i.
$$</div>
<p>The constraints normalize the nearest functional margin to one, so minimizing $\lVert w\rVert$ maximizes the geometric width $2/\lVert w\rVert$ between the two supporting planes.</p>
<h4>2. Soft margin, slack, and $C$</h4>
<p>Real data are rarely perfectly separable. Introduce $\xi_i\ge0$:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\min_{w,b,\xi}\frac12\lVert w\rVert_2^2+C\sum_i\xi_i,
\qquad
y_if(x_i)\ge1-\xi_i.
$$</div>
<p>Large $C$ penalizes violations heavily, producing a potentially more complex boundary that is sensitive to outliers. Small $C$ tolerates violations to obtain a wider margin and stronger regularization. The equivalent unconstrained objective uses hinge loss:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\min_{w,b}\frac12\lVert w\rVert_2^2
+C\sum_i\max(0,1-y_if(x_i)).
$$</div>
<p>A correctly classified point still has positive hinge loss if it lies inside the margin.</p>
<h4>3. Support vectors and dual intuition</h4>
<p>In the dual representation,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
w=\sum_i\alpha_i y_i x_i,
\qquad
f(x)=\sum_i\alpha_i y_i\,x_i^{\mathsf T}x+b.
$$</div>
<p>Only observations with $\alpha_i&gt;0$ affect the boundary. These support vectors lie on or inside the margin or are misclassified. Easy points far from the boundary have $\alpha_i=0$ and can be removed without changing the fitted solution.</p>
<h4>4. Kernel trick</h4>
<p>When a linear boundary is insufficient, replace the inner product by $K(x_i,x)$ and fit a linear separator implicitly in a high-dimensional feature space:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
f(x)=\sum_i\alpha_i y_iK(x_i,x)+b.
$$</div>
<p>A valid kernel produces a symmetric positive-semidefinite Gram matrix. Common choices include linear, polynomial, and RBF:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
K_{\mathrm{RBF}}(x,z)=\exp(-\gamma\lVert x-z\rVert_2^2).
$$</div>
<p>Large $\gamma$ makes influence very local and boundaries flexible; small $\gamma$ gives smoother boundaries. Jointly cross-validate $C$ and $\gamma$.</p>
<h4>5. Practice and comparison with logistic regression</h4>
<ul>
<li>Scale features within training folds; otherwise distance, margins, and RBF are dominated by large-scale variables.</li>
<li>An SVM naturally returns a decision score, not probability; Platt scaling or another calibrator is additional.</li>
<li>Multiclass SVMs commonly use one-vs-rest or one-vs-one, and imbalance can be handled with class weights.</li>
<li>Linear SVM and logistic regression both give linear boundaries, but hinge loss concentrates on margin points whereas log loss uses all observations and naturally models probabilities.</li>
</ul>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> larger $C$ does not mean stronger regularization; it means stronger violation penalties and relatively weaker regularization. A nonlinear kernel is not automatically better, RBF can be expensive for large $n$, and tuning $C,\gamma$ on unscaled data is usually meaningless.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~
~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-knn">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ k-NN: scaling, distance metrics, concentration, and bias–variance</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. How does k-NN predict?</h4>
<p>k-nearest neighbors has almost no explicit training: it stores the training set. For a new $x$, find the $k$ closest training points and use majority vote/class proportions for classification or average response for regression. Distance weighting such as $w_i=1/(d_i+\varepsilon)$ gives closer neighbors more influence. It is a local, nonparametric method whose key assumption is that nearby observations have similar labels.</p>
<h4>2. Why is scaling essential?</h4>
<div class="math-display">$$
d(x,z)=\sqrt{\sum_{j=1}^p(x_j-z_j)^2}.
$$</div>
<p>A numerically large-unit feature dominates Euclidean distance. Fit standardization or robust scaling inside each training fold. Integer encoding of categories creates fake ordinal distances; use one-hot, Hamming/Gower distance, or a method designed for mixed data.</p>
<h4>3. How does $k$ control bias and variance?</h4>
<ul>
<li>$k=1$ creates a jagged boundary: low training bias, high sensitivity and variance.</li>
<li>Larger $k$ smooths neighborhoods, reducing variance but mixing local structures and increasing bias.</li>
<li>$k=n$ predicts the global majority class or overall response mean.</li>
</ul>
<p>Cross-validate $k$, metric, and weighting. An odd $k$ merely reduces binary voting ties; it is not a theoretical optimum.</p>
<h4>4. Curse of dimensionality and distance concentration</h4>
<p>High-dimensional data are sparse, and irrelevant coordinates add noise to every distance. Nearest and farthest distances become increasingly similar, so “nearest” may no longer mean locally close. Use feature selection, PCA/embeddings, or a domain-informed metric. Naive prediction costs $O(np)$; KD-trees help mainly in low dimensions, while approximate nearest-neighbor methods help at scale.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> tuning $k$ before scaling is usually meaningless; more features can destroy neighborhoods; neighbor class fractions are not automatically calibrated probabilities; and scaling, dimension reduction, and tuning must all occur within training folds.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

=== 5.5 Probabilistic and unsupervised models \[P1\]

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-naive-bayes-lda-qda">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Naive Bayes conditional independence; LDA and QDA covariance assumptions</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Shared Bayesian classification framework</h4>
<p>For class $Y=k$ and features $x$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
P(Y=k\mid X=x)\propto\pi_k f_k(x),
$$</div>
<p>where $\pi_k=P(Y=k)$ and $f_k(x)=p(x\mid Y=k)$. Naive Bayes, LDA, and QDA differ mainly in how they make $f_k$ estimable.</p>
<h4>2. Naive Bayes: conditional feature independence</h4>
<div class="math-display">$$
p(x_1,\ldots,x_p\mid Y=k)=\prod_{j=1}^p p(x_j\mid Y=k).
$$</div>
<p>The assumption is independence <strong>given the class</strong>, not marginal independence. Multinomial NB suits counts, Bernoulli NB binary features, and Gaussian NB continuous features. Compute in log space:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\log P(Y=k\mid x)=C+\log\pi_k+\sum_j\log p(x_j\mid Y=k).
$$</div>
<p>Laplace smoothing adds pseudo-counts so an unseen class-feature combination does not force the whole likelihood to zero. Even when independence is false, ranking can work well, though probability calibration may be poor.</p>
<h4>3. LDA: class Gaussians with shared covariance</h4>
<p>LDA assumes $X\mid Y=k\sim N(\mu_k,\Sigma)$. The common full covariance makes quadratic terms cancel between classes, yielding linear discriminants</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\delta_k(x)=x^{\mathsf T}\Sigma^{-1}\mu_k
-\frac12\mu_k^{\mathsf T}\Sigma^{-1}\mu_k+\log\pi_k.
$$</div>
<p>Pooling covariance across classes uses fewer parameters and is often stable with limited data.</p>
<h4>4. QDA: a covariance per class</h4>
<p>QDA assumes $X\mid Y=k\sim N(\mu_k,\Sigma_k)$ and uses</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\delta_k(x)=-\frac12\log|\Sigma_k|
-\frac12(x-\mu_k)^{\mathsf T}\Sigma_k^{-1}(x-\mu_k)+\log\pi_k.
$$</div>
<p>The boundary is quadratic. QDA is more flexible but estimates roughly $p(p+1)/2$ covariance parameters per class; high dimension or small classes make estimates unstable or singular, calling for shrinkage, dimension reduction, or LDA.</p>
<h4>5. Compact comparison</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Gaussian NB:</strong> diagonal covariance per class; ignores feature correlation.</li>
<li><strong>LDA:</strong> one shared full covariance; linear boundary.</li>
<li><strong>QDA:</strong> one full covariance per class; quadratic boundary and highest data demand.</li>
<li><strong>Logistic regression:</strong> models $P(Y\mid X)$ directly instead of specifying $p(X\mid Y)$.</li>
</ul>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> LDA here means Linear Discriminant Analysis, not Latent Dirichlet Allocation; “naive” means conditional rather than marginal independence; QDA's lower bias can be outweighed by higher variance; and changing class priors changes posteriors and decision boundaries.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~
- □ k-means objective, Lloyd iterations, initialization and local minima.
~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-em-algorithm">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ EM and GMM: E-step, M-step, lower bound, convergence, and local optima</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. What difficulty does EM solve?</h4>
<p>Let $X$ be observed data, $Z$ latent variables, and $\theta$ parameters. If $Z$ were known, the complete-data likelihood $p(X,Z\mid\theta)$ might be easy to optimize. With only $X$ observed, however,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\ell(\theta)=\log p(X\mid\theta)
=\log\sum_Zp(X,Z\mid\theta),
$$</div>
<p>or an integral for continuous $Z$. The sum/integral inside the logarithm complicates differentiation. EM alternates between inferring a soft distribution over missing structure and optimizing parameters under that soft assignment.</p>
<h4>2. E-step and M-step</h4>
<p>Given current parameters $\theta^{(t)}$:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\text{E-step:}\quad
q^{(t)}(Z)=p(Z\mid X,\theta^{(t)}),
$$</div>
<p>and compute the conditional expected complete-data log-likelihood</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
Q(\theta\mid\theta^{(t)})
=\mathbb E_{Z\mid X,\theta^{(t)}}[\log p(X,Z\mid\theta)].
$$</div>
<p>Then perform</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\text{M-step:}\quad
\theta^{(t+1)}=\arg\max_\theta Q(\theta\mid\theta^{(t)}).
$$</div>
<p>Alternate until likelihood improvement, parameter change, or responsibility change is sufficiently small.</p>
<h4>3. Why does likelihood not decrease?</h4>
<p>For any distribution $q(Z)$, Jensen's inequality gives the evidence lower bound (ELBO):</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\log p(X\mid\theta)
\ge \mathbb E_q[\log p(X,Z\mid\theta)]-\mathbb E_q[\log q(Z)].
$$</div>
<p>The E-step sets $q$ to the current posterior, making this lower bound touch the log-likelihood at $\theta^{(t)}$. The M-step raises the bound. Thus observed-data likelihood is nondecreasing, but monotonicity guarantees only convergence to a stationary point, not a global maximum.</p>
<h4>4. What happens in a GMM?</h4>
<p>For $p(x_i)=\sum_{k=1}^K\pi_k\mathcal N(x_i\mid\mu_k,\Sigma_k)$, E-step responsibilities are</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
r_{ik}=P(Z_i=k\mid x_i)
=\frac{\pi_k\mathcal N(x_i\mid\mu_k,\Sigma_k)}
{\sum_{j=1}^K\pi_j\mathcal N(x_i\mid\mu_j,\Sigma_j)}.
$$</div>
<p>Writing $N_k=\sum_i r_{ik}$, the M-step is weighted MLE:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\pi_k=\frac{N_k}{n},
\qquad
\mu_k=\frac1{N_k}\sum_i r_{ik}x_i,
\qquad
\Sigma_k=\frac1{N_k}\sum_i r_{ik}(x_i-\mu_k)(x_i-\mu_k)^{\mathsf T}.
$$</div>
<p>Unlike k-means' hard assignments, a GMM gives every point a probability across clusters. With equal spherical covariances tending toward zero, its behavior approaches k-means.</p>
<h4>5. Initialization, singularities, and workflow</h4>
<ul>
<li>Initialize with k-means or multiple random starts and compare several restarts.</li>
<li>Monitor log-likelihood rather than raw parameter order because mixture labels can permute.</li>
<li>A Gaussian component can collapse onto one point with covariance approaching zero, making likelihood unbounded; use covariance floors, regularization, or priors.</li>
<li>EM also appears in missing-data models, HMMs, and factor analysis whenever conditioning on latent structure simplifies estimation.</li>
</ul>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> the E-step computes posterior responsibilities rather than forcing a hard class; nondecreasing likelihood does not make every parameter monotone or find a global optimum; and permuted component labels represent the same mixture.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-pca">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ PCA: maximum variance, minimum reconstruction error, SVD, explained variance, and practice</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. What direction does PCA seek?</h4>
<p>Center each column of $X\in\mathbb R^{n\times p}$. The first principal direction is the unit vector that maximizes projected variance:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
v_1=\arg\max_{\lVert v\rVert=1}\operatorname{Var}(Xv)
=\arg\max_{\lVert v\rVert=1}v^{\mathsf T}Sv,
\qquad S=\frac{X^{\mathsf T}X}{n-1}.
$$</div>
<p>$v_1$ is the eigenvector of $S$ with largest eigenvalue. Subsequent directions maximize remaining variance subject to being orthogonal to earlier directions.</p>
<h4>2. Exact relationship with SVD</h4>
<p>Take the SVD of centered data:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
X=U\Sigma V^{\mathsf T}.
$$</div>
<ul>
<li>columns of $V$ are principal directions/loadings, showing how original features combine;</li>
<li>$XV=U\Sigma$ are scores, locating observations in principal-component coordinates;</li>
<li>component $j$ has sample variance $\sigma_j^2/(n-1)$ and explained-variance ratio $\sigma_j^2/\sum_\ell\sigma_\ell^2$.</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. Why is PCA also minimum reconstruction error?</h4>
<p>Keep $V_k$, set low-dimensional scores $Z=XV_k$, and reconstruct</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\hat X_k=ZV_k^{\mathsf T}=XV_kV_k^{\mathsf T}.
$$</div>
<p>The Eckart–Young theorem says this minimizes $\lVert X-\hat X_k\rVert_F^2$ over all rank-$k$ approximations. Maximizing retained variance and minimizing squared reconstruction error are therefore equivalent.</p>
<h4>4. Centering, scaling, and intuition</h4>
<p>Centering is almost always required. If units differ greatly, standardize first, which amounts to PCA on the correlation matrix; otherwise high-variance units dominate. For two highly positively correlated standardized features, the first component is roughly $(1,1)/\sqrt2$, capturing common movement, and the second roughly $(1,-1)/\sqrt2$, capturing their difference.</p>
<h4>5. Choosing $k$ and limitations</h4>
<p>Use a scree plot, cumulative explained variance, or preferably downstream cross-validation to choose $k$. PCA is unsupervised and maximizes variation in $X$; a low-variance direction may still be most predictive of $y$. Loading signs are arbitrary, outliers can rotate components strongly, and nonlinear manifolds need other methods. Fit centering, scaling, and PCA only on each training fold.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> components are linear combinations, not selected original variables; high explained variance is neither causality nor predictive relevance; PCA does not automatically discover nonlinear structure; and fitting PCA on all data before cross-validation leaks validation-set structure.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~
- □ Anomaly detection and density-estimation basics.

=== 5.6 Neural networks \[P1; P0 for AI-quant roles\]

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-neural-network-foundations">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Affine layers, activations, depth, width, and universal-approximation intuition</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Basic building block</h4>
<div class="math-display">$$
z^{(\ell)}=W^{(\ell)}h^{(\ell-1)}+b^{(\ell)},
\qquad
h^{(\ell)}=\phi(z^{(\ell)}).
$$</div>
<p>If input width is $d_{\ell-1}$ and output width $d_\ell$, then $W^{(\ell)}\in\mathbb R^{d_\ell\times d_{\ell-1}}$. Checking tensor shapes prevents many implementation errors. Without nonlinear activations, stacked affine maps collapse into one affine map, so depth adds no new nonlinearity.</p>
<h4>2. Depth versus width</h4>
<p><strong>Width</strong> learns many features in parallel at one level; <strong>depth</strong> composes simple representations into complex ones and reuses intermediate features. A shallow network may represent the same function in principle but require exponentially more units.</p>
<h4>3. What universal approximation does—and does not—say</h4>
<p>Under suitable activations and on a compact domain, a sufficiently wide single-hidden-layer network can approximate any continuous function arbitrarily well. This is an existence theorem. It does not say that finite data identify the function, gradient descent finds the parameters, required width is practical, extrapolation works, or test error is small.</p>
<h4>4. Match the output layer to the task</h4>
<p>Use a linear output for unconstrained regression, one sigmoid logit for binary classification, softmax for mutually exclusive classes, and exponential/softplus for positive intensities. Hidden activation choice and output-distribution choice are separate.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> parameter count alone neither guarantees capacity is useful nor determines generalization; depth without activations is still linear; and universal approximation is not an optimization, generalization, or causal guarantee.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-activations-gradients">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ ReLU, sigmoid, tanh; saturation and vanishing/exploding gradients</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Three common activations</h4>
<div class="math-display">$$
\sigma(z)=\frac1{1+e^{-z}},
\qquad
\tanh z=\frac{e^z-e^{-z}}{e^z+e^{-z}},
\qquad
\operatorname{ReLU}(z)=\max(0,z).
$$</div>
<ul>
<li><strong>sigmoid</strong> maps to $(0,1)$ and suits a Bernoulli output; $\sigma'(z)\le1/4$;</li>
<li><strong>tanh</strong> is zero-centered but also saturates for large $|z|$;</li>
<li><strong>ReLU</strong> has derivative one on the positive half-line and is cheap for deep hidden layers.</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Saturation and gradient products</h4>
<p>Sigmoid/tanh derivatives approach zero in saturated regions. Backprop multiplies layer Jacobians:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\frac{\partial L}{\partial h^{(0)}}
=\frac{\partial L}{\partial h^{(L)}}
\prod_{\ell=L}^{1}\frac{\partial h^{(\ell)}}{\partial h^{(\ell-1)}}.
$$</div>
<p>Typical singular values below one cause exponential vanishing; above one cause explosion.</p>
<h4>3. Dead ReLUs and alternatives</h4>
<p>ReLU alleviates positive-side saturation but has zero derivative for $z&lt;0$. Poor initialization or an excessive learning rate can leave a unit permanently negative (“dead”). Leaky ReLU preserves a small negative slope; GELU/SiLU provide smooth gating and are common in Transformers.</p>
<h4>4. Diagnose the full system</h4>
<p>Initialization, weight spectra, depth, sequence length, normalization, and residual paths all affect gradients. Inspect layer activation/gradient distributions, global gradient norm, NaNs, and dead-unit rates instead of blaming activation alone.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> sigmoid is appropriate for Bernoulli outputs; ReLU does not eliminate all gradient problems; exploding gradients are an optimization issue, distinct from statistical overfitting.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-backprop-training-stability">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Backpropagation, initialization, normalization, residual connections, and gradient clipping</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Backpropagation is a reused chain rule</h4>
<p>For $z_1=W_1x+b_1$, $h=\phi(z_1)$, $z_2=W_2h+b_2$, and $\delta_2=\partial L/\partial z_2$:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial L}{\partial W_2}&amp;=\delta_2h^{\mathsf T},
&amp;\frac{\partial L}{\partial b_2}&amp;=\delta_2,\\
\delta_1&amp;=(W_2^{\mathsf T}\delta_2)\odot\phi'(z_1),
&amp;\frac{\partial L}{\partial W_1}&amp;=\delta_1x^{\mathsf T}.
\end{aligned}
$$</div>
<p>Backprop computes gradients; an optimizer such as SGD or Adam uses them to update parameters.</p>
<h4>2. Initialization preserves signal scales</h4>
<p>All-zero weights leave same-layer units symmetric. Xavier/Glorot variance around $2/(d_{\mathrm{in}}+d_{\mathrm{out}})$ suits tanh-like activations; He variance around $2/d_{\mathrm{in}}$ suits ReLU. The aim is stable forward-activation and backward-gradient variance across layers.</p>
<h4>3. Normalization and residual paths</h4>
<p>Normalization stabilizes intermediate scales and conditioning. BatchNorm uses batch statistics; LayerNorm normalizes within each observation and suits sequences/small batches. A residual block</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
h_{\ell+1}=h_\ell+F(h_\ell;\theta_\ell)
$$</div>
<p>creates an identity gradient path $I+\partial F/\partial h_\ell$, making deep optimization easier.</p>
<h4>4. Clipping and regularization</h4>
<p>Global norm clipping replaces $g$ by $cg/\lVert g\rVert$ when $\lVert g\rVert&gt;c$, limiting episodic explosions, especially in RNNs. It treats a symptom rather than replacing a sensible learning rate. Weight decay shrinks parameters; early stopping halts when validation loss stops improving. A larger model can sometimes generalize because optimization, data, architecture, and implicit/explicit regularization matter—not parameter count alone.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> backprop is not an optimizer; clipping is not arbitrary elementwise truncation; initialization can determine trainability; and none of these methods fixes target leakage or a misspecified objective.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-batch-normalization">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Batch normalization: training statistics and inference running statistics</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Training computation</h4>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mu_B=\frac1m\sum_i x_i,
\quad
\sigma_B^2=\frac1m\sum_i(x_i-\mu_B)^2,
\quad
\hat x_i=\frac{x_i-\mu_B}{\sqrt{\sigma_B^2+\varepsilon}},
\quad
y_i=\gamma\hat x_i+\beta.
$$</div>
<p>Learned $\gamma,\beta$ restore useful scale and location. During training, an example's output depends slightly on other batch members, adding regularizing noise.</p>
<h4>2. Why inference is different</h4>
<p>Deployment may use a single example or a differently composed batch. Evaluation mode uses running means/variances accumulated during training. Forgetting eval mode makes predictions batch-dependent; accidentally freezing running statistics during training also damages learning.</p>
<h4>3. Batch size and alternatives</h4>
<p>Tiny or strongly correlated batches give noisy statistics. Careless batching in time series can create inappropriate cross-example coupling. LayerNorm is batch-independent and common in Transformers; GroupNorm is useful for small-batch vision.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> BatchNorm does not replace input preprocessing; inference does not use current test-batch variance; $\gamma,\beta$ are learned; train/eval mode is part of the model definition.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-dropout">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Dropout: training versus inference behavior</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Random masking during training</h4>
<p>With keep probability $q=1-p$, inverted dropout samples $m_j\sim\operatorname{Bernoulli}(q)$ and uses</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\widetilde h_j=\frac{m_j}{q}h_j,
\qquad
\mathbb E[\widetilde h_j\mid h_j]=h_j.
$$</div>
<p>Each batch sees a different sparse subnetwork, discouraging excessive co-adaptation and resembling an ensemble of weight-sharing subnetworks.</p>
<h4>2. Inference behavior</h4>
<p>With inverted scaling already applied in training, standard inference disables random masks and uses full $h$ with no extra scaling. Forgetting eval mode makes predictions random. A non-inverted implementation instead multiplies by $q$ at inference; do not mix conventions.</p>
<h4>3. Tuning and context</h4>
<p>Excessive dropout underfits. BatchNorm, data augmentation, weight decay, dataset size, and architecture affect whether it helps. CNNs may use spatial dropout; Transformers use it in attention/MLP/residual paths. MC dropout intentionally samples masks at inference for rough uncertainty, not an exact posterior.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> dropout rate usually means drop probability; training and inference differ; dropout neither repairs leakage nor guarantees calibration; adding it to an underfit model is usually harmful.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-neural-architectures">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Core inductive biases of CNNs, RNNs, LSTMs, GRUs, and Transformers</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Inductive bias</h4>
<p>An inductive bias is a structural assumption built in before seeing data. A suitable bias learns with less data; a poor one constrains the model incorrectly. Architectures mainly differ in their assumptions about locality, order, and long-range interaction.</p>
<h4>2. CNN: locality and weight sharing</h4>
<p>A convolution reuses a local kernel across locations, assuming a pattern has similar meaning wherever it occurs. This is parameter-efficient and translation equivariant. Stacking layers expands the receptive field; pooling/stride aggregates context but can discard precise position.</p>
<h4>3. RNN, LSTM, and GRU: recurrence and gates</h4>
<p>A vanilla RNN uses $h_t=\phi(W_xx_t+W_hh_{t-1}+b)$, sharing parameters over arbitrary sequence length and preserving order. Long backprop chains cause vanishing/exploding gradients and sequential computation. LSTM input/forget/output gates plus cell state provide an additive memory path; GRU uses simpler update/reset gates. Gates improve but do not guarantee arbitrary long-term memory.</p>
<h4>4. Transformer: attention and position</h4>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{Attention}(Q,K,V)
=\operatorname{softmax}\!\left(\frac{QK^{\mathsf T}}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right)V.
$$</div>
<p>Each position directly retrieves information from others, shortening dependency paths and enabling parallel training. Scaling prevents dot products from saturating softmax. Attention alone has no order, so positional information is necessary; autoregressive prediction also needs a causal mask. Standard attention costs $O(T^2)$ memory/time.</p>
<h4>5. Choosing an architecture</h4>
<p>CNNs suit local grid structure; RNN/GRU/LSTM suit streaming sequential state; Transformers suit long-range retrieval and parallel scale. Hybrids are common.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> Transformers do not automatically know order; bidirectional attention leaks future data without a causal mask; LSTM gates do not eliminate all long-dependency issues; and architecture names cannot replace correct timestamps, validation, and compute budgeting.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

=== 5.7 Metrics and validation \[P0\]

~~~
{}{raw}
<ul class="concept-list">
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-regression-metrics">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Regression: MSE, RMSE, MAE, Huber loss, and $R^2$</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. MSE and RMSE emphasize large errors</h4>
<p>For residual $e_i=y_i-\hat y_i$,</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{MSE}=\frac1n\sum_i e_i^2,
\qquad
\operatorname{RMSE}=\sqrt{\operatorname{MSE}}.
$$</div>
<p>MSE is smooth, corresponds to Gaussian negative log-likelihood, and strongly penalizes large errors, but one outlier can dominate. RMSE has the target's units and is easier to interpret; its model ordering is identical to MSE because square root is monotone.</p>
<h4>2. MAE and Huber improve robustness</h4>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{MAE}=\frac1n\sum_i|e_i|,
\qquad
L_\delta(e)=
\begin{cases}
\frac12e^2,&amp;|e|\le\delta,\\
\delta(|e|-\frac12\delta),&amp;|e|&gt;\delta.
\end{cases}
$$</div>
<p>MAE penalizes tails linearly and targets the conditional median, whereas MSE targets the conditional mean. Huber is quadratic near zero and linear in the tails; smaller $\delta$ resembles MAE and larger $\delta$ resembles MSE.</p>
<h4>3. $R^2$ compares with a mean baseline</h4>
<div class="math-display">$$
R^2=1-\frac{\sum_i(y_i-\hat y_i)^2}{\sum_i(y_i-\bar y)^2}.
$$</div>
<p>On one test set, zero means matching a constant-mean squared-error baseline and negative means worse. In-sample OLS with an intercept has nonnegative $R^2$, but out-of-sample $R^2$ can be arbitrarily negative. It does not directly measure direction, ranking, tail behavior, or trading costs.</p>
<h4>4. Select metrics from the actual loss</h4>
<p>Use MSE/RMSE when extreme errors are disproportionately costly; MAE/Huber when linear tail cost or robustness is appropriate. Report error quantiles and segment/regime performance alongside one aggregate.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> RMSE is not residual standard deviation; $R^2$ may be negative and is not comparable across different targets/test sets without care; removing large residuals can merely hide hard cases.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-classification-metrics">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Classification: confusion matrix, accuracy, precision, recall, specificity, and F1</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Begin with the confusion matrix</h4>
<p>At a chosen threshold, predictions produce true positives $TP$, false positives $FP$, false negatives $FN$, and true negatives $TN$:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{Accuracy}&amp;=\frac{TP+TN}{TP+FP+FN+TN},\\
\mathrm{Precision}&amp;=\frac{TP}{TP+FP},
&amp;\mathrm{Recall}&amp;=\frac{TP}{TP+FN},\\
\mathrm{Specificity}&amp;=\frac{TN}{TN+FP},
&amp;\mathrm{FPR}&amp;=1-\mathrm{Specificity}.
\end{aligned}
$$</div>
<h4>2. What does each answer?</h4>
<p>Precision asks how many flagged positives are real; recall/sensitivity asks how many real positives were found; specificity asks how many real negatives were rejected. Accuracy can be misleading: with 100 positives among 10,000 observations, always predicting negative gives 99% accuracy but zero recall.</p>
<h4>3. F1 and thresholds</h4>
<div class="math-display">$$
F_1=\frac{2PR}{P+R}=\frac{2TP}{2TP+FP+FN}.
$$</div>
<p>The harmonic mean is pulled down by the weaker of precision and recall, but ignores true negatives and treats the two components symmetrically. If error costs differ, choose thresholds by cost or use $F_\beta$. Lower thresholds typically increase recall and reduce precision/specificity. For multiclass reporting, distinguish macro, weighted-macro, and micro averaging.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> metrics depend on positive-class definition and threshold; precision depends strongly on deployment prevalence; F1 does not evaluate calibration; threshold choice must be made on validation rather than test data.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-roc-pr-imbalance">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ ROC-AUC, PR-AUC, class imbalance, thresholds, and false-positive burden</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. ROC and ranking</h4>
<p>Across score thresholds, ROC plots</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\mathrm{TPR}=\frac{TP}{TP+FN}
\quad\text{against}\quad
\mathrm{FPR}=\frac{FP}{FP+TN}.
$$</div>
<p>ROC-AUC is the probability that a random positive receives a higher score than a random negative, up to tie handling. It evaluates ranking, not calibration or one deployment threshold.</p>
<h4>2. Why PR is useful under severe imbalance</h4>
<p>Precision–Recall plots precision against recall. Its random-ranking baseline equals positive prevalence, whereas ROC's remains 0.5. At 1% prevalence, even 1% FPR may create roughly as many false as true positives at high recall, so PR displays operational burden more directly. PR-AUC changes with prevalence and should not be compared across datasets without context.</p>
<h4>3. Thresholds and costs</h4>
<p>Business uses one operating point, not the whole AUC. Select it from $C_{FP},C_{FN}$, capacity, prevalence, and calibrated probabilities on validation data, then report its confusion matrix. Class weighting/resampling changes scores and may require recalibration.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> high global ROC-AUC need not mean strong performance in the low-FPR region; PR-AUC has no universal 0.5 baseline; AUC omits size and costs; never choose the best-looking test threshold.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-probability-metrics-calibration">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Log loss, Brier score, calibration curves, ranking, and calibration</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Two proper probability scores</h4>
<div class="math-display">$$
\operatorname{LogLoss}
=-\frac1n\sum_i[y_i\log p_i+(1-y_i)\log(1-p_i)],
\qquad
\operatorname{Brier}=\frac1n\sum_i(p_i-y_i)^2.
$$</div>
<p>Log loss imposes unbounded penalty on confident mistakes; Brier is bounded and resembles probability MSE. Both are proper scoring rules: reporting the true probability minimizes expected score.</p>
<h4>2. Calibration curves</h4>
<p>Bin predictions and compare each bin's mean predicted probability with observed positive frequency. Predictions near 0.7 are locally calibrated if about 70% are positive. Bin design and sample size matter, so small-bin noise needs uncertainty rather than overinterpretation.</p>
<h4>3. Ranking, calibration, and sharpness differ</h4>
<p>A monotone score transform preserves AUC but changes probabilities and log loss. Always predicting prevalence can be calibrated yet non-discriminating. A useful probability model seeks sharp, informative probabilities while remaining calibrated. Fit Platt/isotonic calibration on held-out or out-of-fold scores and monitor after prevalence/regime drift.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> accuracy/AUC do not assess probability magnitude; a diagonal calibration plot does not prove discrimination; probability clipping must be transparent; never train a calibrator on the test set.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-time-series-validation">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ Time-series expanding/rolling walk-forward validation, purging, and embargo</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Why random k-fold fails</h4>
<p>Random shuffling lets future regimes, distributions, or entity states help predict the past. Time-series validation must keep all training timestamps before validation and mimic the actual retraining schedule.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Expanding:</strong> accumulate from a fixed start; maximum data when old observations remain relevant.</li>
<li><strong>Rolling:</strong> retain a fixed recent history; fewer samples but adapts to drift.</li>
<li><strong>Gap:</strong> leave space between training end and validation start for latency/dependence.</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Overlapping labels leak information</h4>
<p>If a label at $t$ uses the next $H$ days, nearby train and validation labels share future prices even when features are timestamped correctly. <strong>Purging</strong> removes training observations whose label intervals overlap validation; <strong>embargo</strong> leaves an additional buffer around validation. Length should follow label horizon, lookback, and execution—not a ritual fixed number.</p>
<h4>3. Tuning and final evaluation</h4>
<p>Refit preprocessing, feature selection, and model inside every walk-forward fold. Use inner temporal splits for hyperparameters and retain a truly untouched final period. Report fold/regime dispersion, turnover, and post-cost behavior in addition to the mean metric.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> a date split alone does not eliminate overlapping-label leakage; expanding is not universally better than rolling; excessive embargo wastes data; future observations must not influence normalization, imputation, or feature selection.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<details class="concept-detail" id="concept-grouped-validation-pipelines">
<summary><span class="concept-label">□ k-fold, stratified/GroupKFold, nested CV, and training-fold preprocessing</span><span class="concept-action"><span class="when-closed">Expand</span><span class="when-open">Collapse</span></span></summary>
<div class="concept-content">
<h4>1. Split by the independent unit</h4>
<p>Ordinary k-fold treats rows as approximately i.i.d. Stratification preserves class proportions but still permits one entity across folds. Repeated rows from one company, patient, user, security, or event make random-row validation optimistic. GroupKFold keeps a group in one fold and measures generalization to unseen groups. If deployment predicts future observations of known groups, the split must also respect time.</p>
<h4>2. Why nested CV?</h4>
<p>Repeatedly choosing the best hyperparameters on one CV overfits validation noise. Nested CV uses inner folds for tuning and outer folds only to estimate the complete tuning procedure. A final test set should still be used once.</p>
<h4>3. Pipeline prevents preprocessing leakage</h4>
<p>Fit imputation, scaling, winsorization, PCA, feature selection, target encoding, and calibration inside each training fold, then transform its validation fold:</p>
<div class="math-display">$$
\text{train fold}\xrightarrow{\mathrm{fit\ preprocessing}}
\text{transform train/validation separately}.
$$</div>
<p>Full-data standardization leaks validation means/variances; supervised selection and target encoding leak even more. A pipeline binds preprocessing to the estimator so CV refits every stage.</p>
<h4>4. Uncertainty</h4>
<p>Fold scores are not fully independent, so their standard deviation is not automatically an exact confidence interval. Report fold-level values, group/regime coverage, split sensitivity, and a final holdout.</p>
<p class="concept-check"><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong> stratification does not solve group leakage; GroupKFold does not solve time leakage; unsupervised preprocessing can still leak; outer folds in nested CV cannot be reused for tuning.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
</ul>
~~~

=== 5.8 Quant-research-specific model judgment \[P0\]

- □ Define the target at prediction time and prove every feature is available then.
- □ Cross-sectional IC\/rank IC versus time-series prediction metrics.
- □ Neutralization\/exposure control, universe selection and missing-data semantics.
- □ Backtest net of fees, spread, slippage, market impact and turnover.
- □ Survivorship, look-ahead, stale prices, corporate actions and delisting bias.
- □ Multiple factors\/hyperparameters create multiple-testing and selection bias; keep a true holdout and report uncertainty.
- □ Regime stability, rolling diagnostics and stress tests matter more than one aggregate score.
- □ A model with higher offline R² can have worse PnL because ranking, tails, capacity and costs differ.

== 6. One-page formula recall

~~~
{}{raw}
